\par \par \uc2 As the debt limit is reached and the Treasury Department exhausts emergency measures, lawmakers will face pressure to act. PHOTO: Eric Lee for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 The nation's borrowing limit and financial regulation will be closely watched issues on Wall Street in the next two years.\par \par \uc2 Some GOP lawmakers have signaled a willingness to use negotiations over raising or suspending the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-wont-hit-borrowing-limit-until-second-half-of-2023-according-to-estimate-11655238310"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nation's debt limit }}}\uc2  as a way to demand spending cuts. Those dynamics set up the potential for a high-stakes partisan conflict during the negotiations, which could come in the second half of 2023.\par \par \uc2 * Once the debt limit of roughly $31.4 trillion is reached and the Treasury Department exhausts emergency measures, lawmakers would need to raise the limit or else the government would be forced to delay or suspend payments on its obligations, potentially causing a default on bond payments. Such a scenario would likely rattle financial markets and the U.S. economy, which faces heightened risk of recession.\par \par \uc2 * Bank regulators in Washington, including at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , are contemplating {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-weighs-new-rules-for-regional-banks-complicating-merger-plans-11659748861"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more stringent merger restrictions }}}\uc2  for big regional banks, regulatory requirements for large banks to build bigger financial buffers and revamped standards for lending in lower-income communities. \uc2 The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2  faces a legal challenge to the constitutionality of its funding that could reach the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  is pushing to implement an overhaul to the stock market's plumbing. Also on the agency's agenda are rules related to climate change and increased transparency requirements for hedge funds and private equity.\par \par \uc2 Amara Omeokwe\par \par \uc2 Inflation\par \par \uc2 The return of divided government means little when it comes to how Washington is likely to address {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/inflation-definition-cause-what-is-it-11644353564"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high inflation }}}\uc2  because lawmakers in both parties largely agree that the Fed is responsible for taking action that will restrain demand to reduce high prices.\par \par \uc2 * Even before losing control of the House, the White House had largely run out of room to push for higher spending because of resistance in the evenly divided Senate from Sen. Joe Manchin (D., W.Va.), who argued that more spending would fuel higher inflation. Mr. Biden in August used executive authority to approve an estimated $400 billion in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/bidens-student-loan-forgiveness-plan-could-cost-400-billion-cbo-says-11664227318"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 student-loan forgiveness }}}\uc2  that economists have said could contribute to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-student-loan-forgiveness-raises-inflation-budget-risks-11661457157"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 higher inflation }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * Republicans could seek to force spending curbs, as they did after taking control of Congress during the first term of the Obama administration. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/some-investors-bet-fed-could-lift-rates-to-two-decade-high-11667945255"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fed's aggressive rate increases }}}\uc2  this year are likely to slow economic growth next year faster than any fiscal policy might achieve.\par \par \uc2 * While some Democratic lawmakers have called on the central bank {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/fed-approves-fourth-0-75-point-rate-rise-hints-at-smaller-hikes-11667412242"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to slow down or pause its rate increases }}}\uc2 , there is little ability for Congress to influence the Fed directly. Mr. Biden this year appointed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/senate-confirms-philip-jefferson-as-federal-reserve-board-governor-11652313097"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three new governors }}}\uc2  to the Fed's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-nominee-for-feds-top-banking-regulator-confirmed-as-fed-governor-11657731124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seven-member board }}}\uc2 , which has no vacancies. The term of one of those new governors, Lisa Cook, expires in January 2024, and Mr. Biden would need to secure Senate support to reappoint Ms. Cook or nominate someone else to a full 14-year term.\par \par \uc2 Nick Timiraos\par \par \uc2 Foreign policy\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kherson-residents-tell-of-torture-abuse-during-russian-occupation-11668600584"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 war in Ukraine }}}\uc2  and competition with China are expected to dominate both parties' foreign-policy agendas for the next two years. Republicans are likely to use their new platform to question U.S. spending on foreign conflicts and investigate the Biden administration's decision-making on the global stage.\par \par \uc2 * Support in Congress for pumping aid to Ukraine is starting to fracture as many Republicans in the House question whether the money would be better spent combating China and tackling economic problems facing the U.S., lawmakers and congressional staff from both parties have said. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-wall-street-journal-poll-11656538220"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Polls show }}}\uc2  Republican voters are increasingly opposed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/republican-opposition-to-helping-ukraine-grows-wsj-poll-finds-11667467802"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to helping Ukraine }}}\uc2 . Calls to dial back the assistance are likely to continue.\par \par \uc2 * Competition with China continues to be a theme that both Democrats and Republicans stress as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/china-congress-xi-jinping-us-11665753002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 core national-security priority }}}\uc2 . Republicans could push for more confrontational policies by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-taiwan-kick-off-talks-to-deepen-trade-economic-ties-11667911877"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bolstering U.S.-Taiwan relations }}}\uc2  and for more trade barriers. They might try to draw parallels between job losses in the U.S. and competition from China ahead of the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 * The Biden administration's tumultuous and deadly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/afghanistans-taliban-seize-jalalabad-as-panic-grips-kabul-11629005282"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 withdrawal from Afghanistan }}}\uc2  in 2021 could be the focus of new investigations. Republicans have accused the administration of failing to act fast enough {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/afghans-fleeing-taliban-grow-more-desperate-as-clock-ticks-on-u-s-evacuation-11629797542"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to evacuate U.S. personnel and Afghan partners }}}\uc2 . Mr. Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-to-defend-afghanistan-withdrawal-ending-nearly-20-year-war-11630435444"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has defended his administration's evacuation efforts }}}\uc2  and said he didn't want to continue what he has criticized as a "forever war."\par \par \uc2 Vivian Salama\par \par \uc2 Abortion\par \par \uc2 Divided government means bipartisan clashes over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception access are likely to continue. Federal legislative change is unlikely, as state lawmakers move to either codify {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access }}}\uc2  or further restrict it.\par \par \uc2 * GOP lawmakers will continue to block efforts by Democrats to ensure that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal throughout the U.S. Such efforts, passed twice in the House, have failed to garner sufficient Senate support.\par \par \uc2 * Senate Democrats and Mr. Biden will likely be able to block any Republican push for a broader national ban. Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R., S.C.) has proposed banning abortions after 15 weeks nationwide, with some exemptions, though some in his party said the issue should be left to states. The Republican Study Committee, the most influential conservative caucus in the House, has backed legislation from Rep. Mike Kelly (R., Pa.) that would ban abortions after the detection of a fetal heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 * Republicans could seek to block other administration efforts such as a federal fund that Mr. Biden supports for women who need to take time off work or find child care as they prepare to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and they are likely to try to block \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers such as \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2  from getting federal family-planning grants.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Armour\par \par \uc2 Healthcare\par \par \uc2 Democrats and Republicans are gearing up for fights over {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-warns-gop-would-slash-social-security-medicare-in-pitch-to-older-voters-11667329464"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 entitlement programs }}}\uc2  such as Medicare and a drug-pricing provision passed in 2022. Major legislation is unlikely, but Republicans could seek to attach some changes to must-pass bills to force Democrats to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 * House Republicans have proposed raising the Medicare eligibility age and adding means-testing. Mr. Biden has said he would block any cuts to entitlement programs.\par \par \uc2 * Republicans are expected to investigate the administration's Covid-19 response, how pandemic relief funds have been deployed and the origins of the virus.\par \par \uc2 * Gridlock in Congress will likely mean more health-policy actions will occur at the state level. Nevada lawmakers are moving ahead with a state public health insurance option that is set to launch in 2026, joining Colorado and Washington state.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Armour\par \par \uc2 Energy and climate\par \par \uc2 Divided government could slow the pace of climate-focused initiatives that Mr. Biden has rolled out since entering the White House. Republicans are expected to dial up pressure on the Biden administration to look more favorably on fossil fuels and try to increase domestic oil-and-gas production in response to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ukraines-zelensky-sets-conditions-for-genuine-peace-talks-with-russia-11667907501?mod=hp_lead_pos6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Russia's invasion of Ukraine }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * Mr. Biden and Democrats in Congress have tried to curb greenhouse-gas emissions and meet global climate goals by passing a $1 trillion infrastructure plan with measures to help communities endure extreme weather and a separate $370 billion of climate-focused provisions, mostly in the form of tax breaks for major wind and solar project developers. Republicans are unlikely to back more spending on climate initiatives and plan to push for more development of American energy sources.\par \par \uc2 * Republicans and Democrats could work together on changing the laws that govern the U.S. permitting process for major infrastructure projects. Republicans say speeding up new oil-and-gas production would eventually bring down energy prices for consumers. Democrats who support the permitting changes say they would ease the path for new renewable-energy projects. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrats-effort-to-speed-up-energy-project-permitting-faces-more-opposition-11662857310"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Permitting changes }}}\uc2  were blocked in the last Congress in the midst of opposition from lawmakers on both sides of the aisle, but they are likely to come up again.\par \par \uc2 * Republicans could launch investigations and hold hearings to slow down new regulations. More stringent requirements from the Biden administration for vehicle tailpipe emissions haven't taken effect, for example. Republicans could also push for budget cuts for federal agencies that are charged with implementing environmental regulations.\par \par \uc2 Katy Stech Ferek\par \par \uc2 Gun control\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has repeatedly called on Congress to pass major gun-control measures such as reinstating the federal ban on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/podcasts/google-news-update/why-congress-isnt-banning-assault-weapons/bc6f8bf2-7c00-488b-91bd-3382cfef3fd0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 certain semiautomatic weapons }}}\uc2 , but such efforts have stalled even with Democratic majorities in both chambers. Congress passed a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/bipartisan-gun-bill-set-to-advance-past-senate-filibuster-hurdle-11655996527"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 modest bipartisan gun package }}}\uc2  after the Uvalde, Texas, massacre, which included funding for states to implement and enforce red-flag laws to remove guns from people deemed dangerous; expanding background checks for gun buyers under 21 to include juvenile records; and tougher sentences for gun traffickers.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What changes to domestic policy do you anticipate with Republicans in charge in Congress? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 * Divided government likely snuffs out Mr. Biden's hopes of passing significant gun-control measures in Congress. No major gun-control bills have ever passed with a Republican-led House.\par \par \uc2 * The GOP will have trouble advancing a gun-rights agenda with a Democrat in the White House. Only under Republican presidents have major pro-gun laws been enacted, such as the 2005 law signed by \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  that limited liability for gun manufacturers when their weapons are used in crimes.\par \par \uc2 * Mr. Biden could advance gun-control measures through executive orders, as he did in ordering the \uc2 Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives\uc2  to craft new restrictions on homemade firearm kits.\par \par \uc2 Zusha Elinson\par \par \uc2 Investigations\par \par \uc2 Republicans plan to initiate an aggressive series of investigations aimed at Mr. Biden and his administration. House Republicans spent much of the past year issuing document-preservation requests to various departments to lay the groundwork for the probes.\par \par \uc2 * Republicans expect to focus on Mr. Biden and his family. At least one likely committee chairman has said he wants to determine whether the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/hunter-bidens-ex-business-partner-alleges-father-knew-about-venture-11603421247"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 business dealings }}}\uc2  of Mr. Biden's son \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  compromise national security. Both Bidens have denied wrongdoing. The Wall Street Journal, reporting on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business dealings, didn't find President Biden to have been involved in his son's efforts.\par \par \uc2 * Republicans are likely to dig into the Justice Department and the \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2 . Some in the GOP say the Justice Department has become politicized. Among other things, Republicans were infuriated when Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  authorized the application for a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/merrick-garland-weighed-search-of-trumps-mar-a-lago-for-weeks-11660601292"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warrant to seize records }}}\uc2  with more than 700 pages of classified material that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took to his Florida home. Mr. Garland has said that he is focused on upholding the rule of law fairly.\par \par \uc2 * House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy (R., Calif.), {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kevin-mccarthy-faces-first-test-in-speakership-bid-11668513601"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who will become speaker }}}\uc2  if his party stays united behind him, has been weighing whether to continue the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/jan-6-committee-hearing-on-capitol-riot-set-to-feature-video-interviews-and-live-testimony-11654765200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 select committee }}}\uc2  investigating the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/jan-6-2021-how-it-unfolded-11613047105"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jan. 6, 2021, riot at the Capitol }}}\uc2 . The committee is supposed to dissolve 30 days after the issuance of a final report, which is slated to be released soon. Reinstating the panel would allow Republicans to reframe the probe, but it would also keep the issue in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 Siobhan Hughes\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-gop-control-of-the-house-means-for-inflation-taxes-healthcare-11668681002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 What GOP Control of the House Means for Inflation, Taxes, Healthcare }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221117eibh00132\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
