\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Politics Loom Over U.S. Catholic Bishops' Vote; The group's elections follow controversy over how to deal with President Biden and other Catholic politicians who support  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By Francis X. Rocca \par \uc2 1,071 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2022\par 14:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. Catholic bishops will choose a national president this week in elections that will help shape their public policy agenda and hence their relations with Washington\u8212\'20\'14and the Vatican\u8212\'20\'14over the next three years.\par \par \uc2 The outcome is likely to signal that the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops, which is holding its annual fall assembly Nov. 14-17 in Baltimore, will continue to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/two-california-bishops-personify-catholic-divide-over-politics-of-abortion-11655038801"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 give priority to opposition to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  over other issues }}}\uc2  on which it is active, including poverty and migration, rather than take its cues from \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 . The pope has spoken out strongly against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet given greater emphasis to other issues, including social and economic justice and the environment, and he has taken a more conciliatory approach than the USCCB leadership to President Biden, a practicing Catholic who supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 All U.S. bishops agree that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is wrong and should be illegal, but disagree over whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should outrank other concerns and how to deal with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/catholic-bishops-debate-communion-for-biden-11623937299"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Catholic politicians who dissent from the church's teaching }}}\uc2 . Most of the 10 bishops on the ballot for USCCB president this week would be likely, based on their records, to pursue the current leadership's line on both matters.\par \par \uc2 Chieko Noguchi, spokeswoman for the USCCB, declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The USCCB voted to call opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  its "pre-eminent priority" in 2019, over vocal opposition from a prominent minority of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/pope-struggles-to-contain-conservative-liberal-tensions-in-catholic-church-11616245220"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 U.S. bishops allied with Pope Francis }}}\uc2 . The election of Mr. Biden heightened tensions over the issue among the bishops and between USCCB leaders and the Vatican.\par \par \uc2 Archbishop Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Gomez of Los Angeles, the current president of the USCCB, marked Mr. Biden's inauguration day with a statement that criticized the new president for supporting "policies that would advance moral evils," including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , contraception and same-sex marriage. \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  sent a congratulatory message the same day that didn't mention any of those issues.\par \par \uc2 The USCCB's internal politics were dominated for much of 2021 by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/catholic-bishops-drop-effort-to-ban-communion-for-politicians-who-support-abortion-rights-11637169752"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 debate over whether to deny Communion }}}\uc2  to Mr. Biden and other Catholic politicians who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, such as House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . A proposal for national guidelines on when to impose such a ban was opposed by the Vatican, which cautioned that it could be divisive. The pope himself warned against politicizing the reception of Communion.\par \par \uc2 Faced with that opposition, the bishops ultimately adopted a document on the Eucharist that didn't explicitly address the matter.\par \par \uc2 But in May, Archbishop Salvatore Cordileone of San Francisco said he would ban Communion for Mrs. Pelosi, whose home district is in his archdiocese, because of her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Archbishop Cordileone is a candidate for USCCB president. At least two other candidates, Archbishop Paul Coakley of Oklahoma City and Bishop Michael Burbidge of Arlington, Va., publicly supported Archbishop Cordileone in his action regarding Mrs. Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 She has in the past defended her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a separation between her religious and political views.\par \par \uc2 Bishop Burbidge is one of two candidates for president, along with Archbishop William Lori of Baltimore, to have issued strong statements in recent weeks denouncing Mr. Biden's pledge to codify a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in federal law.\par \par \uc2 Another candidate, Bishop Kevin Rhoades of Fort Wayne-South Bend, Ind., who oversaw the drafting of last year's statement on Communion, publicly criticized the \uc2 University of Notre Dame\uc2 , a Catholic institution that is in his diocese, for honoring then-Vice President Biden in 2015. The bishop cited Mr. Biden's support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and same-sex marriage as reasons for his objection to the honor.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What outcomes do you hope to see from the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 According to USCCB insiders, an especially strong candidate for president is Archbishop Timothy Broglio, a former Vatican diplomat who now oversees ministry to Catholics in the U.S. Armed Forces and holds the conference's third-highest post of secretary. He has been a less-prominent voice on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than some other bishops but is considered a doctrinal conservative, and last year supported military members seeking a religious exemption from a Covid-19 vaccine mandate because of the vaccines' links to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -derived stem-cell line. The archbishop noted that the Vatican and the USCCB had concluded that it was morally permissible for Catholics to receive the vaccines.\par \par \uc2 The bishops could signal a shift in their approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics if they elect Archbishop Paul Etienne of Seattle, who was one of a minority of bishops who last year opposed the drafting of guidelines on Communion for Catholic politicians who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. He argued that such a statement would inappropriately enmesh the Eucharist with politics.\par \par \uc2 According to Massimo Faggioli, a professor of theology at \uc2 Villanova University\uc2 , it is noteworthy that the roster of presidential candidates includes none of the four current U.S. bishops raised to the rank of cardinal by \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 , all progressives who oppose the USCCB's current approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics.\par \par \uc2 He suggested that none of the cardinals ran to avoid a loss that would underscore the pope's failure to change the conference's orientation. Such a loss "would be a sign of weakness," Mr. Faggioli said.\par \par \uc2 The bishops could show support for the pope's wider agenda by electing Bishop Daniel Flores of Brownsville, Texas, who oversaw the USCCB's contribution to the ongoing global synod, a consultation with clergy and laypeople that the pope inaugurated last year.\par \par \uc2 That process, scheduled to culminate in a gathering at the Vatican in 2024, has disquieted conservatives by raising previously taboo issues such as LGBTQ relationships and women's ordination.\par \par \uc2 But according to Philip Lawler, editor of Catholic World News, the bishops' willingness to go their own way on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics exemplifies the decentralized approach to church governance that \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  is encouraging with the synod.\par \par \uc2 "If you are promoting a synodal government of the church, then that's one of the possibilities: that different synods or different episcopal conferences will have different ideas and different views or priorities," he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Francis X. Rocca at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:francis.rocca@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 francis.rocca@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-politics-loom-over-u-s-catholic-bishops-vote-11668348001"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Politics Loom Over U.S. Catholic Bishops' Vote }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221113eibd000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
