\par\par \uc2 Midterm Election 2022\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Midterm Electorate Deeply Troubled by Inflation, America's Direction; National survey of Americans shows about half of voters considered inflation the single most important factor for their decisions\b0\par\par\uc2 By John McCormick \par \uc2 1,622 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par 02:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Two years after handing Democrats control of Washington, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/election-midterms-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voters on Tuesday cast ballots }}}\uc2  with overwhelming angst about the economy and little faith in President Biden's abilities to fix the nation's ills.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-different-groups-voted-in-the-2022-midterm-elections-11667955705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 large national survey of the electorate }}}\uc2  showed many Americans were looking to the midterm election for a change of direction in Washington at a moment when Republicans were well-positioned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/gop-prospects-rise-amid-economic-pessimism-wsj-poll-finds-11667295002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to take control of at least one chamber of Congress }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The highest inflation in four decades {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/inflation-strains-voters-across-income-levels-ahead-of-midterm-elections-11667817001"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was a dominant issue }}}\uc2 , with roughly half the electorate saying it was the single most important voting factor, according to preliminary results from a poll of more than 93,000 registered voters for The Wall Street Journal, the Associated Press and Fox News.\par \par \uc2 Creating a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/economic-pressures-weigh-on-voters-in-final-midterms-campaign-stretch-11667035804"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 challenging political environment for the party in power }}}\uc2  in Washington, roughly three quarters of voters said they think the nation is headed in the wrong direction. About six in 10 disapproved of the way Mr. Biden is handling the economy, while a majority said his policies have made inflation worse.\par \par \uc2 When combined with those who saw inflation as an important factor, but not the single most important one, roughly 9 in 10 voters identified rising prices as a significant consideration in their decision making.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-different-groups-voted-in-the-2022-midterm-elections-11667955705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 How People Voted }}}\par \par \uc2 Results of survey of about 115,000 registered voters\par \par \uc2 For those who said inflation was a factor in their vote, which ranked as a top concern, roughly half selected groceries or food prices as most burdensome to them among everyday expenses. Less than a fifth picked gas or transportation costs.\par \par \uc2 In addition to asking about inflation, the survey also asked voters to classify three other issues on a scale of importance in determining their vote: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/election-midterms-2022/card/in-janesville-wis-voters-express-concerns-about-democracy-and-economy-YtREJeQcnTEHSF6pjpPK"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the future of democracy }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/crime-ads-play-big-role-in-competitive-house-senate-races-11665454787"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 violent crime }}}\uc2 , and the Supreme Court's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-features-prominently-in-midterm-elections-after-demise-of-roe-v-wade-11667826002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision to eliminate the constitutional right to an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 . Voters could select multiple issues as important.\par \par \uc2 The future of democracy was rated by more than four in 10 as the single most important factor. The Supreme Court's decision and violent crime were each listed by about a quarter as their single most important issue.\par \par \uc2 Creating a challenging political environment for the party in power in Washington, roughly three quarters of voters said they think the nation is headed in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 The AP VoteCast survey, conducted Oct. 31 through Nov. 8 by NORC at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2 , has a margin of error of plus or minus less than 1 percentage point on the full sample.\par \par \uc2 Roughly three-quarters of voters in the survey said the economy was poor, or not so good, including a third who said it was in poor shape.\par \par \uc2 Offered a list of nine issues facing the country, almost half said the economy and jobs were the top issue facing the nation. No other issue, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and crime, was cited by more than about a tenth of voters as the nation's most important issue.\par \par \uc2 While views of the economy were overwhelmingly negative, many voters felt comfortable with their own finances. About two-thirds said their family's finances were holding steady or improving, while about a third said they are falling behind. Similarly, about two-thirds said they are confident that they could keep up with expenses, while one-third said they were not.\par \par \uc2 More voters disapproved than approved of the job being done by President Biden, but an even larger share felt negatively about Congress.\par \par \uc2 More than half of the electorate thinks Mr. Biden, 79, lacks "the mental capacity to effectively serve as president."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's age has prompted Republicans to question his ability to lead and caused some Democratic voters and officials to suggest he shouldn't run for another term in 2024. Mr. Biden's allies have dismissed those comments, pointing to his achievements in office, including a series of legislative victories, as evidence of his fitness.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who said he plans to make an announcement about his political future on Nov. 15, is viewed very unfavorably by about half of the electorate and more than half in total when those who feel somewhat unfavorable about him are included.\par \par \uc2 Among Republicans and those who lean that way, about two thirds consider themselves part of the Make America Great Again movement Mr. Trump started.\par \par \uc2 Roughly a fifth of voters view both parties as too tolerant of extremist groups. About a third say that of the GOP, while another third say that of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 Candidates and their allies spent roughly $9 billion on advertising during the two-year election cycle that led up to the election. More than half of voters said they have known all along how they would cast their ballot, while a third said over the course of the campaign and about a tenth decided in the last few days.\par \par \uc2 Separate Wall Street Journal interviews with voters from coast to coast showed it was pocketbook issues such as the price of food and gasoline that tended to be more dominant on the minds of those casting ballots than divisive socials issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and crime.\par \par \uc2 Ronald Clement, a 76-year-old retired truck driver and independent, said he supported all Republicans as he filled out his ballot Tuesday in Manchester, N.H. The state, where Democratic Sen. \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  is running for a second term against Republican challenger Don Bolduc, could play a role in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/midterm-elections-2022-results-senate-congress-11667880011"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 determining Senate control }}}\uc2 . \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
