\par \par \uc2 Joel Ungar split his vote in Pennsylvania's Senate and governor races because he wanted to back centrist candidates. PHOTO: Kris Maher/The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 At a polling location in Hialeah, Fla., a heavily Hispanic city west of Miami, Leticia Montero voted to re-elect Republicans \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  for governor and \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A 38-year-old Cuban-American with no party affiliation, Ms. Montero said Republicans are more aligned with her conservative views and more focused on strengthening the economy and spending less. She praised Mr. DeSantis for reopening the Florida economy early in the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden and Democrats, on the other hand, are mismanaging the economy and illegal immigration at the border, Ms. Montero said. She said that she voted for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  in the 2008 presidential election and used to be more open to Democratic candidates, but that the party had become too liberal and more focused on issues such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead of the economy.\par \par \uc2 "They have gone way to the left," said Ms. Montero, who works as an office manager. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
