\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters demonstrated outside the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  in late June. PHOTO: Allison Shelley for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 What does the race to win the House look like?\par \par \uc2 Most seats are considered safe by both parties and nonpartisan election analysts. The Cook Political Report, a nonpartisan election watcher, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cookpolitical.com/analysis/house/house-overview/house-rating-changes-10-more-blue-districts-move-gops-direction?redirect=6362b7589b90d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rates 35 of the country's 435 seats as tossups, }}}\uc2  meaning that either party has a good chance of victory. Of those tossups, 10 are seats that Republicans are defending. The remaining ones are all pickup opportunities for Republicans, who need less than half a dozen seats to snatch the majority.\par \par \uc2 These tossup races include seats in the Las Vegas area, where shifting dynamics among Latino voters are helping make some House races competitive; Ohio and Pennsylvania, Rust Belt states that have become a top-tier battleground since 2016; and Nebraska and Michigan, where looming threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in those states are testing whether Democrats can win over some Republicans who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Races in Virginia and the Northeast are also competitive.\par \par \uc2 Could Republicans or Democrats take control of the Senate?\par \par \uc2 As of Monday, Nov. 7, FiveThirtyEight, a poll aggregator, rated the outcome in the Senate as slightly favoring a GOP takeover.\par \par \uc2 Some of the crucial states include Pennsylvania, where Democratic Lt. Gov. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is facing Republican Mehmet Oz, the celebrity physician known as Dr. Oz, for retiring GOP Sen. \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 's seat. In Ohio, author and venture capitalist J.D. Vance is running against Rep. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , a Democrat, for retiring GOP Sen. Rob Portman's seat; and in North Carolina, Rep. \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  is running against Cheri Beasley, the former chief justice of the state Supreme Court, for retiring GOP Sen. \uc2 Richard Burr\uc2 's seat.\par \par \uc2 What are the top races for governor?\par \par \uc2 Some marquee races include incumbent Democrats running for re-election: Govs. \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2  of Nevada, \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  of Wisconsin and \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2  of Kansas.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, where GOP Gov. \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  is prevented by term limits from running again, Secretary of State \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , a Democrat, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kari-lake-unites-many-arizona-republicans-after-partys-earlier-divisions-over-trump-11666690203"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is running against }}}\uc2  Republican Kari Lake, a former television news anchor who has centered much of her campaign on supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's false claims of widespread fraud on the 2020 election. Polls show that race is tight.\par \par \uc2 Michigan will also be watched closely, as Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat, is leading in polls against her Republican challenger, Tudor Dixon, a political newcomer and conservative commentator.\par \par \uc2 And in Georgia, GOP Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is leading in polls against Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , the former state lawmaker whom he narrowly defeated in 2018. That rematch is one of the marquee races of the year, though Ms. Abrams is going into the fall as the underdog.\par \par \uc2 When will races be called?\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press can make projections on winners any time after polls close, though some tight races might take days before calls are made. It takes even longer for states to officially certify results. Still, as polls close on Election Day, the outlook should become clearer. Here is a run of show for the evening, all times Eastern:\par \par \uc2 7 p.m.: Virginia, and Georgia's Senate and governor's races\par \par \uc2 7:30 p.m.: Ohio and North Carolina's Senate races\par \par \uc2 8 p.m.: Pennsylvania and New Hampshire\par \par \uc2 9 p.m.: Arizona, Wisconsin and Colorado\par \par \uc2 10 p.m.: Nevada\par \par \uc2 11 p.m.: Washington Senate and 10 California House races\par \par \uc2 How are the election winners certified, and when are winners sworn in?\par \par \uc2 Even though the House and Senate are federal races, it falls on state and local officials to oversee the elections and postelection process. Unlike presidential elections, during which states select electors to the Electoral College and the results are certified by Congress, the final results in House and Senate races are certified at the state level.\par \par \uc2 As for the running of the elections themselves, that is typically handled by counties and towns, where votes are counted. There are over 10,000 election jurisdictions in the U.S., according to the National Conference of State Legislatures, a nonpartisan group.\par \par \uc2 Many states have an elected secretary of state as the top authority on elections who certifies results. Others have a chief election official selected by the legislature or the governor. Some have an election board or commission.\par \par \uc2 Thirteen states, including New Hampshire and Virginia, which have important midterm races, have as many as seven days to certify their election results, according to the NCSL.\par \par \uc2 Twenty states, including the Senate battlegrounds of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/in-wisconsin-election-skeptics-deploy-as-poll-watchers-for-midterms-11663579802?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wisconsin }}}\uc2 , Nevada, Georgia, Pennsylvania and Florida, have between eight and 14 days to certify their results.\par \par \uc2 Thirteen states, including Rust Belt states such as Michigan and Ohio, have as many as three weeks to certify their elections.\par \par \uc2 And just four states, including New York and California, which host important House matchups that could determine control of that chamber, have 22 or more days to certify results.\par \par \uc2 The new Congress meets on Jan. 3, 2023, and will convene for two years.\par \par \uc2 This explanatory article may be periodically updated.\par \par \uc2 Write to Joshua Jamerson at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:joshua.jamerson@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joshua.jamerson@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-to-know-about-midterm-elections-why-they-are-important-11663359723"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 When Are the 2022 Midterm Elections? What to Know }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221103eib3008px\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
