\par\par \uc2 Midterm Election 2022\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Midterm Elections 2022: A Guide to the Issues and Races to Watch; This special edition of the Capital Journal newsletter explains what you need to know as Election Day approaches\b0\par\par\uc2 By Miguel Gonzalez \par \uc2 1,215 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2022\par 13:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Welcome to a special edition of WSJ's politics newsletter looking at the 2022 midterm elections. To receive our weekday edition and future special editions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/newsletters?sub=230&mod=djemCapitalJournalDaybreak"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sign up }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/newsletters?sub=230"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Three Questions for WSJ's Catherine Lucey\par \par \uc2 WSJ: Why are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/white-suburban-women-swing-toward-backing-republicans-for-congress-11667381402"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suburban white women }}}\uc2  such a closely watched group of voters in the midterm elections?\par \par \uc2 Catherine Lucey: This has been a closely watched group for several reasons. White suburban women make up 20% of the electorate and really helped power Democratic victories in House races in 2018, when the party gained more than 40 seats and took control of the House. Also, Democrats have been hopeful that\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-pushes-some-suburban-women-in-west-michigan-toward-democrats-11666085403"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  would motivate }}}\uc2  this group in the midterms, following the Supreme Court decision {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-does-overturning-roe-v-wade-mean-explained-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  in June, which ended the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and returned the issue to the states.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: What have these voters said about their political leanings over the course of the campaign?\par \par \uc2 Catherine Lucey: The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/WSJ_Poll_Redacted_Oct_2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 latest Wall Street Journal poll }}}\uc2  shows a clear shift with this group since our last poll in August. The new survey found that they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/gop-prospects-rise-amid-economic-pessimism-wsj-poll-finds-11667295002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 favor Republicans }}}\uc2  in congressional races by 15 percentage points. In the Journal's August poll, white suburban women favored Democrats by 12 points. And that seems to be because these women are turning their attention to inflation and the economy over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and they think Republicans do a better job on those issues.\par \par \uc2 These voters' views of the economy had grown substantially more pessimistic from August to October, with 54% of them saying they think the U.S. is already in a recession and 74% saying the economy is headed in the wrong direction in October, compared with 43% and 59%, respectively, in the August poll.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: Are there any signals that President Biden should be concerned about in what white suburban women said in the survey?\par \par \uc2 Catherine Lucey: Definitely. His approval rating has dropped among white suburban women, with 38% approving and 60% disapproving of his job performance. In August, 51% approved and 48% disapproved.\par \par \uc2 He also is now losing among this group in a hypothetical 2024 matchup with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . In this poll, 41% of white suburban women said they would vote for Mr. Biden and 52% for Mr. Trump. In August, 55% said Mr. Biden and 39% Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Lucey is a reporter in The Wall Street Journal's Washington bureau. You can follow her on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  at @ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/catherine_lucey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 catherine_lucey }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/news/author/catherine-lucey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 read her articles }}}\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Election Day is two days away. Democrats and Republicans across the country are fighting for control of the House of Representatives, Senate, governors' mansions and state legislatures. The party in power in Washington typically loses ground in midterm elections, and this year the Democrats control the House, Senate and presidency.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal's final poll of the election season, released Nov. 1, showed Republicans getting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/gop-prospects-rise-amid-economic-pessimism-wsj-poll-finds-11667295002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a boost in support }}}\uc2  just ahead of Election Day, as pessimism about the economy and the direction of the country jumped to their highest levels of the year. The survey suggested that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights had grown less important in voting decisions than voters had indicated in the summer, after the Supreme Court in June ended the federal constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and returned the issue to the states.\par \par \uc2 Share of voters who said they preferred Republicans when asked which party they would support in their congressional district, according to the WSJ poll, compared with 44% for Democrats\par \par \uc2 While the two-point Republican lead is within the poll's margin of error of plus or minus 2.5 percentage points, it also marks a meaningful improvement for the GOP since the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democratic-midterm-prospects-improve-as-races-heat-up-wsj-poll-finds-11662024601"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last WSJ survey, }}}\uc2  in August, when Democrats held a three-point advantage on which party voters would back for Congress. Republican voters also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/midterms-elections-voting-2022-10-31/card/key-findings-from-wsj-s-poll-of-registered-voters-p1l1NDO3kTKPyT34a16v"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 show more enthusiasm }}}\uc2  to vote than do Democrats, the survey finds.\par \par \uc2 The House\par \par \uc2 Most seats are considered safe by both parties and nonpartisan election analysts. The Cook Political Report, a nonpartisan election watcher, rates 35 of the country's 435 seats as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cookpolitical.com/analysis/house/house-overview/house-rating-changes-10-more-blue-districts-move-gops-direction?redirect=6362b7589b90d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 "tossups," }}}\uc2  meaning that either party has a good chance of victory. Of those tossups, 10 are seats that Republicans are defending. The remaining ones are all pickup opportunities for Republicans, who need less than half a dozen seats to snatch the majority.\par \par \uc2 These tossup races include seats in the Las Vegas area, where shifting dynamics among Latino voters are helping make some House races competitive; Ohio and Pennsylvania, rust-belt states that have become a top-tier battleground since 2016; and Nebraska and Michigan, where looming threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in those states are testing whether Democrats can win over some Republicans who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The Senate\par \par \uc2 Poll aggregator FiveThirtyEight rated the outcome in the Senate as slightly favoring a GOP takeover as of Nov. 3. Some of the key states include {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/troubles-for-oz-and-fetterman-in-pennsylvania-keep-them-neck-and-neck-11667565820"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pennsylvania }}}\uc2 , where Democratic Lt. Gov. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is facing Republican Mehmet Oz, the celebrity physician known as "Dr. Oz," for retiring GOP Sen. \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 's seat. In Ohio, author and venture capitalist J.D. Vance {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ohios-ryan-offers-potential-road-map-for-democrats-struggling-in-rust-belt-11664616604"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is running against }}}\uc2  Rep. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , a Democrat, for retiring GOP Sen. Rob Portman's seat; and in North Carolina, Rep. \uc2 Ted Budd{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/north-carolina-senate-race-spotlights-shrinking-slice-of-persuadable-voters-11666603802"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is running against }}}\uc2 Cheri Beasley\uc2 , the former chief justice of the state Supreme Court, for retiring GOP Sen. \uc2 Richard Burr\uc2 's seat.\par \par \uc2 Gubernatorial Races\par \par \uc2 Closely watched races include incumbent Democrats {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/in-kansas-governor-race-even-the-democrat-touts-ties-to-donald-trump-11667310073"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 running for re-election }}}\uc2 : Govs. \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2  of Nevada, \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  of Wisconsin and \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2  of Kansas. In Arizona, where GOP Gov. \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  is prevented by term limits from running again, Secretary of State \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , a Democrat, is running against Republican Kari Lake, a former television news anchor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kari-lake-unites-many-arizona-republicans-after-partys-earlier-divisions-over-trump-11666690203"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who has centered much of her campaign }}}\uc2  on supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's false claims of widespread fraud in the 2020 election. Polls show that race is very tight.\par \par \uc2 Michigan {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-pushes-some-suburban-women-in-west-michigan-toward-democrats-11666085403"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will also be closely watched }}}\uc2 , as Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat, is leading in polls against her Republican challenger, Tudor Dixon, a political newcomer and conservative commentator.\par \par \uc2 And in Georgia, GOP Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is leading in polls against Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , the former state lawmaker whom he narrowly defeated in 2018. That rematch is one of the marquee races of the year, though Ms. Abrams is going into the fall as the underdog.\par \par \uc2 Further Reading\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/economic-pressures-weigh-on-voters-in-final-midterms-campaign-stretch-11667035804"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Economic Pressures Weigh on Voters in Final Midterms Campaign Stretch }}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/midterm-elections-2022-how-economic-concerns-are-shaping-the-vote-11665250300"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 How Economic Concerns Are Shaping the Vote }}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-pushes-some-suburban-women-in-west-michigan-toward-democrats-11666085403"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Pushes Some Suburban Women in West Michigan Toward Democrats }}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/some-black-chicagoans-hit-by-crime-consider-skipping-midterm-elections-11664309629"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Some Black Chicagoans Hit by Crime Consider Skipping Midterm Elections }}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/immigration-becomes-key-issue-in-competitive-pennsylvania-house-race-11666603802"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Immigration Becomes Key Issue in Competitive Pennsylvania House Race }}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/in-wisconsin-election-skeptics-deploy-as-poll-watchers-for-midterms-11663579802"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In Wisconsin, Election Skeptics Deploy as Poll Watchers for Midterms  }}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/latino-voters-republican-midterm-elections-11663166135?st=f83ypx483erhid0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Latino Voters, Once Solidly Democratic, Split Along Economic Lines }}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/uvalde-massacre-prompts-new-debate-on-guns-among-residents-11660037401"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Uvalde Massacre Prompts New Debate on Guns Among Residents }}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/as-biden-zeroes-in-on-student-loan-forgiveness-decision-voter-anxiety-grows-11653298200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 As Biden Zeroes In on Student-Loan Forgiveness Decision, Voter Anxiety Grows  }}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/covid-school-reopenings-midterm-elections-democrats-republicans-11649286564"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 School Reopening Mess Drives Frustrated Parents Toward GOP  }}}\par \par \uc2 About Us\par \par \uc2 This newsletter is written by the WSJ Washington bureau. Sign up {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/newsletters?sub=230"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here }}}\uc2 . Send feedback to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:capitaljournal@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 capitaljournal@wsj.com }}}\uc2 . You can follow politics coverage on our {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/news/politics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Politics }}}\uc2  page and at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WSJPolitics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @wsjpolitics }}}\uc2  on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221106eib6000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
