\par \par \uc2 The poll shows white suburban women trusted Republicans over Democrats to handle the economy and inflation. PHOTO: Emily Elconin for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14White suburban women, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wsj.com/livecoverage/latest-updates/midterms-elections-voting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a key group of midterm voters }}}\uc2 , have significantly shifted their support from Democrats to Republicans in the closing days of midterm campaigning because of rising concerns over the economy and inflation, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/WSJ_Poll_Redacted_Oct_2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the latest Wall Street Journal poll }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The survey, which included 297 white women living in suburbs, found that they favor Republicans in congressional races by 15 percentage points. That represented a substantial shift in recent weeks among a group that makes up 20% of the electorate. In the Journal's August poll, white suburban women favored Democrats by 12 percentage points. It also suggests that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-pushes-some-suburban-women-in-west-michigan-toward-democrats-11666085403"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the topic of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights }}}\uc2  has faded in importance after Democrats saw energy on that issue this summer in the wake of the Supreme Court's decision {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-does-overturning-roe-v-wade-mean-explained-11656081705?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to overturn Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We're talking about a collapse, if you will, in that group on the perceptions of the economy," said Republican pollster Tony Fabrizio who conducted the poll with Democratic pollster John Anzalone. The poll showed that 54% of white suburban women think the U.S. is already in a recession and 74% think the economy is headed in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 Views of the economy among the group were substantially more negative than in the Journal's most recent prior survey. In August, 43% thought the economy had entered a recession, and 59% said the economy was headed in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 The movement comes as several voter groups {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/gop-prospects-rise-amid-economic-pessimism-wsj-poll-finds-11667295002?mod=politics_lead_pos1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are giving Republicans a boost }}}\uc2  in the final stretch before Election Day. White suburban women were a powerful force in the Democrats' sweeping victories in House races in 2018, when the party gained more than 40 seats, many of them in suburban districts, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/graphics/midterm-house-races-2018/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 retook control of the House }}}\uc2 . Democrats had been optimistic over the summer that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would help motivate this group to turn out and back their candidates in November.\par \par \uc2 According to the survey, rising prices were the top issue motivating these voters, with 34% putting that as their number one priority, followed by 28% citing threats to democracy and 16% choosing the Supreme Court overturning Roe. White suburban women trusted Republicans over Democrats to handle the economy and inflation. They also expressed more negative views toward the state of the country and President Biden's leadership compared with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democratic-midterm-prospects-improve-as-races-heat-up-wsj-poll-finds-11662024601?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a previous survey in August }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A total of 85% of these voters said they were very motivated to vote, making them among the most-motivated groups of those surveyed.\par \par \uc2 Dana Gianassi, 68 years old, of Lincoln, Calif., said she has already voted for a straight Republican ticket. A registered independent, she leans Republican, but said she has never before voted straight GOP. "Right now I feel the Democrats are ruining our country," she said, citing her concerns about securing the southern border and high prices.\par \par \uc2 "We're on fixed incomes," she said. "The gas is unbelievable."\par \par \uc2 Asked who has a better economic plan, 50% of this cohort said Republicans in Congress and 35% said Democrats. On inflation, 55% said Republicans would get it under control and 24% said Democrats. In August, Democrats easily beat Republicans on the economic plans, though Republicans were ahead on the inflation question by a smaller margin.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Anne Ramsey, 76, of Darien, Conn., is an independent voter who has not made up her mind yet, but said she is leaning Republican because of her concerns about the economy.\par \par \uc2 "I think that generally I would trust Democrats on social issues but trust the Republicans more on monetary issues," she said. "I think that the economy is number one in my mind. It's costing people so much more money to live."\par \par \uc2 A combined 66% of white suburban women also said rising costs are causing a major or minor financial strain, compared with 54% in August.\par \par \uc2 "It is impacting us personally. I came out with a few grocery bags and I paid $120," said Susan Smith, 76, of Perkasie, Pa., a Republican who plans to vote for the GOP. "I eat Cheerios every morning and they've doubled in price."\par \par \uc2 Overall consumer prices rose 6.2% in September from a year earlier, the same pace as in August and near a 40-year high, according to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's preferred inflation measure, the personal-consumption expenditures price index. Households continue to benefit from a strong labor market, and gasoline prices are also down from a mid-June peak.\par \par \uc2 Overall, white suburban women trust Republicans more on crime and foreign policy. They said Democrats were better able to deal with lowering prescription drug prices. The parties were closely tied on which is best at handling the issues of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and education.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a concern for these women, but has been crowded by other issues, the pollsters said, noting that time has passed since the high court's ruling and the immediate outrage among some voters. In competitive districts, voters are seeing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/crime-ads-play-big-role-in-competitive-house-senate-races-11665454787"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advertising pushing other issues, such as crime }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It's absolutely true that these women have shifted their gaze more on the economy than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Democratic pollster Molly Murphy, who works with Mr. Anzalone. She said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still a concern, but "they think we're in a recession. A majority are feeling financial strain in this economy."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could play a bigger role in some states such as in Michigan, where the November ballot {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/michigan-supreme-court-orders-abortion-rights-proposal-be-included-on-november-ballot-11662674386"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 includes a question for voters }}}\uc2  about whether to provide a state constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Karen Streisfeld-Leitner, 58, of Croton-on-Hudson in Westchester County, N.Y., said she would be voting straight Democratic and said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains her top issue.\par \par \uc2 "I think it's a critical right being threatened and in many places being taken away, and I think there's a GOP assault that is happening on that right," she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Gianassi, the California voter, said she has concerns about crime. "We don't go out at night any more because of it," she said. "We don't go to Sacramento after dark. It has affected our lives."\par \par \uc2 The poll offered some warnings for Mr. Biden. His approval rating has dropped among white suburban women, with 38% approving and 60% disapproving of his job performance. In August, 51% approved and 48% disapproved. A total of 74% of white suburban women said the country was headed in the wrong direction, compared with 65% in August.\par \par \uc2 If the 2024 election were between Mr. Biden and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , 41% of white suburban women said they would vote for Mr. Biden and 52% Mr. Trump. In August, 55% said Mr. Biden and 39% Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal poll included 1,500 registered voters, who were reached by phone and text from Oct. 22-26. The sample of white suburban women was smaller and has a margin of error of plus or minus 5.7 percentage points or 8 percentage points on some of the policy questions.\par \par \uc2 Write to Catherine Lucey at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:catherine.lucey@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 catherine.lucey@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/white-suburban-women-swing-toward-backing-republicans-for-congress-11667381402"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 White Suburban Women Swing Toward Backing Republicans for Congress  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221102eib2000jh\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
