\par\par \uc2 Midterm Election 2022\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Republican Election Prospects Rise as Inflation Overshadows  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , WSJ Poll Finds; New survey shows GOP with an edge in midterm voting enthusiasm as economic pessimism grows\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joshua Jamerson \par \uc2 1,209 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2022\par 14:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Voters are giving Republicans a late boost in support just ahead of the midterm elections, as pessimism about the economy and the direction of the country jump to their highest levels of the year, a new Wall Street Journal poll finds.\par \par \uc2 The survey, conducted about two weeks before Election Day, suggests that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are less important in voting decisions than voters indicated in the summer, after the Supreme Court in June {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-does-overturning-roe-v-wade-mean-explained-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ended the federal constitutional right to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 . Republicans have regained momentum since then and now hold a slight edge over Democrats, 46% to 44%, when voters are asked which party they would support in their congressional district if the election were held today.\par \par \uc2 View the results\par \par \uc2 See how the poll results have changed over the last year.\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/WSJ_Poll_Redacted_Oct_2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 WSJ October Survey }}}\par \par \uc2 "The focus on the economic stuff, particularly inflation, is helpful to the GOP headed into the final stretch," said Republican pollster Tony Fabrizio, who conducted the survey with Democratic pollster John Anzalone. Mr. Fabrizio said that voters who haven't decided which party to back for Congress show high levels of concern about the economy and "look more likely to break Republican than they do Democrat, if they vote."\par \par \uc2 While the two-point Republican lead is within the poll's margin of error of plus or minus 2.5 percentage points, it also marks a meaningful improvement for the GOP since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democratic-midterm-prospects-improve-as-races-heat-up-wsj-poll-finds-11662024601"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Journal's last survey }}}\uc2 , in August, when Democrats held a three-point advantage on which party voters would back for Congress. Republican voters also show more enthusiasm to vote than do Democrats, the survey finds.\par \par \uc2 The shift in preference for the GOP comes as views of the economy have turned more dour than at any point in the past year. Only 19% say the economy is headed in the right direction, down 11 percentage points from August, while 71% say the economy is on the wrong track.\par \par \uc2 Voters lack confidence in President Biden's economic leadership, with only 27% saying his policies have had a positive impact on the economy and 54% saying they have had a negative impact. A majority of voters\u8212\'20\'1455%\u8212\'20\'14disapprove of Mr. Biden's overall job performance, compared with 43% who approve.\par \par \uc2 The GOP has seen a shift in its favor among several voter groups, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/story/working-class-latino-voters-once-solidly-democratic-are-shifting-toward-republicans-a7578ecc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including Latino voters }}}\uc2  and women, and particularly white suburban women. That group, which the pollsters said makes up 20% of the electorate, shifted 26 percentage points away from Democrats since the Journal's August poll and now favors the GOP by 15 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Midterm elections are generally considered a referendum on the party in power, and Democrats control both chambers of Congress and the White House.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Anzalone said the most concerning matter for Democrats is that independent voters don't trust the party to handle rising prices. Whichever party is in power, he said, finds that independent voters "break against the party when you have economic diagnostics like we're seeing. End of story, done." He added, "Voters trust the Republicans on that, right now. That's tough sledding for us."\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Hackworth is a stay-at-home mother of three from Cranberry Township, Pa., outside of Pittsburgh. She's noticed her kids' clothes getting more expensive, as well as rising prices for milk and other grocery staples. "The Dems have kind of proved that they haven't been able to make a change with [inflation]. It's just gotten worse in the last couple of years," said Ms. Hackworth.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hackworth, 39 years old, said she typically leans Republican but had considered voting for Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/midterms-elections-voting-2022-10-31/card/q-a-pennsylvania-s-top-contests-in-focus-f0INQphkLJtDgE7Gfvq7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the state's hotly contested Senate race }}}\uc2 . While still undecided, she's leaning toward the GOP's Mehmet Oz, despite feeling more aligned on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with Mr. Fetterman, who supports legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "While I would want to support the right for a woman to choose, I also want to support our economy getting better, because it's not right now," Ms. Hackworth said, adding later: "Give the Republicans a chance in Congress. See what they can do."\par \par \uc2 Some 48% of voters said they thought congressional Republicans were best able to get inflation under control\u8212\'20\'14the largest share in Journal polling this year. By comparison, 27% said congressional Democrats were best able to rein in inflation. Democrats were viewed as the party best able to handle \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, lower prescription drug prices and improve education.\par \par \uc2 In other signs of concern about the economy, 64% of poll respondents said that inflation is rising and straining their finances, including 36% who described those strains as major. Both figures are the highest in The Journal's polling this year. Only 27% of respondents rate the economy as excellent or good, down from 35% in August.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say they are more motivated to vote than Democrats, the poll found. Some 68% of Black voters and 64% of Hispanic voters\u8212\'20\'14groups that favor Democrats\u8212\'20\'14rated themselves at the highest level of motivation to vote, far below the 83% of white voters and 79% of voters overall.\par \par \uc2 Survey participants also rated the GOP as the party best able to reduce crime. Republican admakers have flooded competitive House districts and Senate races {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/crime-ads-play-big-role-in-competitive-house-senate-races-11665454787"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 with a barrage of attacks }}}\uc2  focused on voters' increased fears about violent crime.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Smith, a supermarket worker in Brooklyn, said that crime is at the top of mind for her this year, in addition to the economy. Still, Ms. Smith, a self-described liberal, said she was leaning toward backing Democratic Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  for re-election over her GOP challenger \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . That race has gotten unexpectedly competitive in recent weeks, with Mr. Zeldin {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/midterms-elections-voting-2022-10-24/card/hochul-zeldin-face-off-in-new-york-gubernatorial-debate-KFVeRy42PNdE1ifOJVgP"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 putting an emphasis on cutting crime }}}\uc2  in New York City.\par \par \uc2 "The crime is always going to be there. It's New York,'' said Ms. Smith. "It's just a point of keeping it at a steady level so that we don't have to feel terribly scared to walk outside."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Smith, 32, said that despite a spike in crime at a time when Democrats lead local government, she trusted Democrats more to address the issue and thought Mr. Zeldin was unlikely to come to New York City communities if elected governor.\par \par \uc2 Even though the midterm elections haven't concluded yet, both parties have started to think about who might be their standard-bearer in 2024. Mr. Biden has said he intends to run again; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has teased another White House bid but hasn't announced a decision.\par \par \uc2 The Journal poll found that a hypothetical 2024 rematch between Messrs. Biden and Trump is tied, with each man drawing 46%. In August, Mr. Biden was ahead by 6 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal poll included 1,500 registered voters, who were reached by phone and text from Oct. 22-26.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What are you prioritizing as you vote in the midterms this year? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Write to Joshua Jamerson at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:joshua.jamerson+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joshua.jamerson+1@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/gop-prospects-rise-amid-economic-pessimism-wsj-poll-finds-11667295002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republican Election Prospects Rise as Inflation Overshadows \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22 , WSJ Poll Finds  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221101eib10010a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
