\par\par \uc2 Film Review\par \uc2 Arts\par \b \uc2 'Call Jane' Review: Feminists Underground; Elizabeth Banks and Sigourney Weaver star in a film about the Jane Collective, a Chicago activist group that facilitated abortions in the years before Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Smith \par \uc2 730 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2022\par 22:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The pointed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drama "Call Jane" seems unlikely to change any minds when it comes to an always-contentious issue, but this late-'60s period piece successfully channels some of the righteous rage that fueled second-wave feminism, which engineered one triumph after another and completely reconfigured women's role in American society within a few years.  \par \par \uc2 As the film opens, patriarchy rules and is little questioned by such unassuming figures as Joy, a naive, timid Chicago housewife sensitively played by Elizabeth Banks. In an early scene, Joy is puzzled by a 1968 street protest indicating growing political anger that seems alien to her quiet and well-ordered life. Her husband, a litigator named Will (Chris Messina), relies heavily on her input when writing briefs, suggesting that she could be a better lawyer than he if they lived in a society that recognized women's talents. Neither spouse considers that possibility; women are simply second-class citizens. \par \par \uc2 When Joy's doctor tells her that her unborn child has a severe birth defect and she must have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because her life is in danger, she submits to the indignity of asking the hospital's all-male board for permission. Her request is unanimously rejected. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is generally illegal, but fliers have begun appearing on Chicago streets directing women who are unhappy about their pregnancies to "Call Jane," meaning contact a secretive activist group of women. There is no Jane, but the Jane Collective, led by a cynical, tough feminist named Virginia (Sigourney Weaver), has a running agreement with a sketchy male abortionist (Cory Michael Smith) to pick up clients and bring them blindfolded to his office, where he charges $600 for each termination. \par \par \uc2 After undergoing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  herself and befriending the leader of the activist group, Joy has a kind of feminist awakening and starts spending evenings with her new pals to discuss what was then just starting to be called women's liberation. Her husband grouses that she is spending so much time at "art class" (her cover story) that he is stuck eating frozen meat loaf. Nor does her adolescent daughter (Grace Edwards) fail to notice her mother's suspicious behavior. Joy dismisses their complaints; the collective becomes her spiritual family, and she starts to play an increasingly assertive role within it, for instance strategizing about how more abortions might be made available for more women, including those unable to pay.  \par \par \uc2 "Call Jane" lacks a substantial plot\u8212\'20\'14it's an unabashed piece of propaganda in the form of a character study\u8212\'20\'14and its script is thin. Despite the illegal nature of what the Jane Collective does, neither director Phyllis Nagy nor her screenwriters Hayley Schore and Roshan Sethi create any suspense around the possibility of getting caught. The collective pays protection money to mobsters, which amounts to a user fee that enables them to proceed with impunity. Mostly the film is a dramatically underwhelming series of scenes built around talking points aimed wholly at a pro-choice audience eager to applaud feminism (the real-life Jane Collective claimed to facilitate 11,000 abortions) and to sneer at the men in the film, none of whom come off well. Will, Joy's husband, is a decent fellow, but he is so steeped in sexism that he can't even conceive of making his own dinner even as he leverages his wife's editing skills to rise in his profession. \par \par \uc2 The film's timing would appear to lend it some salience, but really it doesn't. Though it was shot before the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June and arrives in theaters with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as hotly debated as it has ever been, it has no new insights or approaches to the topic. When it comes to a subject that has been exhaustively debated for more than 50 years, simply having characters rehash those arguments isn't enough. There is less artistry to the film than there is sloganeering. "Call Jane" would be more effective if it stuck to human drama rather than having its characters make sweeping assertions that sound like stump speeches given at political rallies.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/call-jane-review-roe-v-wade-abortion-movie-11666908268"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 'Call Jane' Review: Feminists Underground }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221027eiar0083p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
