\par\par \uc2 Midterm Election 2022\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Mehmet Oz, John Fetterman Clash Over Crime,  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  in Pennsylvania Senate Debate; Democratic nominee, who suffered a stroke in May, stumbled over his words in the sole public face-off\b0\par\par\uc2 By Aaron Zitner \par \uc2 1,207 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par 16:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz clashed over crime, fracking and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/john-fettermans-health-in-spotlight-after-uneven-debate-against-mehmet-oz-11666805642"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a testy Pennsylvania Senate debate }}}\uc2  Tuesday that featured frequent verbal stumbles by Mr. Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 The debate put the communication challenges related to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/john-fettermans-health-clouds-pennsylvania-senate-race-11657713602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a stroke Mr. Fetterman suffered }}}\uc2  earlier this year in the foreground of one of the year's most consequential elections for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The debate between Mr. Oz, the celebrity heart doctor, and Mr. Fetterman, Pennsylvania's lieutenant governor, was a highly anticipated event in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wsj.com/livecoverage/latest-updates/midterms-elections-voting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 neck-and-neck Senate contest }}}\uc2 , which offers the Democratic Party {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/fetterman-facing-health-questions-boosts-public-schedule-in-pennsylvania-senate-bid-11663418351"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its best chance to pick up a seat }}}\uc2  now held by Republicans in the 50-50 chamber.\par \par \uc2 The two candidates differed sharply on multiple issues during the debate, held in a Harrisburg television studio. But Mr. Fetterman's verbal missteps were among the most notable aspects of the evening, as he continually tripped over his words. He opened his first answer by saying, "Good night, everybody," rather than "Good evening."\par \par \uc2 At another point, Mr. Fetterman was asked to square his support for natural-gas fracking, an important economic driver in parts of Pennsylvania, with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/10/18/politics/john-fetterman-pennsylvania-fracking-support-kfile/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 past statement in opposition }}}\uc2 . "I do support fracking, and I don't, I don't\u8212\'20\'14I support fracking and I stand, and I do support fracking," he said.\par \par \uc2 At Mr. Fetterman's request, video monitors had been placed on the debate stage so that he could follow the conversation through closed-captioning. Calling his stroke "the elephant in the room," Mr. Fetterman said at the start of the evening: "I might miss some words during the debate, mush two words together. But it knocked me down, but I'm going to keep coming back up."\par \par \uc2 Connecting his struggles to those of voters, he said he would fight "for everyone in Pennsylvania that ever got knocked down."\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Emilia Rowland, a Fetterman campaign spokeswoman, dismissed the idea that his support among voters might be hurt by his debate performance. "Political \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is one thing,'' she said, "but real people who watched the debate last night\u8212\'20\'14their takeaway is here's a guy who was in the hospital months ago and is now on the debate stage talking about the issues, and his positions and his values are crystal clear.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Oz used the debate to call Mr. Fetterman too lenient on criminals, the main topic in a barrage of ads that he and allied GOP groups have run in recent weeks. "\uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  during this crime wave has been trying to get as many murderers convicted and sentenced to life in prison out of jail as possible," Mr. Oz said. His comment was a reference to Mr. Fetterman's role in state government as head of the Board of Pardons, where he has pushed to increase the number of pardons.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fetterman responded that he had run for mayor of Braddock, a small borough near Pittsburgh, in 2005 because he was disturbed by gun violence.\par \par \uc2 "And working with the police and working with our community, I would say I was able to stop gun violence for 5\u189\'00\'BD years as mayor," he said. "I'm the only person on this stage right now that is successful about pushing back against gun violence."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fetterman pressed his case that Mr. Oz doesn't understand the economic challenges facing Pennsylvanians, because he had moved into the state from his longtime home in New Jersey at about the time he announced his candidacy. "I'm running to serve Pennsylvania. He's running to use Pennsylvania," Mr. Fetterman said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Oz answered later that he had traveled across the state and had heard the problems of its residents. Mr. Oz, who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/pennsylvania-senate-nominee-john-fetterman-discloses-heart-condition-following-stroke-11654296676"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 narrowly won the GOP primary }}}\uc2  with a late endorsement from former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , also sought to portray himself as a uniting figure.\par \par \uc2 "I'm a surgeon. I'm not a politician," he said. "We take big problems. We focus on them and we fix them. We do it by uniting by coming together, not divided."\par \par \uc2 A series of recent polls show Mr. Fetterman with a narrow lead of about 2 percentage points, within the surveys' margins of error. The winner will succeed GOP Sen. \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 , who is retiring.\par \par \uc2 The candidates differed sharply on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with Mr. Fetterman saying that he would fight to re-establish a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as established under Roe v. Wade, the 1973 Supreme Court ruling that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was struck down by the high court }}}\uc2  this summer. Beyond saying he supported Roe v. Wade, Mr. Fetterman wouldn't specify whether he backed any restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Oz focused on his stance that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation should be left to the states rather than the federal government, though he didn't answer directly when asked if he would support a proposal from GOP Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R., S.C.) for a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/lindsey-graham-defends-abortion-proposal-despite-gop-worries-11663524124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 federal ban at 15 weeks of pregnancy }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I want women, doctors, local political leaders, letting the democracy that's always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward so states can decide for themselves," Mr. Oz said. He didn't fully state his position on legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which according to his campaign is that it should be illegal except in cases of rape, incest and when the life of the mother is at risk.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the federal minimum wage, Mr. Fetterman said he supported raising the current $7.25 hourly rate. "If you work full time, you should be able to live in dignity," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Oz said market forces had already pushed up wages, and he suggested that a higher minimum wage would present a challenge to employers. "You can't put businesses out of commission in order to pay more wages," he said, adding that "the wages will go to zero."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Oz didn't refer directly to his opponent's verbal missteps but repeated his assertion that Mr. Fetterman had shielded himself from voters and the media to hide his policy views and health condition.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How do you think the Fetterman-Oz debate will affect the race? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 "This is the only debate I could get you to come to talk to me on, and I had to beg on my knees to get you to come in," Mr. Oz said.\par \par \uc2 The Oz campaign had wanted multiple debates, and it wanted some to be held before Sept. 19, when state law allowed counties to begin early voting. Already, more than 550,000 people have cast ballots in the state, according to Democratic-allied TargetSmart, a voter-data firm. That total is about 11% of the ballots cast for Senate in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether he had been forthcoming about his health challenge, Mr. Fetterman said: "To be for transparency is about showing up. I'm here today to have a debate."\par \par \uc2 Write to Aaron Zitner at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:aaron.zitner@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aaron.zitner@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/john-fetterman-mehmet-oz-meet-for-lone-debate-in-pennsylvania-senate-race-11666699201"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mehmet Oz, John Fetterman Clash Over Crime, \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  in Pennsylvania Senate Debate }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221025eiap001p5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
