\par\par \uc2 Midterm Election 2022\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Key Democrats Urge Candidates to Focus on Economy in Midterm Elections; Many campaigns have avoided addressing inflation, focusing instead on issues less on voters' minds\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Thomas \par \uc2 1,239 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par 22:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Patrick Gaspard is president and chief executive of the Center for American Progress, a Democratic think tank. An earlier version of this article incorrectly identified the organization as the Center for American Prosperity. (Corrected on Oct. 27)\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14With their narrow majorities in Congress imperiled, some high-profile Democrats are urging their midterm election candidates to confront head-on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/high-inflation-darkens-global-economic-outlook-11665918002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high inflation }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/businesses-expect-economy-to-weaken-feds-beige-book-says-11666204332"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 economic anxiety }}}\uc2  and boost efforts to define Republicans as obstructing attempts to rein in costs facing Americans.\par \par \uc2 The message comes as some Democrats fear the party has focused too heavily on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/midterms-elections-voting-2022-10-24/card/q-a-abortion-s-impact-on-the-2022-midterm-elections-b6njyQcM3PBf0gynlQnj"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 restoring \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights }}}\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's attacks on democratic institutions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/republican-midterm-prospects-brighten-in-closing-weeks-of-election-11666171802"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ceding ground to Republicans }}}\uc2  on the economy as voters struggle with high living costs. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/midterm-elections-2022-how-economic-concerns-are-shaping-the-vote-11665250300"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 importance of the economy }}}\uc2 to voters and polls showing their preference for the GOP on the issue have made nonpartisan analysts more confident that Republicans will capture control of the House, while the Senate majority remains a tossup.\par \par \uc2 "If you go out and you ask the average American what's on their mind, what's on their mind is the economy, what's on their mind is inflation. It's not conceivable to me that you can run away from those issues," said Sen. Bernie Sanders, a Vermont independent who twice sought the Democratic presidential nomination and will be appearing at campaign events this week in Oregon, Nevada, California, Texas and Florida.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sanders added: "There has not been enough of a contrast between the reactionary Republican position on the economy and what Democrats have done, can do, and should do."\par \par \uc2 The Nov. 8 elections will serve as a progress report on President Biden's first two years in office and Democrats' razor-thin majorities in the Senate and the House, with the party in power nearly always losing seats in midterms. It will coincide with levels of inflation not seen since the early 1980s but a resilient jobs market, which showed an unemployment rate in September of 3.5% and has averaged gains of about 420,000 jobs a month in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Complicating the picture is a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/talk-of-fed-rate-slowdown-offers-respite-for-bonds-and-stocks-11666542473"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 campaign of interest-rate rises }}}\uc2  by the Federal Reserve\u8212\'20\'14including another expected 0.75 percentage point increase in November\u8212\'20\'14that is designed to tamp inflation by slowing the economy without causing steep job losses in an economy that some already believe is in recession. So far, inflation has remained high and mortgage rates and other interest-rate-sensitive sectors have become more expensive.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden, in both his campaign appearances and official events from the White House, has sought to brand the coming elections as a choice between his policies and those likely to emerge under Republican rule. He has frequently accused Republicans of seeking to slash Medicare and Social Security benefits and, more recently, of using future increases in the debt ceiling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-says-he-wouldnt-support-eliminating-debt-ceiling-11666372197"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to extract spending cuts }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How should Democrats discuss the economy on the campaign trail? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Some key Republicans have suggested changes to entitlement programs, such as means-testing or raising the eligibility age for recipients. House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy (R., Calif.) has said his party would work to strengthen those programs, but also said in a recent interview with Punchbowl News that Republicans would use raising the debt limit as a way to demand spending cuts. "You can't just continue down the path to keep spending and adding to the debt," Mr. McCarthy said.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court's decision in June to overturn Roe v. Wade, which had established a federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Democrats from Mr. Biden on down have also emphasized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , crediting the ruling with energizing rank-and-file Democrats and boosting voter registration, particularly among women.\par \par \uc2 And Mr. Biden used a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-to-say-in-prime-time-address-democracy-is-at-stake-in-midterm-elections-11662048825"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high-profile address }}}\uc2  in September outside Philadelphia's Independence Hall to frame the elections as a continuing battle for the "soul of the nation," warning that "equality and democracy are under assault."\par \par \uc2 Yet polls have consistently shown that voters care most about economic issues, and they give Republicans an advantage on the topic. A New York Times/Siena College poll conducted Oct. 9-12 found that 26% identified the economy as the most important problem facing the country, followed by inflation at 19%. The state of democracy registered at 7%, while \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was identified by 4%.\par \par \uc2 Pointing to these trends, a group of prominent Democratic strategists recently urged the party's candidates to talk more about the economy, emphasizing a starker contrast with Republicans. They said Democrats should make the case that Republicans are beholden to wealthy corporations, and that the GOP tried to block efforts to reduce healthcare costs.\par \par \uc2 "Democrats need to understand that we have a winning message on the economy and inflation. But rising costs will beat us if we avoid the issue," they wrote in a memo published by The American Prospect, a progressive magazine.\par \par \uc2 The strategists\u8212\'20\'14pollsters Stan Greenberg and Celinda Lake, strategist Mike Lux, and \uc2 Patrick Gaspard\uc2 , president and CEO of the \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2 , a Democratic think tank\u8212\'20\'14said that while "no Democratic candidate should stop talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the party's closing message needs to focus on "the cost of living, inflation and the economy."\par \par \uc2 So far, few Democratic campaigns have followed that advice. A search of transcripts compiled by ad-tracker AdImpact shows Republican House and Senate candidates and their allies have mentioned the word inflation in more than 112,000 airings on broadcast and cable television since Labor Day. That compares with about 15,500 for Democrats and their allies.\par \par \uc2 Republicans said the memo's advice was too little, too late to save Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Scott Reed, a Republican strategist, called it "the first volley of the blame game that is coming on hard."\par \par \uc2 "The Democrats missed the messaging this cycle. It didn't help that Biden and the White House were all over the place. But they overplayed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and they missed that everyone is getting their 401(k) retirement reports and they're all getting crushed," Mr. Reed said. The Dow Jones Industrial Average has declined about 12% in 2022.\par \par \uc2 "I think they've been a little tone deaf on what people really care about. And they seem to keep trying to talk them into caring about things they don't care about," he said.\par \par \uc2 Other Democrats argue that beyond Nov. 8, the party faces a long-term challenge of connecting with working-class voters in the years ahead.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  (D., Conn.) said the party needs to develop an economic message to resonate with voters in rural areas and parts of the country that have experienced economic dislocation. But more immediately, he said, the party must compete with Republicans for voters who may not approve of Mr. Biden but are uncertain about what GOP rule in Congress might mean for the country.\par \par \uc2 "I think there are votes to be had amongst people who may not love the Democrat but are really not interested in the next year-and-a-half being about government shutdowns and debt defaults," Mr. Murphy said.\par \par \uc2 John McCormick contributed to the article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ken Thomas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:ken.thomas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ken.thomas@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/key-democrats-urge-candidates-to-focus-on-economy-in-closing-days-of-midterm-elections-11666788987"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Key Democrats Urge Candidates to Focus on Economy in Midterm Elections  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221026eiaq00209\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
