\par \par \uc2 North Carolina Democratic Senate candidate Cheri Beasley, right, says she may sway some voters angry over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. PHOTO: Joshua Jamerson/The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 GREENSBORO, N.C.\u8212\'20\'14In one of the closest Senate contests in the country, both political parties have emphasized energizing their bases more than trying to persuade a shrinking pool of undecided voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wsj.com/livecoverage/latest-updates/midterms-elections-voting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ahead of the midterm elections }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The race between Democrat Cheri Beasley, a former chief justice of the North Carolina Supreme Court, and Rep. \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , a Republican backed by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/primary-election-results-pennysylvania-north-carolina-oregon/card/ted-budd-wins-gop-governor-race-in-north-carolina-SiRGTitdJ5lKc1gWHPyH-SiRGTitdJ5lKc1gWHPyH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has been close for months }}}\uc2 , as many elections in North Carolina have been in recent years. Mr. Budd holds a roughly 2.6 percentage-point edge, according to polling aggregator FiveThirtyEight.\par \par \uc2 There is no incumbent on the ballot as the pair vie to fill an open seat vacated by retiring GOP Sen. \uc2 Richard Burr\uc2 , and the race hasn't gotten the same national attention or become the center of controversy as have some states with high-profile candidates on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 "This is the closest race in the country nobody's talking about," said Morgan Jackson, a Democratic strategist in the state.\par \par \uc2 The slice of the state's electorate that is seen as persuadable is getting smaller, a trend that has shown up in many parts of the country, strategists from both parties said. In North Carolina, where the state's voter-registration system allows for voters to be unaffiliated with either party, strategists say even those voters typically lean heavily to one side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 "It just feels like there are fewer and fewer people who are gettable, and more and more people who put their jerseys on at the beginning of the cycle," said Jordan Shaw, a GOP strategist in North Carolina and a former top aide to GOP Sen. \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Both candidates in recent weeks held events in places where they expected to find their strongest supporters. Ms. Beasley rallied voters at a brewery in downtown Asheville, a Democratic stronghold in the western part of the state. Mr. Budd held a rally with \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 ., the former president's son and a celebrity on the right.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jackson, the Democratic strategist, said Ms. Beasley was unlikely to win many rural counties but said she could convince enough Democrats there to show up to eat into Mr. Budd's margins. Mr. Shaw, the GOP strategist, said Mr. Budd had an advantage in that Republicans are motivated to vote this year in a bad national environment for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Many North Carolinians did split their tickets here in 2020, when former President Trump and Gov. \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , a Democrat, both won the state. But nationally, 2020 marked a 20-year low for ticket-splitting, according to Republican pollster Bill McInturff. Some 11% of the electorate were ticket-splitters in 2020 compared with 20% in 2016 and 36% in 2000.\par \par \uc2 Senate Leadership Fund, the super political-action committee that supports Republican Senate candidates, has reserved a total of $30 million in TV ads in the state, according to ad-tracker AdImpact.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority PAC, the super PAC run by allies of Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  (D., N.Y.), recently injected roughly $4 million in TV ad reservations in the state, bringing its total here to about $12 million, according to AdImpact.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina has backed Republicans for president since 2012 and senate since 2014, but voters here twice picked Mr. Cooper, a Democrat, for governor, giving Democrats hope that Ms. Beasley has a path to an upset victory. Some North Carolina Democrats have been calling on Washington officials to send more money as polls showed a close race. Asked whether she needed more money from the national party, Ms. Beasley said: "I would never say no."\par \par \uc2 The candidates in North Carolina say they could persuade some voters outside their camps. Mr. Budd told reporters that the economy was front of mind for voters and that he thought high prices would persuade some Democrats and independents to vote for him.\par \par \uc2 Asked in an interview whether President Biden deserves some blame {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/americans-spend-less-on-gas-more-on-food-as-inflation-varied-across-items-11665768796"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 for higher prices }}}\uc2 , Ms. Beasley declined to say yes or no. "I think really the bottom line is how do we get out of it," she said. An aide said the campaign was advertising on lowering the cost of living, such as capping the cost of insulin.\par \par \uc2 She said some voters who aren't registered Democrats, especially women, would be persuaded to vote for her because of Mr. Budd's opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He supports a bill introduced by Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R., S.C.) that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/lindsey-graham-defends-abortion-proposal-despite-gop-worries-11663524124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 would limit abortions nationwide }}}\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Budd defended his decision to support the bill, which he described as a counterweight to a Democratic bill that would stop states from enacting restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability. "I'd prefer it be in the state capitals," Mr. Budd said. The GOP bill {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/lindsey-grahams-abortion-bill-gets-cold-shoulder-from-some-republicans-11663098886"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hasn't gotten a vote in Congress }}}\uc2 . The Democratic bill {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/house-set-to-vote-on-bills-protecting-abortion-access-11657896570"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed the House }}}\uc2  but hasn't cleared the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Both Democratic and Republican voters in North Carolina said they liked their party's candidates, but they loathed the opponent just as much if not more.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What's your outlook on the Senate race in North Carolina? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 "If she gets in, I think that our situation here in North Carolina would be awful. I really do," Janet Macrae, a homemaker from just outside Greensboro, N.C., said of Ms. Beasley.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Macrae, who attended the \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 . rally for Mr. Budd, said she generally thought Democrats {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/illegal-border-crossings-hit-record-in-may-11655407924"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 were too soft on illegal immigration }}}\uc2 , which was her No. 1 voting issue this fall, above rising prices. "If we're not secure, I don't think inflation makes any difference," she said.\par \par \uc2 Leslie Carey, a mother of four in Hendersonville, N.C., said that she typically votes Democratic and that the Supreme Court's decision this year {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminating the constitutional right to an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  made her want to double down on Democrats. For the first time, the stress-management consultant is knocking on doors to encourage people to vote in her community, which backed Mr. Biden in 2020 and is surrounded by rural neighborhoods that backed Mr. Trump, according to a WSJ analysis of Census Bureau data.\par \par \uc2 "I thought [\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] was just used to raise money. I didn't really believe that they would take that away," Ms. Carey said. "Because I was feeling so helpless, the only way I sought to control the situation was to go and talk to people on my side."\par \par \uc2 Natalie Andrews contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Joshua Jamerson at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:joshua.jamerson+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joshua.jamerson+1@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/north-carolina-senate-race-spotlights-shrinking-slice-of-persuadable-voters-11666603802"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 North Carolina Senate Race Spotlights Shrinking Slice of Persuadable Voters  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221024eiao00109\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
