\par \par \uc2 Michigan Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , center, has fought to preserve access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Polls have shown Ms. Whitmer is leading her opponent in the final stretch of the campaign. PHOTO: Emily Elconin for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Welcome to a special edition of WSJ's politics newsletter looking at how {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-pushes-some-suburban-women-in-west-michigan-toward-democrats-11666085403"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  is a factor }}}\uc2  in the midterm elections. To receive our weekday edition and future special editions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/newsletters?sub=230&mod=djemCapitalJournalDaybreak"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sign up here }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Three Questions for WSJ's Julie Bykowicz\par \par \uc2 WSJ: How are voters reacting to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrats-move-abortion-fight-to-midterm-elections-after-supreme-court-ruling-11656098522"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court's Dobbs decision }}}\uc2  in June, which ended the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and returned the issue to the states?\par \par \uc2 Julie Bykowicz: There is a sense among political strategists and voters alike that if Democrats do well in the midterms\u8212\'20\'14when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/senate-midterm-map-shaped-by-abortion-and-the-economy-11663247187"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inflation and other factors }}}\uc2  favor Republicans\u8212\'20\'14it will be because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Nationally, voter registrations have been more Democratic, more female and younger than they were before the Dobbs decision in June.\par \par \uc2 I recently spent time in Michigan, where voters are paying extra attention to the issue because in November they will decide whether to codify in state law the right to end a pregnancy. We saw earlier this year when 60% of voters in Republican-leaning Kansas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-abortion-vote-results-11659440554"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejected a measure }}}\uc2  that would have outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that this is a motivating issue.\par \par \uc2 In the suburbs of Grand Rapids, where Democratic Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  grew up, women told me that they were paying closer attention to races up and down the ballot post-Dobbs. One woman I spoke with, Erin Berkeley Weber, said she will vote for Ms. Whitmer\u8212\'20\'14even though she didn't vote for her last time and is generally a Republican\u8212\'20\'14because of the work the governor has done to protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WSJ: How are Republicans responding to suburban women who say they are more motivated to vote for Democrats since the high court's ruling?\par \par \uc2 Julie Bykowicz: Republicans broadly recognize that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could drive up Democratic votes. In quite a few cases, GOP candidates have adjusted how they talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Kansas ban failed. We're seeing them play down the issue and instead focus almost exclusively on the weak economic conditions, a topic that polls have shown benefits Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Tudor Dixon, Ms. Whitmer's Republican opponent, said in a television interview a few months ago that even a teenager raped by a relative shouldn't seek to end her pregnancy. In a recent debate, she said that while she personally opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she would respect what Michigan voters decide on the ballot question about it. That's a significant change in what she is choosing to emphasize about her stance.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: What legislative action on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  might we expect after the election?\par \par \uc2 Julie Bykowicz: President Biden began leaning much harder into \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as an electoral issue recently. He said at a Democratic Party event on Tuesday that his administration would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/midterms-elections-voting-2022-10-18/card/biden-to-push-abortion-rights-bill-if-democrats-keep-control-of-congress-xBC4jF4vDlnbWBNZLvkF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fast-track a bill }}}\uc2  to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in federal law if Democrats keep control of both chambers of Congress. Even aside from the election challenges of keeping control of both chambers, there is an added hurdle of getting such a measure through a closely divided Senate where 60 votes would be needed to overcome a filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R., S.C.) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/lindsey-grahams-abortion-bill-gets-cold-shoulder-from-some-republicans-11663098886"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 introduced a bill }}}\uc2  in September that would ban doctors from performing abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, though some antiabortion Republicans gave the proposal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/lindsey-grahams-abortion-bill-gets-cold-shoulder-from-some-republicans-11663098886"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a chilly reception, }}}\uc2  saying it had no chance of passage and simply served to motivate Democratic voters.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of what happens in the congressional elections, expect to see states continue to address the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue through {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ballot measures and in state legislatures }}}\uc2 . In addition to the Michigan ballot question, voters in California, Kentucky, Montana and Vermont will weigh in on reproductive policies this fall. There is likely to be more along those lines in future elections.\par \par \uc2 Julie Bykowicz is a reporter in The Wall Street Journal's Washington bureau. Follow her on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/bykowicz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @bykowicz }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/news/author/julie-bykowicz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 read her articles }}}\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Wall Street Journal reporters are traveling across the U.S. to report on the neighborhoods and issues that will help decide the November elections. Read other articles in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/midterm-elections-the-neighborhoods-reshaping-political-power-across-america-11655152764"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 this series here }}}\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 In the final stretch of midterm campaigning, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at the center of competing pushes by the two major parties. Since the Dobbs decision, Democrats running for Congress or governor have run more ads that are focused on or mention \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than on any other topic, according to an analysis by The Wall Street Journal of data from ad-tracking firm AdImpact. During that same period, the top issues in Republican ads have been taxes, inflation and Mr. Biden, respectively, the analysis shows.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in areas such as the western part of Michigan, where it was once seen as acceptable or advantageous to oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all circumstances, have changed how they talk about the issue because voters know that, without Roe, those stances have a stronger shot at becoming policy.\par \par \uc2 Michigan political strategists see west Michigan, as it is often referred to, as critical in November. In the past four presidential elections, voters in Kent County alternated between the Democratic and Republican candidates, choosing Mr. Biden in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Polls have shown Ms. Whitmer is leading Ms. Dixon in the final stretch of the campaign. A CBS News/\uc2 YouGov\uc2  survey taken in early October showed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/wisconsin-senate-ron-johnson-mandela-barnes-opinion-poll-2022-10-09/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 economy and inflation }}}\uc2  are the top issues for Michigan voters, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  came in third, with 83% of respondents calling it very or somewhat important. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
