\par\par \uc2 Midterm Election 2022\par \uc2 Politics\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pushes Some Suburban Women in West Michigan Toward Democrats; The end of Roe v. Wade has moved  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  policy from the courts to the hands of voters\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julie Bykowicz | Photographs by Emily Elconin for The Wall Street Journal \par \uc2 1,834 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par 09:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ADA, Mich.\u8212\'20\'14Erin Berkeley Weber served on the student council with \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  when they attended high school in this Grand Rapids suburb. A Republican who favors low taxes and school choice, Ms. Weber voted for her former classmate's GOP opponent in the governor's race four years ago.\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Weber, 51, has known since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  that she will vote to re-elect the Democratic governor this fall. She said she likes how Ms. Whitmer {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/in-michigan-governor-race-legal-missteps-hurt-gop-bid-to-unseat-gretchen-whitmer-11659087002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has fought to preserve access to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , including by waging court battles and backing a ballot initiative to codify in state law a woman's right to end her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 "This is the one thing that gets me," said Ms. Weber, a two-time \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  voter who has raised two sons in the nearby suburb of Rockford. "It breaks my heart that we've jumped back in time as a country."\par \par \uc2 This year's elections mark {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/story/most-suburban-women-support-abortion-rights-but-will-it-move-their-votes-0a93b901"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a test of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  as an electoral issue }}}\uc2  now that voters have new motivations. The Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision in June ended the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 handing the issue to voters }}}\uc2  who will decide on the governors, state lawmakers and ballot initiatives that shape the legality and availability of the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/money-pours-into-senate-races-as-midterm-campaign-enters-home-stretch-11665928130"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the final stretch of midterm campaigning }}}\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at the center of competing pushes by the two major parties. Democrats are centering their advertising on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because polls show they have an advantage with voters on the issue. That has prompted some Republicans in recent weeks to play down and in some cases backpedal on their support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in an effort to minimize the issue in the run-up to Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are instead focusing on the economy, which polling shows is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/midterm-elections-2022-how-economic-concerns-are-shaping-the-vote-11665250300"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a bigger concern for voters }}}\uc2 . They say {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/us-inflation-september-2022-consumer-price-index-11665628037"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rising prices }}}\uc2  and President Biden's low approval rating will continue to supersede \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and drive voters to their side.\par \par \uc2 Since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrats-move-abortion-fight-to-midterm-elections-after-supreme-court-ruling-11656098522"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Dobbs decision }}}\uc2 , Democrats running for Congress or governor have run more ads that are focused on or mention \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than on any other topic, according to an analysis by The Wall Street Journal of data from the ad-tracking firm AdImpact. During that same period, the top issues in Republican ads have been taxes, inflation and Mr. Biden, respectively, the analysis shows.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in areas such as the western part of Michigan, where it was once seen as acceptable or advantageous to oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all circumstances, have changed how they talk about the issue because voters know that, without Roe, those stances have a stronger shot at becoming policy.\par \par \uc2 Tudor Dixon, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/primary-election-results-august-2-az-ks-mo-mi-wa/card/tudor-dixon-wins-gop-nomination-for-governor-setting-up-battle-with-democrat-gretchen-whitmer-UweBl3tsWsGouSatdDPN"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ms. Whitmer's GOP opponent }}}\uc2 , said in a debate this month that while she opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , except in cases where the mother's health is at risk, she will respect what the state's voters decide on the issue. Just a few months ago, Ms. Dixon said in an interview with a local television station, "A life is a life for me, that's how it is," and that even in a theoretical case where a 14-year-old is raped by a family member, she should not seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Polls have shown Ms. Whitmer is leading Ms. Dixon in the final stretch of the campaign. A CBS News/\uc2 YouGov\uc2  survey taken in early October showed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/wisconsin-senate-ron-johnson-mandela-barnes-opinion-poll-2022-10-09/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the economy and inflation are the top issues }}}\uc2  for Michigan voters, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  came in third, with 83% of respondents calling it very or somewhat important.\par \par \uc2 Both parties say that appealing to suburban women, a key swing group, will be critical to winning races across the country this fall. Women are more likely to vote for Democrats, and pollsters and political strategists say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is an issue that could push independent and some Republican suburban women toward the party. Polling has consistently shown that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/support-for-legalized-abortion-grows-since-dobbs-ruling-wsj-poll-shows-11662210020"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most Americans support legal access to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 The effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be even stronger in Michigan, where the November ballot includes a question for voters about whether to provide a state constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . California, Kentucky, Montana and Vermont also are asking voters to weigh in on reproductive policies.\par \par \uc2 The makeup of newly registered voters nationwide\u8212\'20\'14including in Michigan\u8212\'20\'14has been more Democratic, more female and younger since the Dobbs decision, according to an analysis by the Democratic firm TargetSmart. Dobbs also has created voters who are singularly focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and will act as a counterweight to the antiabortion voters who have long helped Republicans win.\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion advocates say their supporters are reliable and will show up in November. "We know this is a watershed year," said Laura Alexandria, president of Grand Rapids Right to Life. "I think our pro-life people are always motivated voters."\par \par \uc2 The effects could flow beyond the governor's race. Hillary Scholten is viewed as one of the Democrats' best hopes for flipping a congressional seat after Republican Rep. Peter Meijer {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/primary-election-results-august-2-az-ks-mo-mi-wa/card/rep-peter-meijer-who-voted-to-impeach-trump-loses-to-john-gibbs-in-michigan-gop-primary-f7h3KSo234FEBI7YeYPP"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lost his primary }}}\uc2  to John Gibbs, who was endorsed by former President Trump. Democrats have a chance to lead at least one chamber of the state legislature that Republicans have controlled for 12 years.\par \par \uc2 Michigan political strategists see west Michigan, as it is often referred to, as critical in November. In the past four presidential elections, voters here in Kent County alternated between the Democratic and Republican candidates, choosing Mr. Biden in 2020.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, urban amenities have attracted more college graduates and Democratic voters to the Grand Rapids suburbs. Gerald R. Ford Republicans that aligned with the nation's 38th president, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB116769005038064229"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who was raised in Grand Rapids }}}\uc2 , have faded in influence as more Trump-style Republican candidates have emerged.\par \par \uc2 "People here feel alienated from the GOP," said state Rep. Rachel Hood, a Democrat who first won in 2018 and is running against Republican Lynn Afendoulis in the district that includes Ada. "A sensible approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  post-Dobbs gives us one more way to reach them, especially suburban women."\par \par \uc2 Ada is an influential place in the life of Ms. Whitmer, who attended Forest Hills Central High School here in the late 1980s. The mostly white, upper-middle-class suburb is home to the multilevel marketer \uc2 Amway Corp\uc2 . and members of the conservative DeVos family that founded it. The wealthy family has backed Ms. Dixon's candidacy.\par \par \uc2 The area has a conservative history in part because the Christian Reformed Church in North America, which has worked to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has deep roots here. It is now more politically split. Voters living in the core of Ada picked Mr. Biden by 8 points in 2020, while those in its more rural outskirts went for Mr. Trump by 14 points.\par \par \uc2 Kelly Cool, a 55-year-old Ada resident and mother of five who is antiabortion, said the increased attention on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this fall is a good thing regardless of its political implications.\par \par \uc2 "We've gone 50 years with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  being legal, and what has changed?" asked Ms. Cool. "I want us as a society to get to a point where people won't do it not because it's illegal, but because it's a morally abhorrent act to take a human life."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cool's 96-year-old father started one of Michigan's first Right to Life chapters. She considers a candidate's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the most important factor in deciding her vote, and said she would vote for Ms. Dixon and against the ballot initiative.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Whitmer barely mentioned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in her 2018 campaign. In an interview, she pointed to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ruth-bader-ginsburg-dies-11600472623"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the September 2020 death }}}\uc2  of liberal Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  as the moment that showed Americans who support the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that they might lose it.\par \par \uc2 "The Dobbs decision was so jarring and so scary that we're seeing a lot more conversation and energy around this issue going into this election," Ms. Whitmer said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dixon, a 45-year-old conservative commentator new to politics, said in a statement that Michigan voters tell her they care far less about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than they do about the increase in violent crime, their children's learning loss during the pandemic and what they see as overregulation that is driving away businesses. She declined an interview request.\par \par \uc2 Melissa Vonk, 51, grew up down the road from the Whitmers in nearby Cascade and is now raising her own five children in Ada. She said her 19-year-old, Molly, asked to register to vote this summer so that she would be eligible to sign the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights ballot initiative.\par \par \uc2 "This issue has awakened something," said Ms. Vonk, who also has a 21-year-old daughter and three teenage sons. "The way my girls have been\u8212\'20\'14the fire in their bellies\u8212\'20\'14has made me see just how important it is not to take this right for granted."\par \par \uc2 Soon after the Dobbs decision, friends Debbie Lown and Michelle Mischley spent one of their weekly neighborhood walks in Ada talking about their divergent views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Divisions over the issue had already imploded their book club.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lown, 61, believes it is a women's health issue that shouldn't be restricted by politicians. Ms. Mischley, 53, wants the procedure outlawed except in situations where the mother's life is at risk. She sees it as a human-rights issue for unborn children.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lown, a mother of two adult children and a retired public health professor, has been knocking on doors to help pass the ballot initiative and re-elect Ms. Whitmer, and is getting involved in down-ballot races for the first time.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mischley voted for Mr. Trump in 2016, Mr. Biden in 2020 and for Ms. Whitmer four years ago. "As long as Democrats don't make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  their main thing, I am comfortable voting for them," said Ms. Mischley, whose three adult children include an adopted son, one reason she said she so strongly opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She said she won't vote to re-elect Ms. Whitmer because the incumbent has made the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate so central to her campaign.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How is the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  influencing your motivation to vote? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Weber, too, has been talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  far more with her friends and family. Her most interesting conversations, she said, have been with her mother, a former healthcare worker who still lives in the house in Ada where Ms. Weber grew up.\par \par \uc2 "She said my generation has never known what it's like not to have this right," Ms. Weber said.\par \par \uc2 Chad Day contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Julie Bykowicz at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:julie.bykowicz@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 julie.bykowicz@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-pushes-some-suburban-women-in-west-michigan-toward-democrats-11666085403"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Pushes Some Suburban Women in West Michigan Toward Democrats }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221018eiai000p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
