\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Doctors Struggle With Navigating  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans in Medical Emergencies; Healthcare providers are concerned they could face charges for providing emergency abortions in some states\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 1,245 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2022\par 18:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Leilah Zahedi-Spung, a high-risk obstetrician in Chattanooga, Tenn., recently saw a pregnant patient with rising blood pressure who the doctor believed could be facing a serious health emergency.\par \par \uc2 The patient was in her second trimester of pregnancy and her unborn baby had been diagnosed with genetic abnormalities that meant the child wasn't expected to survive. Dr. Zahedi-Spung feared the mother was at risk of a severe form of preeclampsia that can cause seizures and ultimately death. The doctor said she thought the patient needed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tennessee has a total ban }}}\uc2  on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The state law, in effect since late August, allows doctors to argue the procedure was necessary to save the life of the patient, but Dr. Zahedi-Spung said she wasn't confident the woman's condition was dire enough to meet that standard and feared that being charged with a crime could upend her life for years, even if she eventually was vindicated. She decided to send the woman on a roughly six-hour ambulance ride to end her pregnancy in North Carolina, where she arrived with dangerously high blood pressure and signs of kidney failure, the doctor said.\par \par \uc2 "She kept asking if she was going to die," Dr. Zahedi-Spung said. "I kept saying, 'I'm trying, I'm trying, we're going to make it happen. We just need to get you to the right place where you can be taken care of.'" When she saw the patient a couple of weeks later, alive, the doctor said she felt a wave of relief.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  in June, ending constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , laws banning many or most abortions have taken effect in over a dozen states. All of these laws make some sort of allowance for doctors to end a pregnancy to save the life of the mother, a core tenet of the mainstream antiabortion movement. But some doctors say that in practice these exemptions are so narrow and the penalties for making the wrong decision so harsh that they have to choose between putting patient lives at risk or putting themselves in legal jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 Tennessee has been at the center of this debate because {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/states-prepare-to-quickly-implement-abortion-trigger-laws-11655385028"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its law is one of the strictest }}}\uc2 , making it a crime to perform any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , punishable by up to 15 years in prison. The law provides doctors with an "affirmative defense," allowing them to argue in court that the procedure was necessary to save the life of the mother or prevent her from serious physical injury.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What do you think is next in the debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 More than 700 medical professionals, including obstetricians and emergency room doctors, published a letter to the Tennessee General Assembly this week asking it to revise the state's law, saying it "criminalizes physicians for providing lifesaving care."\par \par \uc2 Randy McNally, the speaker of the state's Republican-led Senate, said Tennessee's law does provide a safety valve for doctors.\par \par \uc2 "Those driving the narrative that the law does not contain a true exception or that medically necessary care is outlawed are opposed to the law in its totality," Mr. McNally said. "If these imagined 'flaws' in our law were 'fixed,' they would continue to pick apart the law until there was nothing left."\par \par \uc2 Idaho and North Dakota also have laws that criminalize all abortions and provide doctors with defenses they can use in court. Ohio lawmakers are set to debate a similar law in the coming weeks. In other states, doctors can't be charged with a crime for ending a pregnancy as long as it was to save the life of the mother, although some providers say it can still be hard to know how dire a situation must be to qualify.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/idaho-must-allow-abortions-in-certain-emergencies-judge-rules-11661386919"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has challenged Idaho's ban }}}\uc2  in court, arguing it violates a federal law requiring doctors to provide abortions in certain medical emergencies. A federal judge in late August temporarily blocked enforcement of the law in these types of situations, writing that the law's affirmative defense offers "cold comfort" to physicians because "it can only be raised after the physician has already faced indictment, arrest, pretrial detention, and trial for every \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  they perform."\par \par \uc2 North Dakota's law has also been temporarily blocked by a state court judge.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents say doctors and hospitals are being overly conservative in the way they interpret these laws, which all have specific carve-outs allowing physicians to provide lifesaving care. Christina Francis, Chief Executive Officer-elect of the American Association of Pro-Life Obstetricians and Gynecologists, said doctors have years of training that empower them to determine when a patient's situation has become life-threatening.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of the confusion I'm seeing is stemming from hospital administrators, hospital lawyers, not understanding the bill or being too cautious," Dr. Francis said.\par \par \uc2 When Tennessee's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/graphics/state-of-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 initially passed in 2019 }}}\uc2 , it drew fairly little opposition from the medical community, said Ashley Coffield, president and chief executive of Planned Parenthood of Tennessee and North Mississippi. At the time, the law, which would only go into effect if Roe was overturned, had few real-world consequences.\par \par \uc2 Chloe Akers, a criminal defense attorney based in Knoxville, Tenn., read the law after Roe fell and was surprised to see it contained no exceptions, only defenses that doctors could use after the fact. She founded a nonprofit Standing Together Tennessee and began giving seminars to doctors and others about the law.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Akers tells healthcare providers there are ways to manage risk, such as keeping robust records of their decision making. But if a doctor asks how to take that risk to zero, she answers, "You stop providing obstetric care in the state."\par \par \uc2 Paul Linton\uc2 , a Chicago-area attorney who helped lawmakers in several states including Tennessee draft their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, said no prosecutor in the real world would charge a doctor with a crime for acting in good faith in a dire medical situation.\par \par \uc2 "As time goes on and we see that no one is being prosecuted for doing abortions, I think this fear becomes very unsubstantiated," Mr. Linton said.\par \par \uc2 Some doctors say the law has changed how they provide obstetric care. David Soll, an obstetrician in Jackson, Tenn., said in the past when he suspected a patient had an ectopic pregnancy, which develops outside of the uterus, he would give her a drug to stop the pregnancy from growing and try to avoid a more invasive surgical procedure. But now, Dr. Soll said, on the advice of attorneys, he will have to perform surgery to confirm the pregnancy is ectopic, with all the additional risks that entails.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Zahedi-Spung said she has decided the risks of practicing in Tennessee are too high and plans to relocate, recently accepting a job offer in Denver, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal throughout pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 "The idea I've heard from a lot of people is that physicians should just be breaking the law," she said. But doing so means risking "my freedom and liberty and the life that I have worked very hard to have."\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:laura.kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laura.kusisto@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/doctors-struggle-with-navigating-abortion-bans-in-medical-emergencies-11665684225"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Doctors Struggle With Navigating \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Bans in Medical Emergencies }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221013eiad00565\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
