\par \par \uc2 In states that have recently restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some medical-residency programs are making plans for their doctors to receive training elsewhere. PHOTO: Taylor Glascock for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 New \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions are changing where future obstetrician-gynecologists are trained.\par \par \uc2 Residency programs in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/arizona-can-enforce-near-total-abortion-ban-blocked-for-50-years-judge-rules-11663976573"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 states that have restricted \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  since the Supreme Court removed constitutional protection for the procedure said they are making contingency plans for their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/fall-of-roe-v-wade-upends-abortion-landscape-for-american-women-11656081225"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 doctors to train in other states }}}\uc2 . Some medical students said they are limiting their searches to residency programs in states that allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And programs in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-providers-in-illinois-colorado-and-florida-prepare-for-patient-influx-if-roe-is-overturned-11655560800"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 states that offer clinical experience conducting abortions }}}\uc2  said they expect a surge in demand that far exceeds the spots available.\par \par \uc2 Maria Valle Coto, a fourth-year medical student at the University of Illinois College of Medicine at Chicago, said she is using a color-coded map of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws by state to decide where to apply for training. A dean for career advising told her she might need to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/some-doctors-rethink-careers-after-states-restrict-abortions-11657791001"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 consider another specialty }}}\uc2  because competition for OB-GYN slots is so high in states that allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she said.\par \par \uc2 "I've already gone so far into this career that it would be disheartening to do a different specialty," she said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education\uc2 , which certifies physician-training programs, requires that OB-GYN programs provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training for residents who don't opt out. The council said in September that programs in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 states where \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  is restricted }}}\uc2  must help residents get training in another state.\par \par \uc2 "This is a core procedure that has to be learned and experienced by residents," said Dr. John Combes, the council's public-policy officer. He said the council would work with programs that can't meet the requirement.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, where abortions were halted after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-the-supreme-court-overturned-roe-v-wade-and-abortion-rights-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Supreme Court's June decision }}}\uc2 , the UW School of Medicine and Public Health is exploring partnerships with medical schools and clinics outside the state to meet the training requirements, said Dr. Ellen Hartenbach, chair of the department of obstetrics and gynecology.\par \par \uc2 "We're concerned about how it may impact the training program. And we're concerned about how it will impact the decision of our residency graduates to stay in practice in Wisconsin," she said of changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law and practice.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What's the future for access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the U.S.? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Lynne Johnson, executive director of Midwest Access Project, which works with 11 programs that provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training to residents and other medical providers, said that before the Supreme Court decision the group accepted about half of applicants. This year far more people than usual have applied, she said.\par \par \uc2 "We had to end our application cycle early because we couldn't handle the number we were getting," she said.\par \par \uc2 Residency programs that use state funding in places that have restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are navigating financial and legal constraints on where and how their doctors conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training, said Stephanie Toti, senior counsel and project director at the Lawyering Project, a group that advocates for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Jessica Mecklosky, a fourth-year student at Tulane University School of Medicine, in Louisiana, said she was inspired to apply to pediatric residency programs after treating children and teens with pregnancies, many of whom have been sexually abused. She said she had planned to train in the South but now is focused on states including Massachusetts and Pennsylvania that wouldn't restrict her ability to counsel patients on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't feel like a competent physician," she said.\par \par \uc2 Some OB-GYNs who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  said residents get enough exposure to the procedure away from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. The procedure is used to remove fetal tissue after a miscarriage or when a woman's life is threatened from hemorrhage or infection, they said.\par \par \uc2 "The only thing specific \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training gives is how to end a viable pregnancy," said Christina Francis, an OB-GYN in Fort Wayne, Ind., who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 David Eisenberg, an OB-GYN and professor at Washington University School of Medicine in St. Louis, said residents need \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training to learn how to evacuate a uterus under any circumstance.\par \par \uc2 "When a pregnancy goes sideways, you want your health provider to not hesitate," he said. "It should be as second-nature as putting on your scrubs."\par \par \uc2 He said he is working on ways for his students to get the training outside of Missouri, which banned abortions in June except to save a pregnant person's life.\par \par \uc2 Washington University said it would find a way to meet accreditation requirements without breaking the law.\par \par \uc2 "There is a sorting happening in this country," Dr. Eisenberg said.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/state-abortion-law-changes-upend-medical-training-programs-11665020346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 State \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22 -Law Changes Upend Medical Training Programs }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020221006eia60012y\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
