\par\par \uc2 Midterm Election 2022\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Democrats' Political Gains Create Tight Race for Nebraska GOP Rep. \uc2 Don Bacon\uc2 ;  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  ruling and Capitol riot by Trump supporters have nudged some voters in Democrats' direction\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dante Chinni | Photographs by Rebecca S. Gratz for The Wall Street Journal \par \uc2 1,386 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2022\par 12:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OMAHA, Neb.\u8212\'20\'14If Republican Rep. \uc2 Don Bacon\uc2  gets re-elected in November, it will be in part because his efforts to center his competitive House race on inflation, crime and border security helped win over undecided voters like Todd Christensen.\par \par \uc2 If Mr. Bacon loses, it will be partly because voters in this swing district moved toward Democrats after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the overturning of Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  and the congressional hearings on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/as-rioters-again-dispute-trumps-defeat-d-c-police-make-arrests-11609945368"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Jan. 6, 2021, Capitol riot }}}\uc2  by supporters of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Mr. Christensen, who voted for Mr. Bacon in his previous three runs for Congress, said he is unsure about doing so again because he thought Mr. Bacon came across as too divisive when he heard him address constituents at town hall meetings.\par \par \uc2 "Historically, Republicans do a better job of appealing to my beliefs than Democrats do," said Mr. Christensen, a 37-year-old financial analyst who twice voted for Mr. Trump. He is considering giving Mr. Bacon's Democratic opponent, state Sen. Tony Vargas, "a shot" because he seems like "a pretty good dude."\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What issues do you think will prove to be most important in the midterms? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bacon's race is part of a batch of contests where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democratic-midterm-prospects-improve-as-races-heat-up-wsj-poll-finds-11662024601"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats' chances have improved }}}\uc2 . Republicans are still favored to take control of the House, though by a slimmer margin than predicted earlier this year. The outcome hinges on the final decisions of voters in districts like this one, which includes the densely populated core of the city, its wealthy suburbs and the exurban sprawl and rural towns that stretch beyond the metro.\par \par \uc2 Nebraska is generally thought of as a Republican bastion, though the size and sway of Omaha, an urban blue dot on the state's eastern border, has made the Second Congressional District into a battleground. Mr. Bacon won his 2020 race by about 5 percentage points. President Biden beat Mr. Trump here by about 7 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 The biggest chunk of the district's population comes from Douglas County, which is home to colleges and upscale educated neighborhoods, such as Aksarben (Nebraska spelled backward). Full of cafes, bars and restaurants as well as a large food hall, the area is young, trendy and deeply Democratic.\par \par \uc2 The key for Republicans is driving up support and turnout in the exurban and rural areas of the district. There are signs they face challenges with some of those voters.\par \par \uc2 Bruce Meyer, 70, a retired accountant living in this city's western exurbs, was a lifelong Republican until the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade turned him into an independent voter, he said. His daughter, an OB-GYN in the area, told him she may end up leaving the state because she worries that the loss of protections from Roe could eventually impede her ability to work as a fertility specialist.\par \par \uc2 The issue is one reason why Mr. Meyer said he won't vote to re-elect Mr. Bacon despite backing him in his previous three runs. Mr. Bacon has said he opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except to save a mother's life and has co-sponsored a bill that says life begins at conception. He recently said he would accept a compromise that permits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 "The politicians don't look at how the issues in their proposals will impact people," Mr. Meyer said.\par \par \uc2 David Wasserman, senior editor for the Cook Political Report, said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is moving voters "across the board," and not just in the suburban areas that will help decide close races. He is one of several election forecasters who have moved Mr. Bacon's race from "likely Republican" to "tossup."\par \par \uc2 A central challenge for Mr. Bacon is appealing to voters who like Mr. Trump's combative style without alienating voters who dislike it. Mr. Bacon has at times distanced himself from the former president, who called for challenging Mr. Bacon in his primary, and has said he won't support Mr. Trump for the GOP nomination if he seeks the White House again. However, Mr. Bacon didn't vote to impeach the former president for the Jan. 6 riot.\par \par \uc2 Jason Birnstihl, 47, an Omaha real-estate agent, said he plans to vote for Mr. Bacon and described him as "a stand-up guy with a lot of respect from people around here." Yet he said he and others have concerns that Mr. Bacon should be "more aggressive in calling out government overreach."\par \par \uc2 Chad Woodworth, a lifelong Republican who lives in nearby Sarpy County, said the GOP's reaction to the Capitol riot was a deal breaker after voting for Mr. Bacon in his campaigns and for Mr. Trump twice.\par \par \uc2 "That second impeachment, that's where I really got frustrated with a lot of the Republican Party for not voting to impeach him," said Mr. Woodworth. "I sent Bacon a letter saying that you can never have my vote again."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Woodworth, 47, who was a mechanic and is now a community college instructor, said that his family has deep roots in the military, and that he doesn't recognize the current Republican Party, which he said he feels has "betrayed" him.\par \par \uc2 "When did Colin Powell become a bad guy or George Bush or \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2 ?," Mr. Woodworth asked. "They're against [Trump]. They're against this way of acting."\par \par \uc2 Neither Mr. Meyer nor Mr. Woodworth said they are excited about voting for Mr. Vargas, the Democratic nominee, though they said they feel they have no choice. Mr. Meyer said he especially wasn't happy with the Democratic leadership in Congress, though he isn't afraid to vote for a Democrat from Nebraska because "those people are rational."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bacon said he thinks the political environment this year is better than in previous ones, and that he has always treated his races as tossups. He said Mr. Vargas is portraying himself as a moderate when his record is much more liberal, and that research suggested that about half of voters had no opinion of Mr. Vargas.\par \par \uc2 "What the voters end up deciding about Tony Vargas is largely how the race is going to go," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bacon voted with Mr. Trump 92% of the time and liked "the policies, not the temperament," said Derek Oden, Mr. Bacon's campaign manager. He acknowledges that Mr. Trump's continued place in the spotlight has become a challenge.\par \par \uc2 "We want to be talking about the issues, about inflation, crime and the border," said Mr. Oden. "When the former president is out there, you make this a personality competition and the issues get pushed aside."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vargas represents a departure from the opposition Mr. Bacon has faced in the last two races, when his opponent was community organizer Kara Eastman.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vargas, who is Latino, in the Legislature represents South Omaha, where much of the city's Hispanic population lives, giving him the potential to generate more enthusiasm from a part of the city that normally has lower turnout. He emphasizes how he has worked with members from both sides of the aisle, and doesn't cite \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or Jan. 6 as dominant issues.\par \par \uc2 "Voters say they're looking for people that are actually going to fight for the middle class and the working class," he said, noting that his parents worked on factory lines.\par \par \uc2 Omaha is home to a branch of the University of Nebraska as well as \uc2 Creighton University\uc2  and the University of Nebraska Medical Center, among other schools. More than 27,000 students attend the schools and many live here after graduation, adding to the pool of young, college-educated voters.\par \par \uc2 Since 2000, the percentage of adults 25 or older with a college degree has grown by about 10 percentage points in Douglas County to more than 40%. That growth is faster than the national average, and the current college-educated share is more than seven points higher than the national figure.\par \par \uc2 Write to Dante Chinni at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Dante.Chinni@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dante.Chinni@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrats-political-gains-create-tight-race-for-nebraska-gop-rep-don-bacon-11664452803"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats' Political Gains Create Tight Race for Nebraska GOP Rep. Don Bacon }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220929ei9t0028m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
