\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Arizona Judge Allows Near-Total  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban Blocked for 50 Years; Ruling ends months of confusion over  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  access in state after \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto and Jennifer Calfas \par \uc2 1,021 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2022\par 13:54 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 An Arizona state court judge lifted a 50-year-old block on a 19th-century law banning nearly all abortions in the state Friday, ending three months of confusion about the legality of the procedure in the state.\par \par \uc2 Pima County Superior Court Judge Kellie Johnson said in her ruling that a previous decision blocking enforcement of the state's pre-Roe v. Wade ban must now be vacated since the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court overturned Roe  }}}\uc2 , the 1973 decision that created a federal constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Arizona Attorney General \uc2 Mark Brnovich\uc2  , a Republican who filed a motion in mid-July asking a court to lift the injunction on the law, shared a copy of the judge's decision.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Brnovich said he applauded the court's decision. "I have and will continue to protect the most vulnerable Arizonans," he said in a statement Friday.\par \par \uc2 The future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Arizona has been contested since late June, when the Supreme Court's ruling in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization turned the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 's legality over to states. Republicans in the state have been divided over whether the older law banning nearly all abortions or a newer one banning the procedure after 15 weeks of pregnancy should take precedence.\par \par \uc2 Clinics in the state, including Planned Parenthood Arizona, stopped providing abortions because of uncertainty about the law shortly after the high court's decision. Some of those clinics have since reopened to patients. Planned Parenthood Arizona restarted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services at its clinic in Tucson at the end of August.\par \par \uc2 "No archaic law should dictate our reproductive freedom and how we live our lives today," said Brittany Fonteno, president of Planned Parenthood Arizona, in a statement Friday. "This is not the end of the fight; this harmful ban has no place in Arizona and we will persist until that is achieved."\par \par \uc2 The near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban traces back to an 1864 territorial rule\u8212\'20\'14before Arizona was a state. Mr. Brnovich has said the injunction on the law was based solely on the protections formerly provided by Roe. The law bans nearly all abortions in the state except in cases where it is necessary to save the life of the woman. The statute states \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers could face between two to five years in prison for violating the law.\par \par \uc2 The state's 15-week ban, passed by the legislature this spring, is set to take effect Saturday. Some Republican lawmakers have said that their intent was always to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as widely as the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  allowed. The law, however, specifically states the legislation doesn't repeal the state's pre-Roe ban.\par \par \uc2 Since the high court's Dobbs decision, about a dozen states have banned many or most abortions. Meanwhile, the situation in about a half-dozen other states, including Arizona, has remained in flux, and providers have struggled to know whether to keep their doors open or closed. Most recently, an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/indiana-near-total-abortion-ban-temporarily-blocked-11663861798"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Indiana judge temporarily blocked  }}}\uc2  the state's near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban one week after it took effect.\par \par \uc2 Leaders at Planned Parenthood Arizona said the judge's decision Friday failed to clarify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws written and passed in more recent decades, causing more confusion.\par \par \uc2 Judge Johnson, who was appointed by Republican Gov. \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  in 2017, said that wasn't the court's role.\par \par \uc2 "While there may be legal questions the parties seek to resolve regarding Arizona statutes on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , those questions are not for this Court to decide here," Judge Johnson wrote in her decision.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood Center of Tucson, 10 physicians and an anonymous pregnant woman who wished to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  filed the original case in 1971, arguing the 19th-century \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban violated the state and federal constitutions. The state's court of appeals initially ruled against the plaintiffs on Jan. 3, 1973, upholding the law. It reversed itself less than a month later, after the Supreme Court issued its decision in Roe.\par \par \uc2 The attorney general's request to lift a half-century-old injunction was an unusual one, prompted by a Supreme Court ruling that upended some five decades of precedent, and it raised some unique challenges. Seven of the doctors who were among the original plaintiffs are now dead and the remaining three no longer have active medical licenses. Ms. Roe is no longer a party to the case.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, Beau Roysden, a lawyer for the attorney general's office, argued in a hearing last month that the age of the injunction was irrelevant and what mattered was that the court's original reason for granting it was no longer valid.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood Arizona argued the court should modify the injunction and not do away with it entirely, harmonizing the 19th-century law with the regime of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws that have been passed in the five decades since the law was blocked. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  argued that licensed physicians should be governed by the more recent law and be allowed to perform abortions up to 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Questions over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws predating Roe v. Wade have surfaced in other states including Michigan and Wisconsin. A Michigan judge earlier this month blocked enforcement of the state's 1931 ban on the procedure, saying it violated the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, confusion among \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers persists over whether they can be prosecuted under the state's 1849 law. This past week Gov. \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  , a Democrat, called for a special session of the state legislature next month to pass a measure that would allow voters to determine whether to repeal the law.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin Republican legislative leaders\u8212\'20\'14who control both chambers\u8212\'20\'14criticized Mr. Evers's call to action, calling it a "desperate political stunt."\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jennifer.calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jennifer.calfas@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jennifer Calfas at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/arizona-can-enforce-near-total-abortion-ban-blocked-for-50-years-judge-rules-11663976573"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona Judge Allows 