\par \par \uc2 Alaska's ranked-choice voting could benefit Sen. \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2 , who has an advantage in campaign funding. PHOTO: Ash Adams for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Conservative Scott Klein, 58, a contractor in Soldotna, said he is fed up with Mr. Trump and has sworn off voting for Republicans. He might make an exception for Ms. Murkowski because of her vote to convict Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 "It probably just swung my vote a little bit more her way," he said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Murkowski's voting record puts her at the middle of the Senate, more conservative than any Democrat, and more liberal than any Republican save one, Sen. \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine, according to a calculation by FiveThirtyEight.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Ms. Murkowski secured funds for the Port of Nome and other state projects in the roughly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/infrastructure-bill-2021-what-11627515002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $1 trillion bipartisan infrastructure law }}}\uc2  that she helped negotiate. The Nome project will extend the causeway and double the port's depth to about 40 feet, opening the area to bigger ships, a higher volume of goods and more research and tourism.\par \par \uc2 Nome, with a population of about 3,800, is reachable only by boat or plane. It serves as a regional hub for even more isolated outposts, mines and Native villages, and as the finish line of the Iditarod dogsled race.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Tshibaka said that while Nome needs money for its port, she would have voted against the infrastructure bill because it opened the door for more regulations and allowed "exorbitant spending" for a climate-change agenda that she says hurts Alaska's oil-and-gas industry.\par \par \uc2 Unlike Ms. Murkowski, who recently tweeted a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/lisamurkowski/status/1559579901807706114?s=20&t=g-DCOfXDuEXetaqLZ0CHMw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 map promoting her earmarks }}}\uc2 , Ms. Tshibaka said she would oppose the practice of allowing lawmakers to direct funding for specific projects if it came up for a vote. Still, she said as long as the Senate allows earmarks, "We would get what we can for Alaska."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Murkowski is political royalty in Alaska. Her father, Frank Murkowski, appointed her to fill his \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seat in 2002, after he won the governorship. In 2004, the state GOP organized sending more than a million pieces of mail to boost Ms. Murkowski's first re-election, but the relationship deteriorated after that, said Randy Ruedrich, former chair of the Alaska Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 "It's gone from extremely good to extremely poor," Mr. Ruedrich said, citing what he sees as her steady leftward shift. Her stances have "caused more and more and more Republicans to question how they can support her, and ultimately not support her," he said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Murkowski's break with Mr. Trump came in June 2020, when she said she was struggling with whether she could still support him, and he responded by trying to oust her. "Get any candidate ready, good or bad, I don't care, I'm endorsing," Mr. Trump tweeted. "If you have a pulse, I'm with you!" \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw584\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8760\pichgoal7200
