\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Real Midterm Election Stakes; Will voters put a check on the unrequited ambitions of the Democratic left?\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 1,091 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2022\par 20:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 If the election polls are right, Democrats have a good chance of adding to their majority in the Senate and even keeping it in the House. Toward that end they are trying to convince voters that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are the main election issues. But the real issue, by far the most important for actual policy, is whether voters will put a legislative check on the political left.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsjplus.com/invites/opinion-live-congress-dallas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 WSJ Opinion Live: Can Republicans Retake Congress?}}}\par \par \uc2 Join Journal Editorial Page Editor Paul Gigot and Columnists Kimberley Strassel and Karl Rove live from Dallas as they discuss how inflation, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Supreme Court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling will affect the midterms. What's at stake in the House and Senate? Will the red wave hit as many predict? The panel will break down what the election will mean for the economy, President Biden's legislative agenda, and the run up to the 2024 presidential race.\par \par \uc2 WSJ+ members are invited to attend this exclusive member event live in Dallas, TX, or via livestream online on Monday, October 17 at 7:00 PM CT / 8:00 PM ET. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://events.wsjplus.com/OpinionLiveOct"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Purchase}}}\uc2  tickets to the live event in Dallas or to register for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/live-qa/wsj-opinion-live-can-republicans-retake-congress/35FC8ECA-5145-4355-AA65-F235F1F47C30"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 virtual livestream}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump isn't even on the ballot this year, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will be President at least through 2024. No national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law is likely to pass Congress as long as the 60-vote legislative filibuster rule remains in the Senate. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law could change in many states depending on election results, but probably not in most.\par \par \uc2 In other words, the Democratic election strategy is a new version of their 2020 campaign bait-and-switch. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Democrats in Congress won by making the election a referendum on Mr. Trump and Covid-19. But once in office they pivoted to advance a far-left labor agenda and enact the biggest expansion of government in modern history. They succeeded on many fronts, and only the opposition of Sens. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  prevented them from killing the filibuster to do much more.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic left hasn't given up on any of this, however, as they are admitting if you pay attention. The media are ignoring this as they echo the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Trump narrative. So it's worth laying out what Democrats really have in mind, based on what they tried to do this Congress and are promising for the next.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Start with targeting the filibuster, which is priority one because a 51-vote Senate majority opens the door to everything the current House has passed. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  made that clear in a recent speech to the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "With just two more seats in the Senate, we can codify Roe v. Wade, we can put the protections of Roe in law," Ms. Harris said. "With two more seats in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  we can pass the Freedom to Vote Act and the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Act. Two more seats."\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senate candidates in Wisconsin (Mandela Barnes), Pennsylvania (\uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 ) and Ohio (\uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 ) say they'd vote to kill the filibuster. They also favor adding Washington, D.C., as a new state. "50/50 is not enough. We must expand our Democratic Senate majority to abolish the filibuster once and for all. And our campaign is the best chance to do it," Mr. Ryan tweeted in January. And he claims to be a moderate.\par \par \uc2 Arizona Sen. Mark Kelly and New Hampshire Sen. \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  have supported a filibuster carve-out for voting legislation. The H.R.1 voting bill creates a federal right to mail ballots and overrides state laws banning ballot harvesting. It requires same-day voter registration and up to 15 days of early voting.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats also passed H.R.4 that would require the Justice Department or D.C. district court to approve election-law changes in states and localities with a putative history of voting-rights violations. The Attorney General would have broad discretion, and you can bet GOP states will be the main targets as they were under \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  before the Supreme Court voided pre-clearance in Shelby County v. Holder (2013).\par \par \uc2 Once the legislative filibuster goes for one issue, the political pressure will be enormous to kill it for much more. The Pro Act will surely make a comeback. That bill would override state right-to-work laws that give workers the choice of joining a union; impose a backdoor card-check procedure that neuters secret-ballot election; impose the Obama-era joint-employer standard that put corporations and contractors on the hook for workers they don't employ but "indirectly" control, and much more.\par \par \uc2 Democrats also want to pass a CO2 emissions "enforcement mechanism" that failed this year because it violated Senate budget reconciliation rules. Ditto a provision originally in the Inflation Reduction Act that gave the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  sweeping power to regulate CO2 emissions under the Clean Air Act.\par \par \uc2 Build Back Better entitlements including free universal pre-K, child-care subsidies, an expanded refundable child tax credit, paid family leave and Medicare dental and hearing benefits will re-emerge from the grave. Many Democrats are hankering to lower the Medicare age to 60 from 65, and Mr. Ryan co-sponsored legislation in the House.\par \par \uc2 Major tax increases will also be back in play. Ways and Means Chairman \uc2 Richard Neal\uc2  recently told a \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reporter that Democrats will raise individual and corporate tax rates if they keep power. Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  said in a recent speech that the Administration's goal is to return "tax rates for high earners and corporations to historical norms."\par \par \uc2 How high is "historical"? Mr. Neal's original proposal in 2021 raised the corporate rate to 26.5%, and the individual rate to 39.6% from 37% at $400,000 in income and 42.6% above $5 million, and imposed a tax on capital gains at death. With two more Senators, expect those rates to go much higher.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 There's much more we could include here, but you get the idea. If Democrats add seats in the Senate and hold the House, there won't be much of a check on progressive ambitions. If they accomplish this with inflation at 8%, they will be even more emboldened. That is what's really on the ballot in November.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-real-midterm-election-stakes-voting-spending-inflation-environment-filibuster-11663528778"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Real Midterm Election Stakes}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220918ei9i002p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
