\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 State  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans Face Religious-Liberty Lawsuits From the Left; Some faith groups argue new laws burden their religious rights after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 1,311 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2022\par 15:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  take effect in a number of states, faith leaders with liberal views on the issue are taking a page out of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-exempts-some-companies-from-health-care-law-on-religious-grounds-1404138333"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the conservative legal playbook  }}}\uc2 , arguing in new lawsuits that the restrictions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-oks-religious-and-moral-exemptions-for-birth-control-coverage-11594217946"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 infringe on their religious beliefs  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Rabbis, Christian ministers, Buddhists and Quakers are among legal challengers who say the bans are preventing them from exercising their own religious views about when abortions are permissible. They also say the new laws have made clergy afraid that if they counsel their parishioners on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they could face legal risk for aiding and abetting the procedure.\par \par \uc2 About a dozen states {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have begun enforcing laws  }}}\uc2  prohibiting many or most abortions since the Supreme Court issued a decision in June {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning Roe v. Wade  }}}\uc2 , the 1973 decision that created federal constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Many lawsuits challenging the bans tend to focus on arguments that state constitutions offer guarantees of equal protection and personal liberty that include the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The religious-freedom lawsuits are taking a different tack.\par \par \uc2 In Indiana, \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  is representing a group known as Hoosier Jews for Choice and a handful of other religious plaintiffs challenging the state's newly enacted law banning most abortions, arguing it violates the state's Religious Freedom Restoration Act, which prohibits government action that interferes with a person's religious exercise absent a compelling objective.\par \par \uc2 Rabbi Dennis Sasso, who leads an Indiana synagogue that describes itself as egalitarian and embraces people of a variety of races, gender identities and sexual orientations, said that in Judaism life doesn't begin at conception but at birth. "The assumption that we are dealing with a full life competing against the life of the mother is not in keeping with the Jewish moral understanding," which "gives precedence to the established life of the mother," said Rabbi Sasso, who signed an affidavit in the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, plaintiffs in a handful of lawsuits have argued that the state's law banning most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy violates rights to freedom of speech and religious liberty found in the state and federal constitutions, as well as a Florida law designed to protect religious freedom.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, four United Methodist ministers sent a letter to the state's attorney general arguing a new ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  violates Texas's religious-freedom law and said they may sue in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  's office didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Courts in recent years have been receptive to an increasing number of prominent lawsuits challenging government laws based on religious-freedom claims. Typically, those cases have been brought by plaintiffs articulating conservative views.\par \par \uc2 One landmark case came in 2014 when the conservative-led Supreme Court ruled that closely held companies, such as arts-and-crafts chain \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2  , could object on religious grounds to providing contraceptive coverage in their insurance plans. In 2020, the court issued decisions that expanded exemptions for religious employers from healthcare regulations and antidiscrimination laws.\par \par \uc2 Among recent developments, a federal judge in Texas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/judge-rules-against-health-laws-requirement-to-cover-hiv-prevention-drugs-11662580586"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled this month  }}}\uc2  that requiring employers to provide coverage for HIV-prevention drugs violated the rights of an employer who objected to the mandate on religious grounds.\par \par \uc2 In passing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, Republican legislators "haven't taken into account that progressives have religious beliefs that also could be burdened," said Marci Hamilton, a \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2  professor who is representing clergy from a variety of faiths in Florida. "What these lawsuits do is they flip the script."\par \par \uc2 Not everyone agrees. Douglas Laycock, a professor at the University of Virginia Law School, called the cases "wishful thinking." To make a successful freedom-of-religion challenge under the First Amendment or other laws, he said, plaintiffs would have to show not only that their religion permits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but that religious belief is also a substantial motivation for obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That is likely to apply only in a narrow range of cases, Prof. Laycock said.\par \par \uc2 The decision about whether to challenge \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions on religious grounds has presented thorny questions for some left-leaning civil liberties advocates, who have often opposed the expansion of religious rights by lawmakers and the courts.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the first such lawsuit challenging an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban was filed in Florida in June by Barry Silver, a rabbi and civil rights attorney who founded a school of Judaism known as "cosmic Judaism" that endeavors to unite religion and science, on behalf of his synagogue, Congregation L'Dor Va-Dor. Rabbi Silver said he encountered pushback from others in the liberal Jewish community and from civil-rights groups concerned that such claims could also be used to challenge laws that protect gay people and others.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How do you think courts might respond to religious-freedom claims around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 In July, \uc2 Sheila Katz\uc2  , chief executive of the National Council of Jewish Women, sent an email urging Jewish leaders to consult with the group before filing any lawsuits on religious freedom grounds, saying, "A seemingly successful argument can result in bad law that further harms those most impacted on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and potentially create new harms to the LGBTQ community and to minority faith communities."\par \par \uc2 Rabbi Silver said he lost two potential plaintiffs in his suit as a result. He filed a motion this week asking the court to allow him to bring in new plaintiffs, including a Jewish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctor, a Quaker group, a Buddhist and a Unitarian minister.\par \par \uc2 He said liberal religious leaders should take full advantage of religious freedom arguments, even if in the past they have been used by those they disagree with on issues such as gay rights. "Now it's going to be used by everybody," he said.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Ms. Katz said the council is supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawsuits by Rabbi Silver and others and is working with groups to make sure they have the right legal advice and strategy.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for Florida Republican Attorney General \uc2 Ashley Moody\uc2  , one of the defendants in the suit, said, "We will continue to defend the state statute."\par \par \uc2 Nearly two dozen states as well as the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  have passed laws requiring federal or state governments to accommodate individuals' religious beliefs unless there is a compelling interest at stake that can't be attained through other means.\par \par \uc2 Indiana's Religious Freedom Restoration Act was signed by then-Gov. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  in 2015, part of a flurry of similar state laws that were proposed around the time of the Supreme Court decision granting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rules-in-favor-of-same-sex-marriage-1435180972"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 constitutional protection for same-sex marriage  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Indiana ACLU at the time opposed the passage of the state's RFRA, saying it would "allow anyone to use their religious beliefs to claim that they have a right to refuse to follow antidiscrimination protections and other laws."\par \par \uc2 Ken Falk, the legal director of the state's \uc2 ACLU\uc2  , said he views his job as a lawyer to use every legal tool at his disposal.\par \par \uc2 Indiana's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law allows doctors to perform the procedure in medical emergencies that threaten the life or physical health of the pregnant woman, or if the fetus has a lethal fetal anomaly. But the plaintiffs argue this exception is narrower than what Judaism demands.\par \par \uc2 State Attorney General Todd Rokita said he was dedicated to defending the new state law. "The text, history, and structure of our constitution excludes any serious argument that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a fundamental right in our state," he said. "Our legislators voted to stop these inhumane practices."\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:laura.kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laura.kusisto@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/state-abortion-bans-face-religious-liberty-lawsuits-from-the-left-11663343259"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 State \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Bans Face Religious-Liberty Lawsuits From the Left  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220916ei9g003xp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
