\par\par \uc2 Essay\par \uc2 Life\par \b \uc2 Kansas Finds a Path Beyond Partisanship; In a tense election year, voters in a conservative state are showing their independence and avoiding the extremes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gerald F. Seib \par \uc2 2,387 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par 14:54 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In this midterm election year of high national political drama, Kansas has already made its mark.\par \par \uc2 In August, the Sunflower State held a highly anticipated referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the first big test of public opinion on the issue since the Supreme Court's decision striking down Roe v. Wade. The referendum would have overturned a state court ruling that found the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state constitution, thus handing the matter to a state legislature seemingly aiming to further restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In a conservative state that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  carried by nearly 15 percentage points in 2020, independent voters turned out in waves and joined with Democrats and some Republicans to send the proposition down to defeat by 18 points.\par \par \uc2 The size of the turnout and the result shocked even those who had led the campaign for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. "I wasn't surprised we won," said Ashley All, an activist who helped lead the pro-choice forces. "I was absolutely surprised by the margin."\par \par \uc2 Now, with election day fast approaching, Kansas is the site of one of the nation's most competitive governor's races. In what is shaping up as a down year for Democrats, and in a heavily Republican state, polls have shown Democratic Gov. \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2  running almost even in her re-election bid.\par \par \uc2 Just as unexpected in this era of intense and entrenched partisanship, the biggest overall increase in voter registration this year has come not among Kansas Republicans or Democrats but among voters who identify as "unaffiliated." In percentage terms, the biggest registration growth has actually been seen by a small third party, the Libertarians.\par \par \uc2 In sum, Kansas is demonstrating, as it often has in its history, that it is a quirky state. But this time, its quirkiness might be telling other Americans some important things about the strengths of our seemingly troubled democracy.\par \par \uc2 Kansas is clearly conservative and Republican. But its citizens have shown in a variety of ways that they possess a wide independent streak. Even amid ample partisan rancor, Kansas is telling us that voters aren't quite as locked into their partisan silos as many analysts think.\par \par \uc2 In balancing fiercely competing forces, Kansas is showing that, at a time when moderation seems outmoded in American politics, voters will still sometimes seek a middle path. Kansas "is not a state that rewards the extremes," said Eric Pahls, a Republican campaign consultant working on the current governor's race.\par \par \uc2 Kansas might also be showing the political power of women this year\u8212\'20\'14and warning both major parties to be careful about taking their own supporters for granted.\par \par \uc2 I recently returned for the fall to Kansas, my home state, after four decades living and writing in the political hothouse of Washington, D.C. Overall, the state has moved rightward during my decades away.\par \par \uc2 Consider Ellis County, where I grew up. Its citizens of Volga German ancestry once made it a reliably Democratic island in a sea of Western Kansas Republicanism. In 1964, the last time a Democratic presidential candidate carried Kansas, almost seven in 10 Ellis County voters went for Lyndon B. Johnson. Today, Ellis County has gone red. Registered Republicans outnumber Democrats by well over a two-to-one margin, and in 2020 the presidential vote was the reverse of 1964: Mr. Trump was the choice of seven in 10.\par \par \uc2 During my time away, author Thomas Frank wrote a 2004 book, "What's the Matter With Kansas?" that explored why working-class Kansans seemed, in Mr. Frank's view, to be voting against their own economic interests by supporting conservative Republicans rather than a Democratic Party that had traditionally worked on behalf of laborers and farmers.\par \par \uc2 But perhaps Kansans weren't really acting illogically. They were joining many other Americans by giving priority to cultural values and issues at a time when Democrats didn't seem to be offering clear answers to their economic concerns\u8212\'20\'14and at a time when Democrats were moving, in the view of many here, too far left on social issues. For their part, Kansas Democrats will tell you privately that the national party didn't pay much attention to their state.\par \par \uc2 In any case, Kansas over those years helped to lead the national Republican party's move to the right on cultural issues. Social conservatives muscled out traditional moderate Republicans in local races, most notably in the Kansas City suburbs of Johnson County.\par \par \uc2 Former U.S. Sen. \uc2 Sam Brownback\uc2  easily won the governor's mansion in 2010, running as a decidedly more fiscally and culturally conservative candidate than his Republican predecessors. Over time, Republicans solidified their control of the state legislature. Democrats haven't controlled one of its houses in three decades, and Republicans today have a veto-proof majority in the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 Still, beneath the surface, Kansas has held on to its independent and contrarian streaks, which are deeply embedded in its political history. William Allen White, the legendary Kansas newspaper editor, was a close ally of Teddy Roosevelt and helped champion his progressive version of Republicanism when it was brand new. When \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  mounted a challenge to both parties with an independent presidential run in 1992, only three states gave him a higher share of their vote than did Kansas.\par \par \uc2 Women often have been at the forefront as Kansans defied stereotypes. Kansas was home to the nation's first elected female mayor and gave women the right to vote eight years before the country did. In 1978, Republican Nancy Kassebaum was the first woman elected to a full term in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in her own right, without previously being appointed or elected to succeed her husband. Kansas is the only state in the country to have elected three female Democratic governors, notes \uc2 Kathleen Sebelius\uc2 , who was one of them.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What do you think Americans can learn from Kansas about politics? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 One of Kansas's four members of the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2  today is \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 , a lesbian Native American woman, who now is locked in a tough re-election battle after the Republican state legislature drew her into a less-friendly district. Though it hasn't sent a Democrat to the Senate since 1932, Kansas has for a half century followed a consistent pattern of alternating between Democratic and Republican governors.\par \par \uc2 These trends intersect in Kansas's current Gov. Kelly, a onetime independent who later became a Democrat. Polls show her running in a tight race with Republican Derek Schmidt in her bid for re-election. "There's definitely an ebb and flow in Kansas," said Republican \uc2 Jeff Colyer\uc2 , who preceded Ms. Kelly in the governor's office.\par \par \uc2 That ebb and flow may seem contradictory. How can a state become more partisan, as Kansas has in many respects, while retaining an independent streak at the same time? It is a question with national significance right now. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscale