\par \par \uc2 Students, parents and teachers from a Kansas City elementary school march in a parade on Sep. 3. PHOTO: Chase Castor for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 The answer lies in part in the ability of voters to divide their thinking between national and local politics, to compartmentalize their views depending on circumstances and issues, and to send unflinching messages to both parties.\par \par \uc2 As a nation, "we are more polarized and bitterly partisan, often over symbols," said Patrick Miller, a political science professor at the \uc2 University of Kansas\uc2 . "Often Democrats and Republicans may disagree with one another because they think the other person is evil."\par \par \uc2 But, he added, "Two things can be true at the same time. That entire polarized, symbolic partisan warfare scenario can coexist with not everything being perfectly polarized and not neat. And in this sense, Kansas isn't alone."\par \par \uc2 Indeed, in this era in which the country appears to have sorted itself into solidly red and blue enclaves in presidential voting, it's easy to forget that isn't true up and down the ballot. Maryland and Massachusetts are reliably Democratic states that have Republican governors. Montana and West Virginia are two of the most solidly Republican states in presidential politics, yet they have sent two Democrats, Jon Tester and \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , to the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The role model for Kansas may well be the late Sen. \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 , the most formidable political figure the state has produced in recent times. Mr. Dole, a onetime Senate majority leader and GOP presidential candidate, was the ultimate Republican party loyalist and early in his career was seen as a partisan slasher. Yet he also became the Senate's foremost architect of bipartisan legislative achievements, sometimes by working with the chamber's most liberal Democrats.\par \par \uc2 In a similar vein, Kansas voters have shown consistently that they don't trust the national Democratic party\u8212\'20\'14hence the consistent vote for Republicans in presidential elections\u8212\'20\'14but they are willing to give a hearing to Democrats in statewide races.\par \par \uc2 More than that, many Kansans seem to hold the traditional conservative suspicion of giving too much entrenched power to any party or institution, even one they like, and they seem to view the periodic Democratic governor as a way to offset a Republican legislature. Kansans appear to believe that "if you elect nothing but Republicans you might see some of this wild, extreme activity," said Russell Arben Fox, a political science professor at Friends University in Wichita. "You've got to put somebody in the governor's mansion who is going to control it."\par \par \uc2 Moreover, Kansans these days seem to have grown wary of what they see as extremism from any direction. Even the once broadly popular Mr. Brownback fell from favor when voters concluded that his tax-cutting policies had gone too far and hurt the state's schools.\par \par \uc2 Current Republican U.S. Sen. Roger Marshall has succeeded in part by positioning himself as a sensible conservative. He defeated incumbent Republican Rep. Tim Huelskamp in a 2016 primary by portraying him as having gotten too enmeshed in Washington's ideological warfare at the expense of the Kansas farmers he was supposed to represent.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, Mr. Marshall won the GOP nomination for a \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seat by prevailing over a hard-edged conservative, former state attorney general Kris Kobach, whose firebrand style once captivated the state's Republicans. Then Mr. Marshall won the general election by portraying his Democratic opponent, herself a former Republican who was campaigning as a moderate, as someone who had moved too far left for the state.\par \par \uc2 Whether Sen. Marshall himself may someday face a challenge from the center remains to be seen: He was one of only five Senators who voted against certifying President Biden's victories in both Arizona and Pennsylvania in 2020. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
