\par\par \uc2 Upward Mobility\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Polling and Other Republican Midterm Troubles; With weak candidates and awkward pro-life rhetoric, the party worries about its prospects for November.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jason L. Riley \par \uc2 979 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2022\par 22:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the 11 weeks since the Supreme Court's Dobbs decision overturning Roe v. Wade, the Republican Party's ability to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained a work in progress. Between now and November, however, Republicans had better find their tongue on the issue, or Democrats could make them pay.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsjplus.com/invites/opinion-live-congress-dallas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 WSJ Opinion Live: Can Republicans Retake Congress?}}}\par \par \uc2 Join Journal Editorial Page Editor Paul Gigot and Columnists Kimberley Strassel and Karl Rove live from Dallas as they discuss how inflation, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Supreme Court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling will affect the midterms. What's at stake in the House and Senate? Will the red wave hit as many predict? The panel will break down what the election will mean for the economy, President Biden's legislative agenda, and the run up to the 2024 presidential race.\par \par \uc2 WSJ+ members are invited to attend this exclusive member event live in Dallas, TX, or via livestream online on Monday, October 17 at 7:00 PM CT / 8:00 PM ET. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://events.wsjplus.com/OpinionLiveOct"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Purchase}}}\uc2  tickets to the live event in Dallas or to register for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/live-qa/wsj-opinion-live-can-republicans-retake-congress/35FC8ECA-5145-4355-AA65-F235F1F47C30"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 virtual livestream}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When Roe was the law of the land, it was easier for Republican candidates to be pro-life absolutists because the issue was out of their hands, and what they said had no practical consequences. After Dobbs, the conversation is no longer abstract. The decisions of Republican lawmakers will affect real people in dramatic ways.\par \par \uc2 If ducking the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  question for the next eight weeks isn't an option, how should pro-life candidates talk about it? That's the question I posed recently to veteran Republican pollster and political consultant Whit Ayres. "Republicans need to take a mainstream pro-life position," he told me. "What that mainstream position is depends on the particular state. But what it is not is a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all circumstances without exception for rape, incest and the life of the mother." There's no national consensus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he added. "But a mainstream pro-life position includes those exceptions."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ayres also stressed that tone and language matter immensely if the goal is to win over people who disagree with you. "More than half of Americans know someone who has had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or has had one themselves," he said. "So any politician talking about this issue needs to project a tone of tolerance to those with different views. They need to express compassion for women who are struggling with an unwanted pregnancy. Calling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  'murder' will never persuade anyone to join their side."\par \par \uc2 The Dobbs ruling, which has boosted enthusiasm among Democrats generally and younger women in particular, isn't the only reason that Republicans have been forced to temper their hopes of big congressional gains in November. Gasoline prices that many consumers view as a proxy for overall inflation have fallen for 13 straight weeks, helping to neutralize a key vulnerability for Democrats. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Mar-a-Lago home has put the former president back on the front pages, which is where Democrats want him, to divert attention away from violent crime, illegal immigration, the deficit and other domestic problems they'd rather not discuss.\par \par \uc2 The biggest GOP problem, however, may turn out to have been self-inflicted. In several prominent Senate races, Republican voters chose rookie candidates over more experienced rivals, and those nominees are struggling. The Republican nominees in Arizona and Ohio have had trouble raising money. In Georgia, where for months Republican Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  has held a comfortable lead over \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , his Democratic challenger, Senate nominee \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  hasn't been able to pull away from incumbent Democrat Raphael Warnock. Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  took some lumps recently for musing that candidate quality had him worried about the GOP's ability to retake the Senate, but current polling suggests that he was on to something.\par \par \uc2 Of course, citing polls isn't popular with a lot of Republicans these days, even though news media continue to rely on them to gauge voter sentiment and speculate about outcomes on Election Day. When pollsters miss the mark, cynicism rises, and recent election cycles have led to more people questioning the trustworthiness of political polling.\par \par \uc2 In 2016 the polling underestimated support for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and four years later it happened again. According to an analysis published last year by the \uc2 American Association for Public Opinion Research\uc2 , national surveys for the 2020 presidential contests were the least accurate in four decades, and state polls for congressional and gubernatorial races weren't much better. The errors seemed to run in one direction. "Whether the candidates were running for president, senator, or governor, poll margins overall suggested that Democratic candidates would do better and Republican candidates would do worse relative to the final certified voter," the report said.\par \par \uc2 Conspiracy theorists won't be satisfied, but Mr. Ayres and others in his profession insist that the still-unresolved problem boils down to the difficulty of getting Trump voters to engage with pollsters. "The criticism of the polling in 2016 and 2020 was not overstated," he said. "The errors were driven primarily by nonresponse bias\u8212\'20\'14that is, people who supported Trump simply refused to have anything to do with pollsters."\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Ayres warned against extrapolating too much from these past two presidential contests. "In the 2018 midterms, polling nationally was far better than in 2016 or 2020, and our own polling in 2018 was dead on," he said. "I believe the polling error is a Trump phenomenon. Trump was on the ballot in 2016 and 2020 but not in 2018. And he won't be on the ballot in 2022."\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-polling-and-other-republican-troubles-roe-dobbs-midterms-election-candidate-democrats-voters-inflation-gas-prices-11663102690"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22 , Polling and Other Republican Midterm Troubles}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220913ei9d006hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
