\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Support for Legalized  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Grows Since Dobbs Ruling, WSJ Poll Shows; More than half of voters said the issue made them more likely to cast ballots in the midterm elections; majorities oppose 6-week and 15-week  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catherine Lucey \par \uc2 1,134 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2022\par 13:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14Voters have grown more supportive of legalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court overturning Roe v. Wade, }}}\uc2  with a clear majority opposing restrictions, like bans at a certain point of pregnancy or barring women from traveling to get a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to a new Wall Street Journal poll that underscores {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democratic-midterm-prospects-improve-as-races-heat-up-wsj-poll-finds-11662024601"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the importance of the issue in the midterm elections }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to the survey, 60% of voters said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, up from 55% in March. Another 29% said it should be illegal, except in cases of rape, incest and when the woman's life is endangered, compared with 30% in March. And 6% said it should be illegal in all cases, down from 11% in March.\par \par \uc2 The court's decision to end federal constitutional protections for the procedure has injected new Democratic energy into a midterm election that Republicans expected to be dominated by economic issues. About a dozen states {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have banned many or most abortions }}}\uc2  since the Supreme Court's ruling in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not an issue that most people, prior to Dobbs, spent a lot of time thinking about," said Democratic pollster Molly Murphy, whose firm conducted the poll with Republican pollster Tony Fabrizio. "What Dobbs has done is one, we've had a national conversation about it. Two, it has gone from hypothetical to real."\par \par \uc2 More than half of voters said the ruling made them more motivated to vote in the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Asked broadly about their top issue for the midterms, voters cited the economy and inflation first, followed by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But when offered a choice of five issues and asked which made them most likely to vote, they put the Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe v. Wade ahead of inflation.\par \par \uc2 Among those who named the court ruling as the most important issue tested against others, 77% were Democrats, 8% were Republicans and 9% were independents.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What's the future for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights across the U.S.? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 "I was already going to vote anyway in the midterms. What this made me do is really take a harder look at local elections," said Jacob Kendrick, 22, a Democrat and recent college graduate from Detroit. Referring to the Republican candidate for governor in the state, he said: "Now, I'm like, let me make sure they're not a Tudor Dixon candidate who is extreme on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Cecilia Bowman, 63, of Lenoir, N.C., said she is strongly opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A Republican, she runs a food pantry and two thrift stores. Her husband is a Christian pastor.\par \par \uc2 "I think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal," she said, saying that being opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  influences how she votes. "I put all my faith and trust in God. If I were to be raped and I did get pregnant and I could not take care of that baby, somebody would."\par \par \uc2 On which party is best able to handle \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, 48% said Democrats, 27% Republicans, 16% said neither and 6% said both equally. A total of 41% of independents said they trust Democrats most to handle \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, compared with 18% who said Republicans were best.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a motivating issue in some key ballot tests ahead of the midterms. Kansas voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-abortion-vote-results-11659440554"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejected a measure }}}\uc2  that would have amended the state's constitution to explicitly say it doesn't protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen as a key issue for Democrat Pat Ryan, who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrats-outlook-for-midterm-elections-brightens-after-new-york-win-11661368975"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won a special election }}}\uc2 to fill an open \uc2 U.S. House\uc2  seat in New York's Hudson Valley.\par \par \uc2 Support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in most or all cases rose among Catholics to 59%, up from 45% in March. Support from Black voters was at 69%, up from 59%. College-educated women moved to 76% in support, up from 65%.\par \par \uc2 "It's definitely a motivator," said Elizabeth Schoenknecht, 46, of Hudson, Wis., who is a registered Democrat and works in philanthropy. "It's heartbreaking to see the reality come to fruition."\par \par \uc2 Among Democrats, 92% said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, 6% said it should be illegal except in some cases, such as rape, incest, and when the woman's life is endangered, and 1% said it should be illegal in all cases. A total of 59% of independents said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, 26% said illegal except in limited cases and 7% illegal in all cases.\par \par \uc2 With Republicans, 27% said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, 56% said it should illegal except in limited cases and 11% said it should be illegal in all cases.\par \par \uc2 "The truth of the matter is even among Republicans there isn't a clear consensus. They want restrictions, the question is what restrictions and how far should they go," said Mr. Fabrizio, the GOP pollster.\par \par \uc2 The poll also showed clear opposition to the types of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions being enacted or discussed in some states. A total of 62% opposed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban at 6 weeks of pregnancy that only included an exception for the health of the mother, and 57% opposed a ban at 15 weeks with an exception only for the health of the mother. The survey said 77% opposed banning women who live in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal from traveling to other states to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And 81% were against banning all abortions.\par \par \uc2 "I would be comfortable with some restrictions, but not very darn many," said Karen Zabrowski, 71, a Democrat and retired teacher from Eau Claire, Wis. She said she doesn't support late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the mother's life is in danger and said she could support a 15-week ban, but not a 6-week. On \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she said: "It's about taking the freedom of adult people away. It's between a woman and her partner and her god."\par \par \uc2 On prosecuting doctors who conduct abortions, 70% were in opposition. On restricting access to some contraception, like the morning after pill, 78% were opposed.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal poll of 1,313 registered voters, with a margin of error of plus or minus 2.7 percentage points, was conducted Aug. 17-25 by Impact Research and Fabrizio, \uc2 Lee & Associates\uc2 . Half of the interviews were done on cellphones, with the remaining split evenly between landline phones and text messaging accompanied by an online survey.\par \par \uc2 Write to Catherine Lucey at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:catherine.lucey@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 catherine.lucey@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/support-for-legalized-abortion-grows-since-dobbs-ruling-wsj-poll-shows-11662210020"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Support for Legalized \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Grows Since Dobbs Ruling, WSJ Poll Shows  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220903ei93001e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
