\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Ambition of Michigan  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights; More reasons that this initiative isn't in the center of public opinion.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 563 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2022\par 22:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some of our friends on the right say that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-abortion-could-upend-michigan-referendum-novemeber-election-kansas-republicans-gretchen-whitmer-11661898041"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 our Wednesday editorial}}}\uc2  on a potential \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendum in Michigan was too understated about its true ambition. If voters in November agree, the proposed constitutional amendment will protect unrestricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability (about 24 weeks), similar to the line formerly used under Roe v. Wade.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsjplus.com/invites/opinion-live-congress-dallas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 WSJ Opinion Live: Can Republicans Retake Congress?}}}\par \par \uc2 Join Journal Editorial Page Editor Paul Gigot and Columnists Kimberley Strassel and Karl Rove live from Dallas as they discuss how inflation, Donald Trump and the Supreme Court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling will affect the midterms. What's at stake in the House and Senate? Will the red wave hit as many predict? The panel will break down what the election will mean for the economy, President Biden's legislative agenda, and the run up to the 2024 presidential race.\par \par \uc2 WSJ+ members are invited to attend this exclusive member event live in Dallas, TX, or via livestream online on Monday, October 17 at 7:00 PM CT / 8:00 PM ET. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://events.wsjplus.com/OpinionLiveOct"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Purchase}}}\uc2  tickets to the live event in Dallas or to register for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/live-qa/wsj-opinion-live-can-republicans-retake-congress/35FC8ECA-5145-4355-AA65-F235F1F47C30"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 virtual livestream}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But look at the language, our correspondents say. After fetal viability, the proposed amendment would guarantee \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access whenever "an attending health care professional" determined it to be "medically indicated to protect the life or physical or mental health of the pregnant individual." One red flag is the undefined exception for "mental health," which could be misused to justify abortions months after viability for anyone who's in distress.\par \par \uc2 Another point of concern is that the amendment asserts in expansive text that "every individual has a fundamental right to reproductive freedom." Could state courts interpret "every individual" to include pregnant minors, overruling Michigan's parental consent law? If \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is enshrined in the constitution as a "fundamental right," would that invalidate any attempt by the Legislature to single out such procedures for denial of public funding or support?\par \par \uc2 It's difficult to know exactly how Michigan's judiciary could broaden the amendment's effect, but voters who scrutinize the text on the ballot could end up unpleasantly surprised. State supreme courts have a record of creative legal thinking in the service of favored political outcomes, and we can cite recent legal gymnastics in North Carolina and Pennsylvania. Critics of the Michigan proposal have reason to be suspicious of how it brushes past points of public contention.\par \par \uc2 In any case, these details only heighten the responsibility of Michigan Republicans to find an alternative policy that they can defend to the electorate. Last month Kansas voted to keep a constitutional protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 59% to 41%. The lesson for pro-life advocates is that even in a red state like Kansas, if voters are given an all-or-nothing choice on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, they might well go for all.\par \par \uc2 The Michigan amendment isn't in the center of public opinion, but neither is the state's extant 1931 law, which bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with no exception for rape victims. If Republicans want to beat the constitutional amendment at the ballot box, now is the time to tell the public what kind of policy they intend to enact instead.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-ambition-of-michigan-abortion-rights-constitutional-amendment-republicans-ballot-measure-11662049852"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Ambition of Michigan \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Rights}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220901ei91006hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
