\par \par \uc2 The tougher stance on privacy by the \uc2 FTC\uc2  in Washington could lead to big changes in the data broker business and the broader online advertising industry. PHOTO: Elizabeth Frantz for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 The head of \uc2 Kochava\uc2 's data marketplace said Monday that the company was in the process of implementing the capability to remove data on sensitive locations as it had previously announced.\par \par \uc2 "Absent specificity from the \uc2 FTC\uc2 , we are constantly monitoring and proactively adjusting our technology to block geo data from other sensitive locations," said Brian Cox, general manager of Kochava Collective, in a written statement. "Unfortunately the only outcome the \uc2 FTC\uc2  desired was a settlement that had no clear terms or resolutions and redefined the problem into a moving target."\par \par \uc2 Kochava\uc2  said it obtained all of its location data from third-party data brokers, all of whom maintain that the data come from consenting consumers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2  lawsuit against \uc2 Kochava\uc2  and other investigations like it that are under way reflect a major shift in privacy policy under Chairwoman Lina Khan. The \uc2 FTC\uc2 's tougher stance could lead to big changes to the data broker business and the broader online advertising industry, with their reliance on consumer information harvested online.\par \par \uc2 "The massive exploitation of consumer geolocation data, stealthily gathered by apps and mobile devices, has become a fundamental feature of the commercial surveillance system," said \uc2 Jeff Chester\uc2  , executive director of the \uc2 Center for Digital Democracy\uc2  , a group that advocates for online privacy and consumer protections. "The commission has placed online data companies on notice they risk legal action and public exposure."\par \par \uc2 According to a recent \uc2 FTC\uc2  blog post, the agency is targeting use of both information location and data generated by consumers' search and other online activity, such as use of apps to test blood sugar, record sleep patterns or monitor blood pressure. "The potent combination of location data and user-generated health data creates a new frontier of potential harms to consumers," wrote Kristin Cohen, an \uc2 FTC\uc2  privacy official.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 FTC\uc2  is entering a legal gray area with its new approach, according to lawyers. Federal law contains privacy protections for medical information. But the internet has created new classes of easily accessed and revealing information about consumers' health interests and concerns that aren't covered by medical privacy. Protecting those categories of information requires the \uc2 FTC\uc2  to jump through more legal hoops.\par \par \uc2 Connected devices such as smartphones and fitness trackers gather a trail of location data on their users that can find its way into an under-the-radar marketplace, from big tech firms to data aggregators and brokers. They buy and sell the information, often to personalize ads or measure whether ads drove users to make purchases.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2  complaint against \uc2 Kochava\uc2 , filed in U.S. District Court in Idaho, accuses the company of unfairly selling sensitive data and cites the \uc2 FTC\uc2  Act's prohibition on "unfair or deceptive acts or practices in or affecting commerce."\par \par \uc2 Kochava\uc2 's "data may be used to identify consumers who have visited an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic and, as a result, may have had or contemplated having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," as well as medical professionals who provide abortions, the \uc2 FTC\uc2  complaint said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2  said that data available on \uc2 Kochava\uc2 's website as a free sample were sufficient to "identify a mobile device that visited a women's \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic and trace that mobile device to a single-family residence"\u8212\'20\'14thereby inferring the user's home address.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's June decision in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization  }}}\uc2 , and the following wave of state laws banning or restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has drawn attention from the \uc2 FTC\uc2  and others to the $10 billion-plus market for people's mobile-phone location data.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the ruling, several companies with user location data, including \uc2 Google\uc2  owner \uc2 Alphabet Inc\uc2  ., revised their policies to obscure visits to sensitive locations, including facilities that provide abortions.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2  complaint also calls out \uc2 Kochava\uc2 's data on visits to houses of worship, homeless shelters, domestic violence shelters and addiction recovery centers. "The release of this data could expose them to stigma, discrimination, physical violence, emotional distress, and other harms," the \uc2 FTC\uc2  said in a press release.\par \par \uc2 Kochava\uc2  said in its earlier statement that the federal agency's threatened lawsuit against it was a "manipulative attempt\u8230\'20\'26to give the appearance that it is protecting consumer privacy despite being based on completely false pretenses."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2  is investigating a range of companies that handle health data, seeking to force them to agree to tough settlements restricting their use and sale of sensitive data, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the Dobbs decision, President Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-to-sign-order-stepping-up-efforts-to-protect-access-to-reproductive-care-11657278000"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued an executive order  }}}\uc2  encouraging the \uc2 FTC\uc2  to take new actions to protect consumers' privacy when they seek information about \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In July, the \uc2 FTC\uc2  also warned businesses publicly that it intended to go after illegal use and sharing of highly sensitive consumer data, including sensitive health data. In 2021, the agency also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ftc-reaches-settlement-with-flo-health-over-fertility-tracking-app-11610568915"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 took action against fertility app  }}}\uc2 Flo Health\uc2  for sharing sensitive health data improperly with third parties.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ftc-launches-effort-to-expand-online-privacy-protections-11660232848"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The commission also recently announced  }}}\uc2  that it would begin the lengthy process of considering rules to protect the privacy of a range of consumer data.\par \par \uc2 Write to John D. McKinnon at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:john.mckinnon@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 john.mckinnon@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Patience Haggin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:patience.haggin@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 patience.haggin@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Patience Haggin at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ftc-sues-idaho-company-for-selling-sensitive-tracking-data-11661798303"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 FTC Sues Idaho Company for Selling Sensitive Tracking Data  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220829ei8t003jt\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
