\par\par \uc2 Midterm Election 2022\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Republicans Still Favored to Win Back House Control but Outlook Tightens; Polls, nonpartisan analysts suggest Democratic enthusiasm strengthening heading into critical Labor Day stretch\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joshua Jamerson \par \uc2 1,413 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2022\par 16:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Republicans remain the favorites to win control of the House of Representatives in the midterm elections, but Democrats, who for over a year have braced for large losses, are finishing the summer in a stronger position than where they started.\par \par \uc2 Public polling and recent election results are prompting Democrats, nonpartisan election observers and some Republicans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrats-outlook-for-midterm-elections-brightens-after-new-york-win-11661368975"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to expect a more competitive battle this fall }}}\uc2 . Republicans need just a handful of seat pickups to take over the House, which Democrats currently control 220-211 with four vacancies.\par \par \uc2 "The party in power typically doesn't improve its electoral prospects in the final months of a midterm, but that's where we appear to be," Nathan Gonzales and Jacob Rubashkin of Inside Elections, a nonpartisan election watcher, wrote in a report.\par \par \uc2 The analysts cited various factors helping Democrats as they try to close the gap. Those include {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrats-weigh-new-legislation-in-response-to-abortion-ruling-11656424248"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the fight over \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access }}}\uc2 , weak GOP candidates in key races, investigations focused on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/gas-prices-dip-to-4-a-gallon-for-first-time-since-march-11660160057"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a recent drop in gasoline prices }}}\uc2 . They added that districts that Mr. Biden carried by at least 10 percentage points seemed more out of reach for the GOP than at this point last year.\par \par \uc2 The nonpartisan Cook Political Report {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cookpolitical.com/analysis/house/2022-primaries/red-wave-looks-more-ripple?redirect=6309666ab7bf7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lowered its projection for GOP pickups }}}\uc2  to a range of 10-20 seats, still enough to win the chamber but down from 15-30 seats previously. Cook also said it couldn't rule out the possibility that Democrats retain their majority, citing elevated Democratic turnout.\par \par \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , chair of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 , predicted Sunday that the midterms will see Democrats gain seats in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , pick up some governors' mansions and hold onto their majority in the House of Representatives. "That momentum is real," he said on \uc2 CBS\uc2 's "Face the Nation" news show.\par \par \uc2 Maryland Gov. \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , a moderate Republican, said he fears the GOP will fail to retake the Senate or pick up gubernatorial seats, though he still thinks the party will win the House.\par \par \uc2 "This should be a really huge year for Republicans just because of the failures of the Democrats who are in control of everything and Biden's low approval ratings," Mr. Hogan said on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . "But we could blow it by nominating unelectable people\u8212\'20\'14and that's exactly what's happening across the country and why the wave is going to be more of a ripple rather than a tidal wave."\par \par \uc2 Republicans at the National Republican Congressional Committee, the House GOP's campaign arm, say Mr. Biden's stewardship of the economy, which shrank for a second quarter in a row amid inflation that has touched 40-year highs, will catapult the party to victory. Also, the party that controls the White House typically loses seats in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 One late development that could complicate the election outlook is a plan unveiled by President Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-to-announce-student-loan-forgiveness-plan-11661331600"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 this past week to cancel student debt }}}\uc2 , a move demanded by many progressives but criticized by some Democrats in competitive races. Republicans called it a handout to college graduates at the expense of other voters. The resolution of probes surrounding Mr. Trump\u8212\'20\'14including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/donald-trumps-documents-seized-at-mar-a-lago-undergoing-security-risk-assessment-11661625645"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Justice Department investigation }}}\uc2  and the findings of a House panel looking into the Jan. 6, 2021, Capitol riot\u8212\'20\'14could also affect the midterm result.\par \par \uc2 With their outlook brightening, some Democrats are switching gears, putting more money into ads against Republican incumbents in tight races such as Reps. David Valadao of California, \uc2 Yvette Herrell\uc2  of New Mexico and \uc2 Steve Chabot\uc2  of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 House Majority PAC, a major outside group supporting House Democrats, this past week made more than $500,000 in new fall advertising reservations in those districts, officials said. "Given the data we're seeing out of the last few months, we are playing offense," said Ali Lapp, the group's president.\par \par \uc2 One Republican strategist working on House races said that Democratic enthusiasm to vote has increased following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the overturning of Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  by the Supreme Court, but Republicans still felt confident wading into districts that Mr. Biden carried due to a perceived advantage on the economy.\par \par \uc2 "We've seen that voters are still more concerned about inflation and the rising cost of living. That's not to say that voters are not concerned about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because it certainly registers," this person said. The person added that Republicans candidates will need to calibrate their stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the general election to keep in step with their districts.\par \par \uc2 Public polling has shown Republicans' advantages moderating. For much of this year, the GOP held a slim advantage over Democrats when pollsters asked voters which party they'd prefer to see in control of Congress, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fivethirtyeight.com/politics/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 FiveThirtyEight }}}\uc2 , which aggregates top public-opinion surveys. Democrats have regained a slim, half-percentage point advantage this month, suggesting public preference is neck-and-neck.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's approval rating has ticked up in some recent polls after hovering around 40%. A recent Gallup survey put Mr. Biden's approval at 44%, his best number in that poll in a year.\par \par \uc2 A win by Democrat Pat Ryan to fill an open \uc2 U.S. House\uc2  seat in New York's Hudson Valley {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrats-outlook-for-midterm-elections-brightens-after-new-york-win-11661368975"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gave his party a jolt of good news }}}\uc2  last week, pointing to the potential importance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this fall. Mr. Ryan centered his candidacy on a fight for nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 The outcome in the Hudson Valley district came after both parties were surprised earlier this month when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-abortion-vote-results-11659440554"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 60% of voters in Kansas rejected a referendum }}}\uc2  that would have explicitly stated there is no right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state's constitution. Democrats have noted that since the Supreme Court decision, several states have seen more women registering to vote than men.\par \par \uc2 Republicans argue special elections aren't always reflective of the broader, general election environment. November could see "a little more turnout on the Republican side," said Sarah Chamberlain, the head of Republican Main Street Partnership, a group that backs some GOP centrists in tight races this fall. She pointed to general economic malaise as a headwind for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Democrats also say they are going into the fall with new laws that candidates can pitch to voters. Mr. Biden recently signed a $280 billion law, which garnered some GOP support, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/senate-approves-280-billion-bill-to-boost-u-s-science-chip-production-11658942295"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aimed at boosting U.S. semiconductor manufacturing }}}\uc2  and competitiveness with China. He also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/joe-biden-to-sign-bill-aimed-at-lowering-drug-costs-boosting-renewable-energy-11660642202"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed a Democratic-backed healthcare, climate and tax package }}}\uc2  called the Inflation Reduction Act that Republicans opposed, saying it could derail the economy.\par \par \uc2 In town halls this month, Democratic Rep. \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  of Michigan has embraced the chips bill as she tries to win re-election in a district that stretches from Lansing to suburban Detroit, home to auto makers that rely on semiconductors. She also touted the Democrats' climate, healthcare and tax law's most immediate effects, including caps on insulin costs for Medicare beneficiaries. But she said she still hears concerns from voters about rising costs.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What will control of the House look like after the midterm elections? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 "I think it's hard to answer someone who is literally paying more for groceries than they paid a year ago that this bill is going to have a direct impact on their grocery bill. Right? I don't B-S people about that," she said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Slotkin, whose district voted for Mr. Trump in 2020, said she tells voters there is no silver bullet to lower inflation, but she supports suspending federal gasoline taxes, a proposal that has faced skepticism from House leaders. Like other Democratic lawmakers in competitive districts, she criticized Mr. Biden's package to cancel student debt, calling it "a one-time Band-Aid" that doesn't address "the core issue of college affordability."\par \par \uc2 Her opponent, Republican state lawmaker Tom Barrett, said he would try to use Ms. Slotkin's vote for the Inflation Reduction Act against her, calling it an ineffective way to ease rising prices. Mr. Barrett also said Mr. Biden's student debt cancellation plan would add to inflation and turn off independent voters.\par \par \uc2 "The overwhelming concern that [voters] are facing," Mr. Barrett said, "are the issues of economics and security."\par \par \uc2 Paul Kiernan contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Joshua Jamerson at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:joshua.jamerson@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joshua.jamerson@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/republicans-still-favored-to-win-back-house-control-but-outlook-tightens-11661654470"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans Still Favored to Win Back House Control but Outlook Tightens }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220828ei8s000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
