\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Idaho Must Allow Abortions in Certain Emergencies, Judge Rules; Preliminary decision is a win for Justice Department after the Biden administration lost initial ruling in case from Texas\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 1,014 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2022\par 01:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A federal judge blocked Idaho from enforcing its near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in certain emergency situations, an early victory for the Justice Department in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/justice-department-files-lawsuit-challenging-idaho-abortion-law-11659463607"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a case it filed this month }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. District Judge B. Lynn Winmill on Wednesday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.idd.50547/gov.uscourts.idd.50547.95.0_1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued a preliminary injunction }}}\uc2  that prevents the state from enforcing its ban in emergency circumstances where doctors and hospitals deem an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to avoid placing the health of a pregnant patient in serious jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 The judge, an appointee of Democratic President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , said Idaho's criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statute conflicted with a federal law that requires hospitals to provide certain basic levels of emergency care.\par \par \uc2 Medicine's goal, he said, "is to effectively identify problems and treat them promptly so patients are stabilized before they develop a life-threatening emergency. The Idaho law requires doctors to do the opposite\u8212\'20\'14to wait until \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to prevent the patient's death."\par \par \uc2 The ruling was one of two initial tests for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/hospitals-must-provide-abortions-in-emergency-situations-biden-administration-says-11657569637"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden administration efforts to require \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access for emergencies }}}\uc2  in states that have moved to heavily restrict the procedure. A separate ruling from Texas, issued late Tuesday, went against the administration.\par \par \uc2 In that case, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.txnd.365015/gov.uscourts.txnd.365015.73.0.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a federal judge in Lubbock ruled }}}\uc2  that hospitals and doctors for now aren't required to abide by the administration's guidance requiring emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department's Idaho lawsuit alleged the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions unlawfully conflict with a federal law that requires hospitals accepting Medicare to provide emergency treatments, which can sometimes include \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was the Biden administration's first legal challenge over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access since the Supreme Court in June {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  and eliminated federal constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Idaho is one of 13 states that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/states-prepare-to-quickly-implement-abortion-trigger-laws-11655385028"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed so-called trigger laws }}}\uc2  in recent years that were designed to take effect immediately or quickly after a decision overturning Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Idaho law has exceptions allowing doctors to perform abortions to save the life of a pregnant woman or in cases of rape or incest that have been reported to law enforcement. Penalties for providers who violate Idaho's law range from two to five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the Justice Department argued in court Monday that the state's lifesaving exception is far narrower than a provision of the federal Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act that requires hospitals to provide treatment not only to save a patient's life but also to prevent organ dysfunction or serious impairment of bodily function.\par \par \uc2 Under Idaho's law, department lawyers said, physicians could be prosecuted for providing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there was some uncertainty about whether a pregnant patient would die without an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or if she would be likely to suffer long-term disability but not death.\par \par \uc2 Still, the Biden administration's win is a fairly narrow one and would prevent Idaho's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from being enforced only in situations where the life or health of a pregnant patient is facing a serious threat.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said the Justice Department "will continue to use every tool at its disposal to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  protected by federal law."\par \par \uc2 Megan Blanksma, chair of the Idaho House Republican Caucus, said, "The death of even one preborn child in these circumstances is a tragedy, yet such deaths are the purpose and consequence of the current federal administration's case against Idaho."\par \par \uc2 The Texas and Idaho cases are part of a broader debate over whether state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are preventing physicians from providing medical care in life-threatening situations. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups and some physicians have said the fear of prosecution is preventing medical providers from giving treatment for conditions including miscarriages, ectopic pregnancies and infections that might turn into sepsis. Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions say all the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans currently in effect around the U.S. already include exemptions for life-threatening emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Monte Stewart, an attorney for Idaho's legislature, said the Biden administration was trying to second-guess the state's judgment about where to draw a difficult line between the interests of the mother and her fetus. "This legislation is designed to balance this state's determination of the moral value of the unborn child and the often weighty, weighty and heart-wrenching interests of the mother," he said.\par \par \uc2 In the Texas case, U.S. District Judge James Wesley Hendrix, a Trump appointee, said federal law requires hospitals to provide emergency treatment for both the mother and her unborn fetus but is silent on what to do if those interests might be in conflict, such as when an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  might prevent long-term disability in the mother but end the life of the fetus. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.txnd.365015/gov.uscourts.txnd.365015.73.0.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The judge said }}}\uc2  the recently released guidance from the Biden administration incorrectly omitted reference to the interests of the fetus and focused only on the interests of the mother.\par \par \uc2 The Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act "provides no instructions on what a physician is to do when there is a conflict between the health of the mother and the unborn child. State law fills this void," Judge Hendrix wrote.\par \par \uc2 Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  applauded the ruling, saying, "We're not going to allow left-wing bureaucrats in Washington to transform our hospitals and emergency rooms into walk-in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, and the decision last night proves what we knew all along: the law is on our side."\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  said, "Because of this decision, women in Texas may now be denied this vital care\u8212\'20\'14even for conditions like severe hemorrhaging or life-threatening hypertension. It's wrong, it's backward, and women may die as a result."\par \par \uc2 Sadie Gurman contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:laura.kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laura.kusisto@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/idaho-must-allow-abortions-in-certain-emergencies-judge-rules-11661386919"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Idaho Must Allow Abortions in Certain Emergencies, Judge Rules }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220825ei8p0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
