\par\par \uc2 Midterm Election 2022\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Democrats' Outlook for Midterm Elections Brightens After New York Win; Republicans are favored to take back the House, but the Supreme Court's  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ruling appears to have energized Democrats\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joshua Jamerson \par \uc2 1,084 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par 19:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14A win by Democrat Pat Ryan to fill an open \uc2 U.S. House\uc2  seat in New York's Hudson Valley gave his party a jolt of good news as it tries to hang on to the chamber, highlighting the potential importance of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-ruling-poses-fresh-midterm-challenges-for-democrats-republicans-11656272277"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  in the midterm elections }}}\uc2  and prompting election watchers to downgrade their expectations for a Republican wave.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ryan, who is the executive, or top elected official, in Ulster County, defeated Republican \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 , who holds the same position in neighboring Dutchess County, in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/house-democrats-fight-for-redistricted-seats-in-new-york-primaries-11661247002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a special election }}}\uc2  for the seat. After the Supreme Court this summer overturned the landmark Roe v. Wade decision that established a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/candidates-for-congress-in-new-yorks-hudson-valley-are-running-in-two-districts-at-once-11661086801"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mr. Ryan centered his candidacy }}}\uc2  on a fight for nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Mr. Molinaro backed the court's opinion, which held that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was an issue for the states, and personally opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Republicans had grown increasingly confident of taking control of the House, pointing to voters' worries about the state of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/climate-bill-draws-clear-battle-lines-for-midterms-11660222802"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the economy and inflation }}}\uc2 , as well as tendency of the party that controls the White House to lose seats in the midterms. But Mr. Ryan's win, following a victory for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -access supporters in Kansas, sparked new optimism among Democrats who had been bracing for a midterm wipeout.\par \par \uc2 The New York result "sends a clear message that voters are fighting back against Republicans' extreme attacks on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights," said Rep. \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  (D., N.Y.), the head of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee that works to elect House Democrats. "Republicans can say goodbye to their 'Red Wave' because voters are clearly coming out in force to elect a pro-choice majority to Congress this November."\par \par \uc2 Michael McAdams, communications director of National Republican Congressional Committee, the Republican counterpart of the DCCC, said, "Majorities are won in November not August. We look forward to prosecuting the case against Democrats' failed one-party rule that's left American families worse off."\par \par \uc2 share your thoughts\par \par \uc2 What impact will the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue have on the midterm elections? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the nonpartisan Cook Political Report downgraded its outlook for the size of a GOP majority after the midterm elections and said it couldn't rule out the possibility that Democrats maintain their majority, citing elevated Democratic turnout.\par \par \uc2 President Biden had a slight edge in the Hudson Valley district over former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020. With Mr. Biden's approval rating stuck around 40%, the seat seemed poised to flip to the Republicans, as polls had shown Mr. Molinaro with a lead. But Mr. Ryan kept the seat in Democratic hands, the Associated Press projected, holding a lead of roughly 2 percentage points with most of the votes counted.\par \par \uc2 The surprise result came after both parties were caught off guard earlier this month when 60% of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-abortion-vote-results-11659440554"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voters in a Kansas referendum }}}\uc2  rejected a proposal that would have explicitly stated there is no right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state's constitution. Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R., S.C.) called the Kansas outcome "a wake-up call" for the Republican Party, which favored overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 According to an aggregate of recent polls compiled by FiveThirtyEight, Democrats and Republicans are neck and neck when voters are asked which party they would rather see in control of Congress. An NBC News poll this month showed 45% of respondents preferred Democratic control of Congress, compared with 47% who wanted Republicans in charge.\par \par \uc2 Republican strategists and nonpartisan analysts said they felt the national mood favored a GOP takeover in the House and that the economy and rising prices rivaled \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as a midterm issue.\par \par \uc2 "I don't view this race in New York as a bellwether with how the midterms are going to turn out," said Julianne Thompson, a Republican strategist in Georgia working on races there. "People care about the kitchen-table issues. They care about whether or not they can fill their car up with gas. I just don't think that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue is anything that is going to determine the midterms."\par \par \uc2 Sarah Chamberlain, the head of Republican Main Street Partnership, a group that backs some GOP centrists in tight races this fall, was watching Republican turnout in other New York races Tuesday. She said GOP voters stayed home. "It was down all over," she said, cautioning that special election turnout doesn't always mirror general election voting. "I don't think this really means anything for November yet."\par \par \uc2 Some Democratic strategists said even though it remains to be seen how elevated Democratic turnout will be, signs like the New York primary showed there is room for surprise this November. Democrats have noted that since the Supreme Court decision, several states have seen more women registering to vote than men. For example, state election records show that women accounted for 70% of Kansans who registered to vote after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Supreme Court's \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ruling }}}\uc2 , according to \uc2 Tom Bonier\uc2 , a Democratic voter-data analyst.\par \par \uc2 "The Roe decision is just punishing them," said Adrian Hemond, a Democratic strategist based in Michigan. "It's bad for the Republicans, which is not what I was expecting a few months ago. Prior to the leaked decision, I was expecting a blowout for Republicans this year."\par \par \uc2 He said internal polling in competitive Michigan battlegrounds showed there were more voters who disagreed with the Supreme Court's Dobbs decision than people who agreed with it, and that those voters who disagreed with it overwhelmingly said they would vote Democratic.\par \par \uc2 Dave Wasserman, the \uc2 U.S. House\uc2  editor for the Cook Political Report, said after Tuesday night's results that Democrats were more engaged after Dobbs but also that Republican voters appeared less energetic, a dual dynamic that helped explain Tuesday night's outcome. Still, given Democrats slim majority in the House, most analysts still see a tough path for Democrats to maintain their majority this fall.\par \par \uc2 "Republicans don't need a wave to win back the House and the Senate," Nathan Gonzales, editor of the nonpartisan Inside Elections publication, tweeted early Wednesday. "Republicans still have enough takeover targets, and minimal net gain necessary, in the House to get across the finish line."\par \par \uc2 Write to Joshua Jamerson at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:joshua.jamerson+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joshua.jamerson+1@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrats-outlook-for-midterm-elections-brightens-after-new-york-win-11661368975"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats' Outlook for Midterm Elections Brightens After New York Win }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220824ei8o005k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
