\par\par \uc2 Midterm Election 2022\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Nevada Senate Race Emerges as Test of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Issue After Roe v. Wade Overturned; Republicans say November will turn on the economy, but some admit  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights could be a factor\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joshua Jamerson \par \uc2 1,242 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2022\par 14:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LAS VEGAS\u8212\'20\'14Sen. \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , one of the most vulnerable Democrats this year, is urging voters to re-elect her to prevent a Republican takeover of the Senate that she says could lead to a national law banning abortions.\par \par \uc2 "There's no doubt in my mind that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  would put it on the table," Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  said in an interview. She pointed to comments the Republican Senate minority leader made earlier this year that it was "possible" that Congress and states could vote on curbing the procedure in the wake of the Supreme Court decision in June {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  and ending the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A person familiar with Mr. McConnell's thinking said the Kentucky senator doesn't think there will be the 60 votes needed in the chamber to advance any bill related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 's pitch is tricky given that her Republican opponent \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , a former attorney general, says he would also oppose a federal ban, especially since Nevada voters in 1990 passed a ballot initiative that endorsed a state statute protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights through 24 weeks of pregnancy. That law still stands.\par \par \uc2 Passing a national law would "contradict what I have argued for my entire adult life and what the Supreme Court just ordered\u8212\'20\'14to return the issue to the people," Mr. Laxalt wrote in an op-ed to the Reno Gazette Journal this month,` responding to criticism from Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Democrats are using the Nevada race to test the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue after strategists in both parties were surprised in August when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-abortion-vote-results-11659440554"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 60% of voters in Kansas rejected a referendum }}}\uc2  that would have explicitly stated there is no right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state's constitution. Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R., S.C.) called the Kansas outcome "a wake-up call" for the Republican Party, which favored overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  is one of the Republicans' top targets in the November midterm elections as they seek to wrest back control of the 50-50 Senate, where Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  currently breaks ties to the benefit of the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Pat Hickey, a former Republican leader in the Nevada Assembly representing parts of Washoe County, said the economy would remain top of mind for most voters, but anxiety after the Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe could play a role. "I think many women and many Republican women will not be anxious to see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in Nevada change," Mr. Hickey said.\par \par \uc2 Margarita Romano, a Democrat who runs a social-services clinic in Las Vegas and is backing Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , said the court decision led her to get involved in organizing for a political candidate for the first time. "We're talking about the future of choice," she said.\par \par \uc2 During interviews at a local GOP gathering in Las Vegas last week, Republican voters had mixed feelings about Mr. Laxalt's personal stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Mr. Laxalt has said he is personally opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a view he said in the op-ed was informed by "being born to a young single mother who chose not to abort me."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Laxalt, who as attorney general supported restrictions in other states, such as Alabama, also wrote in his op-ed that he would support a new referendum, which currently isn't on the ballot, to change Nevada's law to permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until 13 weeks of pregnancy instead of 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Jacque Parker, a Republican business banker from Las Vegas, said she was leaning toward crossing party lines to vote for Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  based in part on Mr. Laxalt's comments about his personal stance and his support for restrictions to Nevada's state law.\par \par \uc2 "It's my right. Don't tell me what to do with my body," Ms. Parker said.\par \par \uc2 Cathy Freeze, a Las Vegas Republican voter and retired social-services worker, said she doesn't want congressional action on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and she doubts Republicans would try to pass anything in Congress. "They might do it, but I don't think they will," said Ms. Freeze, 71 years old, who plans to vote for Mr. Laxalt. "I think they're overreacting to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  thing," she said of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Strategists in both parties said the recent vote in Kansas demonstrated that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-vote-signals-abortion-could-emerge-as-top-political-issue-in-november-11659555741"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  issue can energize a swath }}}\uc2  of the electorate. Independents there turned out in large numbers, and about a fifth of those voting on the referendum\u8212\'20\'14more than 180,000 people\u8212\'20\'14weighed in only on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot question and didn't vote in the day's Republican or Democratic primaries for governor, the Journal previously reported.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What impact will the overturning of Roe v. Wade have on the election in Nevada in particular and the midterms in general? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Chris Hartline, a spokesman for the National Republican Senatorial Committee, said it made sense for Democrats to try to use \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to increase turnout among their core supporters but that Republican voters would also be motivated by inflation and the direction of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Alena Sheehan, a state employee who lives in Las Vegas and typically votes for Republicans, said she was most fired up to vote for Mr. Laxalt to show her disapproval of the climate and healthcare law Democrats, including Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , voted for this summer. "I work hard for my money\u8230\'20\'26and then you're going to tax me? It's not right," said Ms. Sheehan, 58 years old, who said that broadly speaking, she heard Democrats were raising taxes to offset the cost of the law. The law imposes taxes on large, profitable corporations, but GOP leaders have argued that'll get passed down to everyday Americans.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights issue is also driving some Republican establishment figures in the state to rally around Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , including Pete Ernaut, a longtime Nevada Republican who previously represented deeply conservative areas of Washoe County near Reno in the state legislature. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
