\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 State  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans Face Obstacles as Republicans Clash; GOP lawmakers in Indiana, South Carolina and other states encounter party division on how far to go\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Calfas and Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 1,124 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2022\par 12:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Many Republican-led state legislatures planned to move forward quickly with new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  ended federal protections for the procedure. They are finding it more complicated than they anticipated.\par \par \uc2 In the weeks since the high court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overruled Roe v. Wade  }}}\uc2 , Republicans in a handful of states, including Indiana, West Virginia and Nebraska, have encountered disagreements within their own party about how far to push on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, as well as other stumbling blocks on the path to imposing new laws. Similar tensions are emerging among GOP lawmakers in South Carolina as they begin to debate just how far {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-opponents-consider-how-far-to-press-after-end-of-roe-v-wade-11656273349"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 their proposed \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans  }}}\uc2  should go.\par \par \uc2 "Talking about being pro-life is very different than actually making laws and creating a public policy that will affect real peoples' lives," said Elizabeth Bennion, a political-science professor at \uc2 Indiana University South Bend\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 About a dozen states have moved to enact \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions based on laws that predated the Supreme Court's Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision and were able to go into effect once the court overturned Roe. In the aftermath of the court's decision, Republican lawmakers looking to enact new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-vote-signals-abortion-could-emerge-as-top-political-issue-in-november-11659555741"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are facing challenges  }}}\uc2  as they begin to see the real-life consequences of near-total bans\u8212\'20\'14even as the political will for new restrictions remains strong in many of these conservative states.\par \par \uc2 Before Dobbs, most bans on abortions in the earliest weeks of pregnancy were likely to be blocked in federal court because they violated the protections in Roe. Now those laws\u8212\'20\'14and new ones\u8212\'20\'14are more likely to take effect.\par \par \uc2 That shift has caused some legislators to pay closer attention to the details of bills that previously were unlikely to become enacted law.\par \par \uc2 "The zone of wiggle room is gone," said South Carolina Rep. Micah Caskey, a Republican who pushed for some exceptions and clarifications to the state's proposed near-total ban. "Instead of just saying we had a performative, politicking moment of passing legislation, this time around it's absolutely about making sure that we've drafted a bill that does what we want it to do."\par \par \uc2 South Carolina lawmakers are weighing how to tighten \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions beyond the state's 2021 ban on the procedure after six weeks of pregnancy. An ad hoc committee in the House convened last month to start working out the details of a bill that would ban most abortions. The committee recommended the proposed law include exceptions for life-saving or life-altering medical emergencies for pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 GOP legislators said the expectation that the new law will take effect made the debate more difficult. The \uc2 House Judiciary Committee\uc2  advanced the bill in a 13-7 vote on Tuesday, with three Republicans declining to vote. The House is scheduled to convene Aug. 30.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are divided on whether to include exceptions in the bill for rape, incest and fetal abnormalities, which had been included in the six-week ban that was passed in 2021, but had been blocked by courts before the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 The push for new legislation took on new urgency Wednesday, when the South Carolina Supreme Court temporarily blocked enforcement of the six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 At the judiciary committee meeting Tuesday, Rep. John McCravy defended the decision not to include exceptions for rape and incest, saying "to kill an innocent preborn child will never erase a criminal act."\par \par \uc2 Rep. Neal Collins, a fellow Republican, said he was concerned about the impact of a near-total ban after hearing a story about one woman's medical complications while the state's six-week ban was in effect. He voted in favor of the law last year.\par \par \uc2 "These are affecting people," Mr. Collins said. "What we do matters." He declined to vote to advance the new bill.\par \par \uc2 Republican leaders in other states faced similar hurdles. After West Virginia's governor called lawmakers there into a special session last month, legislative leaders paused consideration of their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban for weeks after disagreements over exceptions and penalties.\par \par \uc2 Nebraska Gov. \uc2 Pete Ricketts\uc2  said he wouldn't call a special session to pass a ban on abortions after 12 weeks of pregnancy after it became clear fellow Republicans didn't have enough support for it in the Legislature. Kansas voters, meanwhile, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-abortion-vote-results-11659440554"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dealt a decisive defeat  }}}\uc2  earlier this month to an amendment to the state's constitution that would have cleared the way for the Republican-led legislature to pass new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Indiana's legislature {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/indiana-legislature-is-first-to-approve-abortion-bans-since-supreme-court-overturned-roe-v-wade-11659755538"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed its \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban  }}}\uc2  earlier this month, after hours of debate that was at times heated, including shouting matches between senators on the floor. The bill bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout pregnancy with exceptions in some cases of rape and incest until around 10 weeks of pregnancy, in addition to exceptions for fatal fetal anomalies and emergencies that threaten the life or physical health of the pregnant woman.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Eric Holcomb\uc2  , a Republican, signed the bill into law shortly afterward. It is slated to take effect Sept. 15.\par \par \uc2 The bill initially faced almost universal opposition from antiabortion groups as well as many Republican legislators. They said some of the exceptions inserted by the state legislature were so broad that they would actually weaken the state's existing laws.\par \par \uc2 Sen. Susan Glick, a Republican sponsor of the bill in the Senate, defended the legislation by saying not every member of the legislature agrees on every part of any bill.\par \par \uc2 Rep. Ann Vermilion, who describes herself as a lifelong Republican, said the Supreme Court decision "has peeled an onion on the details of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , showing layers and layers of such a difficult topic."\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What is the future for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in conservative states? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 The state's representatives weighed more than 20 proposed amendments out of more than 80 that were submitted, rejecting proposals that would have eliminated exceptions for rape and incest, as well as for cases involving fatal fetal abnormalities. They also rejected a Republican representative's proposal to ban most abortions after 13 weeks of pregnancy instead of a near-total ban\u8212\'20\'14an effort lawmakers who supported the proposal framed as a bipartisan compromise.\par \par \uc2 "I wasn't prepared to debate this topic in specificity, much less in two weeks' time," said Ms. Vermilion, who voted against the bill. "We are moving too fast for fruitful discussion."\par \par \uc2 Write to Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jennifer.calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jennifer.calfas@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Laura Kusisto at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/state-abortion-bans-face-obstacles-as-republicans-clash-11660908536"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 State \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Bans Face Obstacles as Republicans Clash  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220819ei8j001s0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
