\par\par \uc2 Politics & Ideas\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Hopeful Signs for Democrats in the 2022 Midterms; Inflation,  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  and bad Republican choices may limit the expected losses.\b0\par\par\uc2 By William A. Galston \par \uc2 822 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2022\par 17:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The race to the midterm elections will accelerate after Labor Day\u8212\'20\'14and if past is prologue, Democrats are likely to lose control of at least one legislative chamber. But current facts are more ambiguous than the historical record.\par \par \uc2 Midterm elections are usually referendums on the incumbent president and his party. Although President Biden's job approval remains low (as it has been for the past year), it appears to have improved by 2 to 3 percentage points in recent weeks. If this trend continues, the president will be less of a drag on his party's candidates than he was at his nadir.\par \par \uc2 Surprisingly, Democrats remain tied with Republicans in the generic congressional ballot, which reflects national preferences for the parties' House candidates. If this is still true on Election Day, Republican gains will be much smaller than they were in 1994 and 2010. Other factors\u8212\'20\'14including the record low number of truly competitive House districts\u8212\'20\'14point in the same direction.\par \par \uc2 In Senate races, candidate quality matters more. As has happened repeatedly in recent cycles, Republicans appear to have damaged their prospects during primary contests by choosing nominees who have more appeal with their party's base than with statewide electorates. In Ohio, Pennsylvania, Georgia and Arizona, nominees backed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  trail their Democratic opponents, several by wide margins.\par \par \uc2 In the race to succeed \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 , the two-term Republican senator from Pennsylvania, Democrat Lt. Gov. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  leads TV personality Mehmet Oz by double digits. In Ohio\u8212\'20\'14which Mr. Trump carried by wide margins in 2016 and 2020\u8212\'20\'14Democratic Rep. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  has moved out to a 4.5-point lead over political neophyte J.D. Vance. If Democrats can pick up this seat, which Republicans have held since 1999, the GOP's chances of retaking the Senate will be dealt a possibly fatal blow, even if \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  manage to eke out victories over Democratic incumbents in Georgia and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Inflation will do more than any other issue to shape this year's midterms, and broad-based price increases have tilted polls toward the Republicans since last fall. But even on this issue recent trends have been favorable for Democrats. According to the AAA's daily survey, gasoline prices have fallen to $3.95 a gallon from a peak of $5.02 two months ago. Lower shipping prices and a strengthening dollar should hold down the prices of imported goods, and bloated inventories will force retailers to give consumers some relief. Although July's more positive inflation report\u8212\'20\'14which showed a modest reduction in year-over-year inflation, to 8.5% from 9.1%\u8212\'20\'14doesn't necessarily signal a trend, a sustained decline between now and November could persuade some voters that the worst is behind them.\par \par \uc2 In midterm elections, turnout is variable\u8212\'20\'14and crucial. When Democratic interest in the 2014 cycle was muted, Republicans added 13 House seats to their already substantial majority. In 2018, by contrast, Democrats surged to the polls to express their opposition to President Trump and gained 41 seats, retaking the majority after eight years in opposition.\par \par \uc2 Democrats' enthusiasm about going to the polls this fall had substantially trailed Republicans'\u8212\'20\'14but recent events have narrowed the gap. After the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, Democratic interest in the midterms {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://morningconsult.com/2022/06/27/roe-v-wade-democratic-enthusiasm-midterm-elections/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surged}}}\uc2 . The passage of the major energy-and-climate bill that many Democrats had given up for dead further lifted their spirits.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue could prove a game-changer. As the results of Kansas' Aug. 2 referendum indicate, the court's decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization isn't popular, and severe restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are even less so. Eighty-five percent of Americans favor allowing abortions in cases of rape, incest and risks to the mother's life, and a strong majority believe that the procedure should be widely available during the first trimester of pregnancy. While only 3 in 10 Americans favor \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand, less than 1 in 10 support an outright ban. Most voters accept \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some circumstances but not others, and candidates who appear dogmatic or extreme will pay a price at the polls. By a margin of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://today.yougov.com/topics/politics/articles-reports/2022/08/12/americans-support-state-abortion-protections-poll"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 25 points}}}\uc2 , voters favor protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state constitutions\u8212\'20\'14a position backed by most demographic groups and even by one-third of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Women care about this issue, which now trails only inflation in their list of top concerns. The pain of loss typically outweighs the satisfaction of gain, and tens of millions of pro-choice women have suffered a loss that until recently seemed unimaginable. If Democrats do better than expected this November, the justices who voted to overturn Roe will be a big piece of the explanation.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/hopeful-midterm-signs-for-democrats-biden-generic-ballot-abortion-inflation-kansas-canidate-quality-dobbs-roe-11660658687"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hopeful Signs for Democrats in the 2022 Midterms}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220816ei8g004jx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
