\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \uc2 Tech\par \b \uc2 Phones Know Who Went to an  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Clinic. Whom Will They Tell? Myriad state restrictions on accessing or performing abortions prompt some companies to pledge not to share sensitive mobile-phone data\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patience Haggin \par \uc2 1,430 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2022\par 12:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In May, shortly after the draft of the Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opinion overturning Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  became public, location-data specialist Tapestri Inc. received unusual requests from two companies.\par \par \uc2 Each wanted to purchase mobile-device data that would reveal users who had visited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics along the Illinois-Missouri border, said Tapestri Chief Executive Walter Harrison.\par \par \uc2 Tapestri declined both requests, saying it doesn't keep such data. "In our view that's the best way to do it: just not collecting it at all," Mr. Harrison said.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's June decision in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization }}}\uc2 , which has been followed by a wave of state laws {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banning or restricting \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , is drawing attention to the $10 billion-plus market for people's mobile-phone location data. The industry is made up of an array of companies, from big tech firms to data aggregators and brokers who buy and sell the information. Every day, connected devices such as smartphones and fitness trackers gather a trail of location data that eventually finds its way into that under-the-radar marketplace. The data often is used for commercial purposes such as customizing ads or apps.\par \par \uc2 Since the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-does-overturning-roe-v-wade-mean-explained-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court's Dobbs ruling }}}\uc2 , companies across the location-data industry are examining and in some cases revising how they handle data regarding {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-abortion-access-has-changed-around-the-world-11659764119"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 visits to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  clinics }}}\uc2 . Some are agreeing voluntarily not to sell the data or say they will store it in ways that mask the location. Some such as Tapestri, which pays consumers for sharing their anonymized location history, delete any health-related location information that they deem to be sensitive.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of the court's ruling and privacy advocates say personal \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  data could be publicized or used to build a legal case against people seeking or providing abortions. "These kinds of requests for user data are going to sharply increase," said Jennifer Lynch, surveillance litigation director at the \uc2 Electronic Frontier Foundation\uc2 , a digital-rights advocacy group.\par \par \uc2 A CEO of one location-data firm said the privacy advocates' fears are overblown, in part because the data is often imprecise and could be challenged in court. Some industry executives and researchers say restricting the collection and use of the data might prohibit other uses, such as studying how people access healthcare.\par \par \uc2 About a dozen states {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/indiana-legislature-is-first-to-approve-abortion-bans-since-supreme-court-overturned-roe-v-wade-11659755538"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have enacted laws that ban }}}\uc2  many or most abortions, and several others are expected to pass similar measures. A few states, including Texas, have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/behind-texas-abortion-law-an-attorneys-unusual-enforcement-idea-11630762683"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 provided financial incentives }}}\uc2  for people to bring civil lawsuits against those suspected of aiding abortions.\par \par \uc2 Thus far there have been few, if any, criminal or civil investigations since states' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans were enacted, in part because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in those states {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/doctors-struggle-with-state-abortion-restrictions-at-odds-with-federal-law-11657445400"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have generally ceased performing the procedure }}}\uc2  if there is any ambiguity about its legality.\par \par \uc2 Concerns over collection and storage of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  data is the latest challenge for the location-data industry, which over the past few years {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/data-privacy-bill-advances-in-congress-but-states-throw-up-objections-11658347139"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has faced scrutiny from lawmakers and regulators }}}\uc2 . Data-privacy laws in California and other states in recent years have placed new restrictions on the companies, such as requiring companies to give consumers the right to opt out of having their data sold.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  last month said it would strictly enforce laws governing the collection, use and sharing of sensitive consumer data. "The misuse of mobile location and health information\u8212\'20\'14 including \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  data\u8212\'20\'14exposes consumers to significant harm," wrote Kristin Cohen, acting associate director for the commission's division of privacy and identity protection.\par \par \uc2 Without clear regulations for the location-data industry's data on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, individual companies are determining how to respond to the implications of the Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 Alphabet Inc\uc2 .'s \uc2 Google\uc2  recently said it would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/google-to-delete-visits-to-abortion-clinics-from-users-location-history-11656710884"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 automatically delete visits to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  clinics }}}\uc2  from its users' location history.\par \par \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 . says it minimizes collection of personal data and that most location data is stored in ways the company can't access. It has no way to access Health and Maps app data for people using updated operating systems, and can't provide such data in response to government requests, the company says.\par \par \uc2 The vast location-data ecosystem includes many other lesser-known companies that are taking a different approach. A trade group for some of those firms, Network Advertising Initiative, announced a new set of voluntary standards for member companies in June, two days before the Dobbs ruling came out.\par \par \uc2 Participating companies, including \uc2 Foursquare Labs Inc\uc2 ., Cuebiq Inc. and Precisely Inc.'s \uc2 PlaceIQ\uc2 , agreed not to use, sell or share precise location data about visits to sensitive locations\u8212\'20\'14including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics\u8212\'20\'14except to comply with a legal obligation.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What concerns do you have about the privacy of your mobile-phone location data? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Some privacy experts said the companies' pledges won't be enough to protect people who seek or provide abortions. "They really shouldn't be collecting unnecessary data in the first place," said Sara Geoghegan, law fellow at \uc2 Electronic Privacy Information Center\uc2 . "And some sort of scout's honor promise is no substitute for enforced privacy rights or legal limits on data processing."\par \par \uc2 Foursquare\uc2 , Cuebiq and \uc2 PlaceIQ\uc2  receive raw latitude and longitude coordinates from the many smartphone apps that embed location-tracking technology, then match that information with a directory of locations to try to determine whether a person visited a particular restaurant, movie theater or other business.\par \par \uc2 Under the new standards, when the coordinates indicate an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic or other sensitive location, like a place of religious worship or military base, the companies won't make that connection in their databases. They might still store the raw coordinates of places where the users visited. Representatives for several other firms said they would follow similar practices.\par \par \uc2 Some users of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -clinic location data say restricting the collection and use of the data would disrupt research projects. Location-data company Gravy Analytics Inc., an NAI member that chose not to follow the new standards, wrote in a blog post that "a wholesale blackout" of mobility data for sensitive locations would cut off valuable data used in analytical and research work.\par \par \uc2 Martin Andersen, an associate professor of economics at the University of North Carolina at Greensboro, relied on mobile-location data provided by SafeGraph Inc. to study how Texas residents accessed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services after the state's new law went into effect. Mr. Andersen downloaded aggregated, anonymized data free from SafeGraph.\par \par \uc2 In May 2022, SafeGraph removed data on family-planning centers from its self-serve data offerings. It did so "in light of potential federal changes in family planning access," SafeGraph's chief executive, Auren Hoffman, wrote in a blog post.\par \par \uc2 "We acknowledge that our decision to take down Patterns for family planning centers could negatively impact this valuable research, but we think this is the right decision given the current climate," Mr. Hoffman wrote.\par \par \uc2 Cuebiq plans to legally challenge any court order or law-enforcement request related to reproductive healthcare, a Cuebiq spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Stacey Gray, director of legislative research and analysis at Future of Privacy Forum, said companies that retain the raw geographic coordinates of users' visits would still be subject to law-enforcement requests.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, she said, it might be difficult for companies to ascertain whether a law-enforcement request concerns \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Authorities might simply say they are seeking information on an individual suspected of wrongdoing\u8212\'20\'14without specifying that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the alleged infraction.\par \par \uc2 Location data has been used to target \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers before. In 2017, the Massachusetts attorney general alleged that Copley Advertising LLC had violated consumer-protection laws when it aimed antiabortion ads at women who were sitting in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic waiting rooms, then served ads to the women's mobile devices for up to 30 days afterward. Copley settled the allegations and agreed not to practice such targeting in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 Location data is just one of many sources of data that could be used to build a case against those who seek or provide abortions. Other such data include users' text messages and search histories. Still other data is commercially available through the digital-ad market, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/period-tracker-apps-aim-for-anonymity-following-roe-v-wade-decision-11656202089"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 such as menstruation history }}}\uc2  and anonymized data on a person's medical procedures.\par \par \uc2 Tim Higgins contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Patience Haggin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:patience.haggin@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 patience.haggin@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/phones-know-who-went-to-an-abortion-clinic-whom-will-they-tell-11659873781"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Phones Know Who Went to an \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Clinic. Whom Will They Tell? }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220807ei8700105\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
