\par \par \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2 , based in Indianapolis, says it is concerned the law will impact its ability to attract 'diverse scientific, engineering and business talent from around the world. PHOTO: Maddie McGarvey for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Indiana-based companies \uc2 Eli Lilly & Co\uc2 . and \uc2 Cummins Inc\uc2 . spoke against {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/indiana-legislature-is-first-to-approve-abortion-bans-since-supreme-court-overturned-roe-v-wade-11659755538"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the state's new \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  law }}}\uc2 , saying the restrictions could hurt the companies' ability to keep and attract employees and could affect where the businesses grow.\par \par \uc2 The Saturday statements came after Indiana's governor on Friday signed a near-total ban on abortions shortly after lawmakers approved it. The ban takes effect Sept. 15 and includes exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Lilly, based in Indianapolis, said the company is concerned the law will impact its ability to attract "diverse scientific, engineering and business talent from around the world." The company, with a market capitalization of $292 billion, is one of the biggest employers in Indiana, with more than 10,000 of its approximately 37,000 global workers in the state.\par \par \uc2 Calling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a "divisive and deeply personal issue," the company said it has expanded its health-plan coverage to include travel for reproductive services unavailable locally. Many businesses {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/supreme-court-decision-roe-v-wade-6-24-2022/card/ceos-explain-policies-to-employees-in-wake-of-decision-7u0QXq7jkSw6X6bn8YvB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have made similar adjustments }}}\uc2  since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  in June {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  wasn't a constitutional right }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Lilly said Saturday such efforts "may not be enough for some current and potential employees," according to the statement. "Given this new law, we will be forced to plan for more employment growth outside our home state."\par \par \uc2 The statement also suggested the law could hurt recruitment of talent to Indiana broadly.\par \par \uc2 Cummins\uc2 , also a major employer in Indiana, said it had shared its concerns about the bill with state legislators before and during the legislative process. It said women should have the right to make reproductive healthcare decisions, and that such an ability was important to equal opportunity and diversity in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 Provisions in the law "impact our people, impede our ability to attract and retain top talent and influence our decisions as we continue to grow," a \uc2 Cummins\uc2  spokesman said on Saturday. The company, valued at about $31 billion, has around 60,000 employees with around 10,000 in Indiana, according to the spokesman.\par \par \uc2 Cummins\uc2  also pays for travel for elective procedures, including \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Indiana is the first state to approve {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  restrictions }}}\uc2  following the Supreme Court's Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision. Other states moved to implement restrictions they had in place in the event that Roe v. Wade, the landmark 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was overturned.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade has created new questions for employers on how, if at all, to respond to the decision in terms of corporate policies, whether to speak up and what to say. For the last several years, leaders of some of the country's largest companies have become more vocal on a range of political and social issues, partly due to pressure they have felt from employees.\par \par \uc2 A number of companies across industries have said they would pay for employees' travel for abortions or other procedures should they need to go out of state, and some expanded what their health insurance covers as it relates to abortions or \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  services more broadly.\par \par \uc2 Others haven't made accommodations and have indicated they have no plans to do so.\par \par \uc2 Indianapolis's Chamber of Commerce earlier this week said it opposed the state bill, asking lawmakers not to pass it. The chamber flagged concerns about attracting and retaining employees in the state amid workforce shortages, legal risk for doctors, and infant and maternal health issues.\par \par \uc2 "Over the last two weeks, the Indiana General Assembly has debated a substantial policy change on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a compressed time frame. Such an expedited legislative process\u8212\'20\'14rushing to advance state policy on broad, complex issues\u8212\'20\'14is, at best, detrimental to Hoosiers, and at worst, reckless," the chamber said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Republican Gov. \uc2 Eric Holcomb\uc2 , in a statement after he signed the measure, said: "I am personally most proud of each Hoosier who came forward to courageously share their views in a debate that is unlikely to cease anytime soon." He added that he will continue to "keep an open ear."\par \par \uc2 Write to Emily Glazer at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:emily.glazer@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emily.glazer@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/eli-lilly-speaks-out-against-indianas-ban-on-most-abortions-11659813615"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Eli Lilly, Cummins Speak Out Against Indiana's Ban on Most Abortions }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220806ei860025t\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
