\par \par \uc2 'I just want to make sure that the right is retained,' said Megan Duckers, who voted in the Kansas primary elections on Tuesday. PHOTO: Fruhlein Chrys Econar/The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 The defeat was an unsettling one for some conservatives, who had argued that voters were focused on issues like the economy and gas prices and not \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "If Republicans think the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't on the minds of voters, tonight's results should put them on notice," said a Republican political strategist in Kansas, who declined to be named.\par \par \uc2 Going into Tuesday's vote, political observers believed the proposed amendment faced favorable odds of passage, though polling last month indicated the race was close. The final tally wasn't, with opponents of the amendment winning by margins that were unexpected.\par \par \uc2 The numbers in Johnson County, the state's biggest, helped tell the story. The county went about 53% for Mr. Biden in the 2020 presidential election. On Tuesday, about 68% of county voters rejected the amendment.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday night, opponents of the amendment hugged, cried and posed for photos in a ballroom of the Overland Park Convention Center. Earlier, at the watch party for supporters of the amendment, a speaker tried to bolster spirits by saying it was going to be a long night. Within about 30 minutes, AP called the race.\par \par \uc2 Other states, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/michigan-court-ruling-allows-potential-prosecutions-for-abortion-11659384864"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including Michigan, }}}\uc2  California and Kentucky, are likely to have measures on the ballot in November that propose either to weaken or enhance protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What will be the future for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Kansas? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Outside a polling place Tuesday at the Journey Bible Church in Olathe, Megan Duckers, a 50-year-old personal assistant, came out with her husband and nephew to oppose the amendment. "I just want to make sure that the right is retained," she said, adding that she was nervous but hopeful about the outcome. "Kansas has surprised me in a good way in recent elections, so I think we might pull this one out."\par \par \uc2 Lisa Liebelt, a 50-year-old who voted for the amendment Tuesday along with her husband and two children, said canvassers supporting the amendment at one point during the campaign trapped her in her driveway to make sure she got their literature. "I've known the gravity of this for a long time," she said.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's turnout outpaced a typical Kansas primary and exceeded expectations from elections officials. A spokeswoman for Kansas Secretary of State Scott Schwab said turnout could be as high as 50%, compared with about 34% in 2020 and about 27% in 2018.\par \par \uc2 "It just takes one question on there that excites everybody, but the whole idea is the more people vote the better. That's the way the system is supposed to work," said Salvatore Migliazzo, supervising judge at the Oak Park Library polling place in Overland Park.\par \par \uc2 On a normal August primary, Mr. Migliazzo said, the site would see 300 or 400 voters, but by 4 p.m., more than 800 people had voted already. "We had a big line to start with in the morning," he said. "We kind of got it down under control, but it's just been steady. A lot bigger number than we thought we'd have."\par \par \uc2 The proposed amendment came in response to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-supreme-court-blocks-abortion-ban-11556298338"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2019 Kansas Supreme Court ruling }}}\uc2  that said the state's constitution protects the right of personal autonomy, including the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates are hoping for Kansas-like rulings in other states now that Roe v. Wade is no more and the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  question {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/state-judges-are-in-spotlight-after-supreme-court-overrules-roe-v-wade-11656444559"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 becomes primarily one for state courts. }}}\par \par \uc2 Lindsay Wise contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:laura.kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laura.kusisto@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Joe Barrett at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:joseph.barrett@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joseph.barrett@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-abortion-vote-results-11659440554"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kansas Votes to Protect \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Rights in State Constitution }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220802ei82001md\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
